window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["gmch-2023"] = {"id": "gmch-2023", "title": "GMCH Chandigarh 2023", "fullTitle": "GMCH Chandigarh 2023, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper, held 2 Sep 2023)", "sub": "100 real questions · printed worked key, checked · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "You hypothesize that paediatric cases of Autism in your clinic are due to maternal vaccination with a Vaccine which was popular few years ago. The design of your study which is ethically acceptable and provides the information in a reasonable time is:", "options": {"a": "Prospective Cohort study", "b": "Case-Control study", "c": "Descriptive study", "d": "Randomized control trial"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a case-control study is the design that is both ethical and quick enough to test this hypothesis. A case-control study starts from the outcome and looks backwards. The researcher selects cases (children who already have autism) and controls (similar children without autism), then compares how often each group was exposed in the past, here to the mother's vaccination. Nobody is followed forward for years and nobody is exposed on purpose, so it is fast, cheap and ethically clean. It is also the best design for a rare outcome such as autism, because it begins with the cases a clinic already has instead of waiting for new ones to appear. The result is expressed as an odds ratio: the odds of exposure among cases divided by the odds of exposure among controls. Example: Dr Meera takes 50 children with autism from her clinic and 100 children without autism from the same area, matched for age and sex, and checks their mothers' vaccination records. If exposure is no more common among the cases, the odds ratio sits near 1 and the hypothesis fails. The best-known vaccine scare, Andrew Wakefield's 1998 paper linking the MMR vaccine to autism, was retracted in 2010, and large later studies found no link. Option b is right because it tests the link using existing cases, without harming anyone and without waiting years. Exam trap: the randomised controlled trial is the strongest design for cause and effect, so students pick it; but deliberately vaccinating mothers to see whether their children develop autism would be unethical. A prospective cohort is ethical but would need thousands of pregnancies followed for years.", "topic": "Case-control study design"}, {"q": "In a Cohort Study design the following is TRUE:", "options": {"a": "It selects people who already have a disease", "b": "It involves a small study population", "c": "It is useful for study of rare disorders", "d": "It provides information on incidence rates"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a cohort study provides information on incidence rates. A cohort is a group of people who share something, such as being born in the same year or working in the same factory. In a cohort study the researcher starts with people who are free of the disease, classifies them by exposure (exposed versus not exposed), and follows them forward in time, counting the new cases that appear in each group. Counting new cases over a period in a population at risk is exactly what incidence means. So a cohort study gives the incidence among the exposed and the unexposed, and from these the relative risk (incidence in exposed divided by incidence in unexposed) and the attributable risk. A classic example is the British Doctors Study of Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill (1951 onward), which followed thousands of British doctors by smoking habit and showed far higher lung cancer death rates among smokers. Example: Arjun, a public health researcher in Pune, enrols 5,000 healthy adults, records who chews tobacco, and checks every year for new cases of oral cancer. Cohort studies need large samples, long follow-up and much money, and people drop out over time, but they show clearly that the exposure came before the disease. Option d is right because only a design that follows a disease-free group forward can count new cases. Exam trap: students mix up the two observational designs. 'Starts with people who have the disease', 'small sample' and 'good for rare diseases' all describe the case-control study. Cohort studies suit rare exposures, not rare diseases.", "topic": "Cohort study and incidence"}, {"q": "The term sensitivity in statistics while using measurement instruments is defined as:", "options": {"a": "Ability to identify a case correctly", "b": "Ability to identify those without disease", "c": "Positive predictive value", "d": "Negative predictive value"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: sensitivity is the ability of a test or instrument to identify a case correctly. Imagine every person tested falls into one of four boxes: true positive (has the disorder, test says yes), false negative (has it, test says no), false positive (does not have it, test says yes) and true negative (does not have it, test says no). Sensitivity = true positives divided by all who truly have the disorder (true positives plus false negatives); it is the true positive rate. Specificity is its partner: true negatives divided by all who are truly free of the disorder. Jacob Yerushalmy (1947) introduced the two terms while evaluating chest X-ray screening for tuberculosis. Example: Riya gives a depression screening questionnaire to 100 patients whom psychiatrists have already diagnosed with depression. If it flags 90 of them, its sensitivity is 90 per cent; the 10 it misses are false negatives. A highly sensitive test rarely misses a case, so a negative result helps rule the disorder out (memory aid SnNout: Sensitive test, Negative result, rules out). That is why screening tests are chosen for high sensitivity and confirmatory tests for high specificity (SpPin). Sensitivity and specificity belong to the test itself and do not change with prevalence; predictive values do. Option a is right because sensitivity asks: of the people who really have the condition, what share does the test catch? Exam trap: examiners reverse the pair, so option b (identifying those without disease) is specificity. They also offer the predictive values, which start from the test result rather than from the person's true disease status.", "topic": "Sensitivity of a test"}, {"q": "The example of a continuous variable is:", "options": {"a": "Height", "b": "Gender", "c": "Blood Group", "d": "Number of children"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: height is a continuous variable. A variable is continuous when it can take any value within a range, including fractions, so that between any two values another is always possible. Height can be 152 cm, 152.5 cm or 152.53 cm; the only limit is the precision of the measuring tape. Continuous variables are measured rather than counted: height, weight, age, blood pressure, temperature and reaction time are typical. A discrete variable can take only separate values, usually whole-number counts with gaps between them, such as the number of children in a family or the number of hospital admissions in a month. Categorical (qualitative) variables place people in named groups with no numerical value, such as gender or blood group. On Stanley Smith Stevens's (1946) levels of measurement these are nominal scales, while height is a ratio scale because it has a true zero. Example: Kabir records four facts about each patient on a ward: height (continuous), number of children (discrete), blood group (nominal) and gender (nominal). Only height can be measured to any decimal place and sensibly summarised with a mean and standard deviation of individual values. Option a is right because height varies smoothly and can be measured to any fineness. Exam trap: 'number of children' is numerical, so it looks continuous, but it is a count. A family can have 2 or 3 children, never 2.4; an average of 2.4 children per family is a summary statistic, not a value the variable itself can take. Numerical does not mean continuous.", "topic": "Continuous versus discrete variables"}, {"q": "The following is not a measure of central tendency in statistics:", "options": {"a": "Mean", "b": "Median", "c": "Mode", "d": "Variance"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: variance is not a measure of central tendency; it is a measure of dispersion. A measure of central tendency gives a single typical value around which the data cluster, and there are three main ones. The mean is the arithmetic average (the sum of scores divided by their number); it uses every score but is pulled by extreme values. The median is the middle score when the data are put in order; it resists outliers, so it suits skewed data such as income. The mode is the most frequent score and the only one usable with nominal data. Variance answers a different question: how spread out are the scores around the mean? It is the average of the squared deviations from the mean, and its square root is the standard deviation. The term was introduced by Ronald Fisher (1918). Other measures of dispersion are the range, the quartile deviation and the mean deviation. Example: Meera's two tuition batches both have a mean score of 60. In batch one every student scored between 58 and 62; in batch two scores ran from 30 to 90. The central tendency is identical, but the variance of batch two is far larger. Two sets of data can share a centre and differ completely in spread, which is why both kinds of measure are reported together. Option d is right because variance describes scatter, not the centre. Exam trap: 'NOT' questions list three members of one family and one from a neighbouring family. Variance, standard deviation and range often hide among mean, median and mode; anything that measures spread belongs to variability, not central tendency.", "topic": "Central tendency versus dispersion"}, {"q": "The frequency distribution of continuous variables can be depicted by a graphic representation called:", "options": {"a": "Histogram", "b": "Composite Bar chart", "c": "Correlation line", "d": "Linear regression"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: a histogram depicts the frequency distribution of a continuous variable. In a histogram the class intervals of the variable (for example, heights of 140 to 145 cm, 145 to 150 cm and so on) run along the horizontal axis, and the frequency of each interval is shown by a bar. The bars touch each other with no gaps, because the variable is continuous: one class ends exactly where the next begins. Strictly, it is the area of each bar that is proportional to the frequency, which matters when class intervals are unequal. Karl Pearson (1895) coined the word 'histogram'. Joining the midpoints of the tops of the bars gives a frequency polygon, and a cumulative frequency curve is called an ogive; both are also used for continuous data. Example: Dr Arjun weighs 200 newborns in a district hospital and groups the weights into intervals of 250 g. A histogram instantly shows whether most babies cluster near 3 kg and whether there is a long tail of low birth weight babies on the left. Option a is right because the histogram is the standard graph built for grouped continuous data. Exam trap: bar charts and histograms look alike. Bar charts (simple, multiple or composite) have gaps between the bars and suit discrete or categorical data, such as the number of patients in each diagnosis. If the variable is continuous, think histogram, frequency polygon or ogive; if it is categorical, think bar chart or pie chart.", "topic": "Histogram for continuous data"}, {"q": "Find the mode and median in the set of data provided for weights of 8 children: 30, 35, 40, 45, 50, 50, 55, 60", "options": {"a": "Mode 30; Median 50", "b": "Mode 47.5; Median 50", "c": "Mode 50; Median 47.5", "d": "Mode 60; Median 50"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the mode is 50 and the median is 47.5. The mode is the value that occurs most often. In the set 30, 35, 40, 45, 50, 50, 55, 60, every weight appears once except 50, which appears twice, so the mode is 50. The median is the middle value once the data are arranged in order. With an odd number of values it is simply the middle one; with an even number it is the mean of the two middle values. Here n = 8, so the middle positions are the 4th and 5th, that is the (n/2)th and (n/2 + 1)th values. The 4th value is 45 and the 5th is 50. Median = (45 + 50) / 2 = 95 / 2 = 47.5. The data are already in ascending order, but always check this before finding a median, or you will pick the wrong middle. For comparison, the mean is 365 / 8, about 45.6. Example: Riya, a school counsellor, records these weights for eight children in her group. If a ninth child weighing 70 kg joins, n becomes 9, the median moves to the single middle (5th) value, which is 50, and the mode stays 50. Option c is right because 50 is the only repeated value and 47.5 is the average of the two central values. Exam trap: option b gives the same two numbers with their roles reversed, catching anyone who calculates correctly but mislabels. Another trap is to take 50 as the median because it is the 5th of 8 values; with an even count there is no single middle value.", "topic": "Mode and median calculation"}, {"q": "From the list below, the highest level of evidence in scientific literature is generated by", "options": {"a": "Expert committee recommendations", "b": "Observational studies", "c": "Randomized controlled trials", "d": "Systematic review of correlation studies"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: of these options, randomised controlled trials generate the highest level of evidence. Evidence-based medicine ranks study designs by how well they protect against bias. The usual hierarchy, from top to bottom, is: systematic reviews and meta-analyses of randomised controlled trials (RCTs); single well-run RCTs; cohort studies; case-control studies; case series and case reports; and finally expert opinion and committee recommendations. RCTs rank high because randomisation spreads both known and unknown confounding factors equally between groups, so a difference in outcome can be credited to the treatment; blinding and concealed allocation reduce bias further. The first modern RCT was the Medical Research Council streptomycin trial for tuberculosis (1948), designed by Austin Bradford Hill. Archie Cochrane (1972), in Effectiveness and Efficiency, argued that health care should be judged by such trials, and the Cochrane Collaboration (1993) now pools them. Example: Kabir wants to know whether a yoga programme reduces anxiety. Twenty observational studies showing that people who do yoga are less anxious cannot rule out that calmer people choose yoga; one RCT that randomly assigns 200 patients to yoga or a waiting list can. Option c is right because it is the only option based on randomised, experimental evidence. Exam trap: option d contains the magic words 'systematic review', but a review is only as strong as the studies it pools. A systematic review of correlation (observational) studies inherits their confounding, so it sits below a well-run RCT. Only a systematic review of RCTs sits above a single RCT.", "topic": "Hierarchy of evidence"}, {"q": "The mean temperature of 25 patients is 100°F and the Standard deviation 0.5. Calculate the standard error of the mean:", "options": {"a": "0.02", "b": "0.05", "c": "0.1", "d": "2.0"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the standard error of the mean is 0.1. The standard error of the mean (SE or SEM) tells us how much a sample mean would vary if we drew many samples of the same size from the same population. The standard deviation (SD) describes the spread of individual scores; the standard error describes the spread of sample means, which is always smaller because averaging cancels out individual extremes. The formula is SE = SD / square root of n. Working: n = 25, so the square root of n is 5. SD = 0.5. SE = 0.5 / 5 = 0.1. The mean temperature itself (100°F) is not needed for the calculation; it is there to distract. The SE is used to build confidence intervals: the 95 per cent confidence interval is the mean plus or minus 1.96 SE, here 100 plus or minus about 0.2, so roughly 99.8 to 100.2°F. Example: Meera measures the temperature of 25 patients in one ward. If she measured 100 patients instead, the SE would fall to 0.5 / 10 = 0.05; quadrupling the sample only halves the error. Larger samples give more precise estimates of the population mean, but with diminishing returns. Option c is right because dividing the SD by the square root of the sample size gives exactly 0.1. Exam trap: dividing by n instead of by its square root gives 0.02 (option a), the most common slip. Remember too that the SE can never be larger than the SD when n is more than 1, which rules out option d at a glance.", "topic": "Standard error of the mean"}, {"q": "Cognitive behaviour therapy was first developed by", "options": {"a": "Beck", "b": "Freud", "c": "Erickson", "d": "Piaget"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: cognitive behaviour therapy was first developed by Aaron Beck. Aaron Temkin Beck (1921 to 2021), an American psychiatrist at the University of Pennsylvania who trained in psychoanalysis at the Philadelphia Psychoanalytic Institute, set out in the late 1950s to test Sigmund Freud's (1917) idea that depression is anger turned inward. Instead he found that his depressed patients had a stream of quick, automatic negative thoughts about themselves, their world and their future: the cognitive triad. He described this cognitive model in Depression (1967) and turned it into a structured, short-term therapy, set out in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979). In Beck's model, early experiences create core beliefs and schemas ('I am unlovable'); in stressful situations these produce automatic thoughts shaped by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, catastrophising and all-or-nothing thinking. Therapy teaches the patient to catch these thoughts, test them against evidence and replace them with balanced ones, using homework, thought records and behavioural experiments. Joined with behaviour therapy techniques, this became what is now called CBT, the most researched psychotherapy. Example: Kabir fails one test and thinks 'I am a failure; I will never get a job'. His therapist asks him to list the evidence for and against that thought and to rate his belief in it before and after. Option a is right because Beck founded cognitive therapy, the core of CBT. Exam trap: Albert Ellis (1955) developed rational emotive behaviour therapy slightly earlier and is a co-pioneer of the cognitive approach; if Ellis and Beck both appear, read the stem carefully, but when only Beck is offered, Beck is the answer.", "topic": "Beck and cognitive therapy"}, {"q": "The symptom that is more suggestive of Mania than Severe depression is:", "options": {"a": "Overconfidence", "b": "Persecutory delusions", "c": "Weight loss", "d": "Psychomotor retardation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: overconfidence points to mania rather than severe depression. A manic episode in DSM-5-TR is a distinct period, lasting at least one week (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed), of abnormally elevated, expansive or irritable mood with increased energy or activity. Its symptoms include inflated self-esteem or grandiosity, decreased need for sleep, pressure of speech, flight of ideas, distractibility, increased goal-directed activity and risky behaviour such as spending sprees. Overconfidence is the everyday face of inflated self-esteem: the patient believes he can do anything, has special talents or will soon make a fortune. Severe depression shows the opposite pole: low self-esteem, guilt, worthlessness, hopelessness, slowed movement and thought, poor appetite and weight loss. Example: Arjun, a 24-year-old clerk, has slept three hours a night for a week, tells everyone he will soon be India's richest film producer, and has taken a large loan to fund the film. His overconfidence and grandiose plans mark a manic episode. Psychotic features can appear at both poles, so their presence does not separate the two; their content does. Manic delusions are usually grandiose (special powers, a mission), while depressive delusions are of guilt, poverty, illness or nihilism. Option a is right because inflated self-esteem is a core criterion of mania and runs counter to the self-blame of depression. Exam trap: persecutory delusions sound dramatic and so feel 'manic', but they occur in severe depression too (the patient may feel he deserves to be punished) and in schizophrenia, so they do not discriminate between the two mood states.", "topic": "Mania versus depression symptoms"}, {"q": "An accident victim loses his right hand and this results in his being unemployed. This situation is best explained by the term", "options": {"a": "Handicap", "b": "Disability", "c": "Impairment", "d": "Illness"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: losing his job because of the lost hand is a handicap. The World Health Organization's International Classification of Impairments, Disabilities and Handicaps (ICIDH, 1980) described the consequences of disease or injury in three linked steps. Impairment is any loss or abnormality of body structure or function, at the level of the body: here, the loss of the right hand. Disability is any restriction or lack of ability to perform an activity in the normal way, at the level of the person: he cannot write, lift or use tools with that hand. Handicap is the disadvantage the person then faces in society, which stops him filling a role that is normal for his age, sex and culture: he loses his job and income. The chain runs disease, then impairment, then disability, then handicap. In 2001 the WHO replaced the ICIDH with the International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health (ICF), which renamed these levels body functions and structures, activity limitations and participation restrictions, and stressed that the environment (ramps, adapted jobs) decides how far an impairment disadvantages someone. Example: Meera, a tailor, loses the sight of one eye after an injury (impairment), cannot thread needles quickly (disability) and loses her customers (handicap). Option a is right because the stem ends in unemployment, a social disadvantage, the defining mark of handicap. Exam trap: examiners offer the first link in the chain (the lost hand, impairment) and the second (cannot do tasks with it, disability); always look at where the stem ends. Loss of a body part is impairment; loss of a social role is handicap.", "topic": "Impairment, disability and handicap"}, {"q": "In Psychological Rehabilitation, the main aim is to", "options": {"a": "Restore social relations", "b": "Provide financial support", "c": "Restore personal confidence", "d": "Restore capacity to earn livelihood"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: psychological rehabilitation aims to restore personal confidence. Rehabilitation is the combined use of medical, social, educational and vocational measures to train or retrain a person to the highest possible level of functional ability after disease or injury. It is the main work of tertiary prevention, once disability has already set in, and it aims at function and a valued life rather than cure. The community-medicine textbook of K. Park (Preventive and Social Medicine) divides it into four areas, each with its own aim. Medical rehabilitation restores function (for example, physiotherapy and an artificial limb). Vocational rehabilitation restores the capacity to earn a livelihood (job training, sheltered workshops, supported employment). Social rehabilitation restores family and social relationships. Psychological rehabilitation restores personal dignity and confidence, helping the person accept the change and regain self-esteem and a sense of worth. Example: Kabir, a 30-year-old driver, loses a leg in a road accident. Surgery and a prosthesis are medical rehabilitation; training as a mobile phone repair technician is vocational; helping his family and friends include him again is social; counselling that helps him stop seeing himself as a burden and believe in himself again is psychological. The four overlap in practice, but each has one defining aim. Option c is right because confidence and dignity are inner, psychological states, the specific target of psychological rehabilitation. Exam trap: all four options sound like good things a rehabilitation team does, so students choose the broadest. Match each aim to its named type instead: earning goes with vocational, relationships with social, confidence with psychological.", "topic": "Types of rehabilitation"}, {"q": "The fluctuation of a disease trend that occurs over a long period of time (years) is defined by:", "options": {"a": "Seasonal trend", "b": "Cyclic trend", "c": "Secular trend", "d": "Propagated trend"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a change in disease frequency over a long period, years or decades, is a secular trend. Epidemiologists describe how disease occurrence varies with time in a few standard patterns. A secular trend is a slow, consistent rise or fall over many years: the long decline of tuberculosis in Europe even before effective drugs, the rise of coronary heart disease, diabetes and obesity in India over recent decades, or the fall in the age at menarche over the last century. Cyclic trends are rises and falls that recur at intervals of a few years, often because the pool of susceptible people builds up and is then exhausted; measles epidemics every two to three years before mass vaccination are the classic example. Seasonal trends repeat within the year, linked to climate or vectors: dengue and malaria peaking during and after the monsoon. Short-term fluctuations, such as epidemics, form a further category. Example: Riya plots district data on hypertension from 1990 to 2020 and sees a steady climb across thirty years; that is a secular trend. If she plotted monthly dengue cases instead, she would see a peak every September or October, a seasonal trend. Option c is right because 'secular' means belonging to an age or long period, which matches 'over a long period of time (years)'. Exam trap: 'propagated' sounds like a time pattern but belongs to the types of epidemic. A propagated epidemic spreads from person to person in successive waves, unlike a common-source epidemic that comes from one shared source such as contaminated water.", "topic": "Time trends in disease"}, {"q": "Confounding Bias in a case-control scientific study can be removed by:", "options": {"a": "Matching", "b": "Purposive sampling", "c": "Accurate recall", "d": "Performing studies in different hospitals"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: confounding in a case-control study can be removed by matching. A confounder is a third variable that is linked to the exposure and is itself a cause of the outcome, so it can create a false association or hide a true one. The classic textbook example: alcohol drinkers seem to get more lung cancer, but drinkers also smoke more, and smoking causes lung cancer, so smoking confounds the alcohol and lung cancer link. In an experiment, randomisation spreads confounders evenly, but a case-control study cannot randomise because the outcome has already happened. Matching is the main remedy at the design stage: for each case, controls are chosen who are similar on the suspected confounder (age, sex, smoking, social class), so that it cannot differ between the groups. Matching can be individual (pair matching, one or more controls per case) or by group (frequency matching). At the analysis stage, confounding can also be handled by stratification and multivariable analysis. Example: Dr Arjun studies whether chewing tobacco causes oral cancer. Because both are commoner in older men, he matches each patient with a control of the same age and sex, so any remaining difference in tobacco use cannot be due to age or sex. A caution: do not match on the exposure itself or on something closely tied to it (overmatching), or the real association is hidden. Option a is right because matching makes cases and controls equal on the confounder, removing its influence. Exam trap: the other options fix different biases. Accurate recall tackles recall bias, an information bias; spreading the study across hospitals tackles selection bias. Neither removes confounding.", "topic": "Controlling confounding by matching"}, {"q": "A study on the experiences of 100 slum-dwelling post-partum women in the first 4 weeks after delivery of live-born baby by use of a questionnaire is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Experimental study", "b": "Cohort study", "c": "Qualitative study", "d": "Interventional study"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a study of the experiences of slum-dwelling post-partum women is best classed as a qualitative study. Qualitative research asks what people experience and what it means to them, rather than measuring how much or how often something happens. It collects words, stories and observations through in-depth interviews, focus groups, open-ended questionnaires and participant observation, and analyses them for recurring themes. Its roots lie in phenomenology, the study of lived experience associated with Edmund Husserl (1913), and in later traditions such as grounded theory (Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, 1967) and ethnography. Samples are usually purposive, chosen because they have lived through the experience, and findings are judged by trustworthiness rather than statistical significance. Quantitative research, by contrast, counts, measures and tests hypotheses with numbers. Example: Riya, a health psychologist, asks 100 new mothers in a Mumbai slum open questions during their first four weeks after birth: how they feel, who helps them, what worries them about feeding and money. She codes the answers into themes such as isolation, family support and fear for the baby's health. Option c is right because the stem's focus on 'experiences' signals qualitative inquiry, and nothing in it involves an intervention or the follow-up of exposed and unexposed groups. Exam trap: the 4-week time frame tempts students towards a cohort study, but a cohort compares the incidence of an outcome between exposed and unexposed groups; here there is no exposure comparison at all. Key note: a questionnaire given to 100 women is, strictly, closer to a descriptive cross-sectional survey; but among the options the study of lived experiences is the qualitative one, and the other three need an intervention or the follow-up of an exposure, so c stands.", "topic": "Qualitative research"}, {"q": "The first experimental psychology laboratory was established in:", "options": {"a": "Johns Hopkins, USA", "b": "University of Leipzig, Germany", "c": "King's College, London", "d": "The Sorbonne, Paris"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the first experimental psychology laboratory was established at the University of Leipzig, Germany. Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920), a physician and physiologist who held a chair of philosophy, set it up in 1879, the year usually given as the birth of psychology as an independent science. It began in a small room where Wundt had stored the apparatus he used in lecture demonstrations; by 1879 students there were running original experiments, and the first dissertation from it was Max Friedrich's study of the duration of apperception. Wundt used controlled introspection by trained observers, together with reaction-time instruments such as the Hipp chronoscope, to study sensations, feelings and attention. He also founded the first psychology journal, Philosophische Studien (1881), and trained many pioneers, including Edward Titchener (1867 to 1927), James McKeen Cattell (1860 to 1944) and G. Stanley Hall (1844 to 1924), who carried the laboratory model to Britain and America. Titchener later systematised the approach as structuralism, which aimed to break conscious experience into its basic elements. Example: if Arjun is asked in an interview why 1879 matters, the answer is that a laboratory devoted to psychological experiments, rather than to physiology or philosophy, first opened in Leipzig that year. Option b is right because Leipzig housed the first formal laboratory built for psychological research. Exam trap: William James (1875) had a small demonstration room at Harvard even earlier, but it was for teaching, not research; and Hall's 1883 laboratory at Johns Hopkins was the first in America, not the first in the world.", "topic": "Wundt's Leipzig laboratory"}, {"q": "The part of the brain that is involved in speech production is called:", "options": {"a": "Broca's area", "b": "Wernicke's area", "c": "Foramen of Monro", "d": "Sylvian fissure"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: Broca's area is the part of the brain involved in speech production. It lies in the left inferior frontal gyrus (Brodmann areas 44 and 45), just in front of the part of the motor cortex that controls the face, tongue and larynx. Paul Broca (1861) described a patient, Louis Victor Leborgne, nicknamed 'Tan' because it was almost the only syllable he could say. He understood speech but could not produce it, and at autopsy Broca found a lesion in the left frontal lobe. After more cases he concluded (1865) that 'we speak with the left hemisphere', the first strong evidence for the localisation and lateralisation of language. Damage to the area produces Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia: slow, effortful, telegraphic speech that drops small grammatical words ('Want... water... now'), with comprehension relatively preserved. Patients are aware of their errors and frustrated by them. Example: after a left frontal stroke, Kabir understands his doctor's questions but can answer only in halting two-word phrases. Option a is right because Broca's area plans the articulation and grammar of spoken language. Exam trap: Wernicke's area is its partner and the commonest distractor. Wernicke's area serves comprehension; damage gives fluent but meaningless speech and poor understanding. Memory aid: Broca means broken speech, Wernicke means wordy nonsense. The arcuate fasciculus links the two, and damage to it causes conduction aphasia, in which repetition is especially poor.", "topic": "Broca's area and speech"}, {"q": "The following system is responsible for the \"fight or flight\" response:", "options": {"a": "Limbic system", "b": "Sympathetic nervous system", "c": "Hippocampus", "d": "Parasympathetic nervous system"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the sympathetic nervous system is responsible for the fight or flight response. The autonomic nervous system, which runs the internal organs without conscious control, has two branches that usually work in opposition. The sympathetic branch mobilises the body for emergencies; Walter Cannon (1915), in Bodily Changes in Pain, Hunger, Fear and Rage, called this the 'fight or flight' reaction. When danger is detected, sympathetic nerves, which leave the spinal cord from its thoracic and lumbar segments, raise heart rate and blood pressure, dilate the pupils and the airways, release glucose from the liver, divert blood from the gut and skin to the muscles, slow digestion and increase sweating. They also stimulate the adrenal medulla to pour adrenaline and noradrenaline into the blood, which prolongs the arousal. The parasympathetic branch (cranial and sacral outflow, largely through the vagus nerve) does the opposite, 'rest and digest': it slows the heart, constricts the pupils and stimulates digestion, restoring calm once the threat passes. Example: Meera is walking home at night when a dog leaps at her; her heart pounds, her mouth goes dry and she runs faster than she thought possible. Ten minutes later, safe at home, her breathing slows as parasympathetic activity takes over. Option b is right because the sympathetic system is the effector system that actually produces the bodily changes of fight or flight. Exam trap: the limbic system, and the amygdala within it, detects threat and triggers the response through the hypothalamus, so it tempts students; but the bodily response itself is carried out by the sympathetic system.", "topic": "Sympathetic fight or flight"}, {"q": "If you are analysing causes of rebirth, then the field of this study is:", "options": {"a": "Developmental psychology", "b": "Para-psychology", "c": "Social psychology", "d": "Precognition"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the study of rebirth belongs to parapsychology. Parapsychology is the scientific study of claimed paranormal phenomena, events that seem to go beyond known physical and psychological explanation. Its main topics are extrasensory perception (telepathy, clairvoyance and precognition), psychokinesis (mind affecting matter), and survival research: near-death experiences, mediumship and claims of reincarnation or rebirth. Joseph Banks Rhine (1934), in Extra-Sensory Perception, made the field experimental at Duke University using Zener cards, where chance is 5 hits in 25. Research on rebirth is linked above all with the psychiatrist Ian Stevenson (1966), whose book Twenty Cases Suggestive of Reincarnation reported children, several of them in India, who claimed memories of a previous life; he tried to check their statements against the lives of people who had died. Most scientists remain sceptical because such findings have not been reliably replicated under controlled conditions. Example: Kabir, a psychology student, hears of a four-year-old girl in a village who says she was a woman from a nearby town in her past life. A study of whether her statements can be verified, or explained without rebirth, falls squarely within parapsychology. Option b is right because rebirth is a claimed paranormal phenomenon, the subject matter of parapsychology. Exam trap: precognition is a tempting distractor because it is a parapsychological term, but it names one phenomenon (knowledge of future events), not a field of study. Developmental psychology studies change across one lifespan, from conception to death, not across lives.", "topic": "Parapsychology"}, {"q": "The basic Laws of Gestalt Psychology include", "options": {"a": "Rationalization", "b": "Perceptual grouping", "c": "Psycho-analysis", "d": "Behaviourism"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: perceptual grouping is one of the basic laws of Gestalt psychology. Gestalt psychology began with Max Wertheimer's (1912) study of the phi phenomenon, the apparent movement seen when two lights flash in quick succession, and was developed with Wolfgang Kohler (1887 to 1967) and Kurt Koffka (1886 to 1941). 'Gestalt' is German for form or whole, and the school's slogan is that the whole is different from the sum of its parts. Against structuralism, which broke experience into elements, the Gestaltists argued that the brain organises sensations into meaningful wholes automatically. Wertheimer (1923) set out the laws of perceptual organisation, or grouping: proximity (things close together are seen as a group), similarity (similar items group together), closure (we fill gaps to see complete figures), continuity or good continuation (we follow smooth lines), common fate (things moving together belong together) and, over all of them, the law of Pragnanz: we see the simplest, most stable organisation possible. Figure and ground, shown by Edgar Rubin's (1915) vase that turns into two faces, is a closely related principle. Example: Riya looks at a row of dots spaced in pairs and sees 'pairs' rather than single dots; that is proximity at work. Option b is right because grouping is the core of the Gestalt laws of perception. Exam trap: every option is a real psychological term, but the other three belong elsewhere. Rationalisation is a psychoanalytic defence, and psychoanalysis and behaviourism are rival schools that the Gestalt psychologists argued against, not laws within Gestalt psychology.", "topic": "Gestalt laws of grouping"}, {"q": "The following is a part of the Peripheral Nervous System:", "options": {"a": "Midbrain", "b": "Cerebellum", "c": "Intercostal nerves", "d": "Thalamus"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the intercostal nerves are part of the peripheral nervous system. The nervous system has two main divisions. The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord, protected by bone, the meninges and cerebrospinal fluid. The peripheral nervous system (PNS) is everything outside them: the 12 pairs of cranial nerves, the 31 pairs of spinal nerves with their branches, and the ganglia. The PNS carries sensory information in and motor commands out, and is itself divided into the somatic nervous system (skin, skeletal muscle, voluntary movement) and the autonomic nervous system (internal organs, with sympathetic and parasympathetic branches). The intercostal nerves are the front branches (anterior rami) of the thoracic spinal nerves T1 to T11, running in the spaces between the ribs. They supply the intercostal muscles used in breathing and the skin of the chest and abdominal wall. Because they lie outside the spinal cord, they belong to the PNS, mainly to its somatic division. Example: Arjun develops shingles in one band across his chest. The virus has reactivated in the dorsal root ganglion of a thoracic spinal nerve and spread along its intercostal nerve, a peripheral structure. Option c is right because it is the only named structure that lies outside the brain and spinal cord. Exam trap: the three distractors come from different levels of the brain (midbrain from the brainstem, cerebellum from the hindbrain, thalamus from the diencephalon), so students who think 'not the cerebrum, therefore peripheral' fall into them. Every part of the brain, however deep or low, is CNS.", "topic": "Peripheral nervous system"}, {"q": "The neurotransmitter that is decreased in anxiety disorders is:", "options": {"a": "Glutamate", "b": "Gamma amino butyric acid", "c": "Serotonin", "d": "Acetylcholine"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: gamma aminobutyric acid (GABA) is the neurotransmitter classically described as decreased in anxiety disorders. GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. When it binds to the GABA-A receptor, a chloride channel opens, negative chloride ions flow into the neuron, and the neuron becomes less likely to fire. It is the brain's brake, balancing glutamate, the main excitatory transmitter. The GABA theory of anxiety grew from drug evidence. Benzodiazepines such as diazepam and alprazolam relieve anxiety within minutes by binding to their own site on the GABA-A receptor and making the channel open more often when GABA is present; barbiturates and alcohol also enhance GABA and also calm anxiety. Drugs that block the receptor provoke anxiety and even seizures, and imaging studies have reported fewer benzodiazepine binding sites or lower GABA levels in some patients with panic disorder. Too little GABA activity is therefore thought to leave the fear circuit, centred on the amygdala, poorly restrained. Noradrenaline from the locus coeruleus drives the alarm, and serotonin modulates it, which is why SSRIs are first-line long-term treatment. Example: Meera, who has panic disorder, is given clonazepam in the emergency room and feels calm within the hour, because the drug strengthens GABA's inhibition. Option b is right because the GABA-benzodiazepine system is the classic anxiety system, and reduced GABA activity is its standard explanation. Exam trap: serotonin is famous from depression, and SSRIs are used in anxiety, so students choose it; but the textbook 'decreased in anxiety' transmitter is GABA. Decreased acetylcholine belongs to Alzheimer's disease.", "topic": "GABA and anxiety"}, {"q": "Hypercalcaemic tetany can occur with disorder of:", "options": {"a": "Pituitary gland", "b": "Adrenal gland", "c": "Thyroid gland", "d": "Parathyroid gland"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: tetany occurs with disorder of the parathyroid glands. The parathyroids are four small glands, each about the size of a grain of rice, on the back of the thyroid in the neck. They secrete parathyroid hormone (PTH), which raises blood calcium by releasing calcium from bone, reducing its loss in urine and, through activated vitamin D, increasing its absorption from the gut. Calcium stabilises nerve membranes. When blood calcium falls, nerves become hyperexcitable and fire spontaneously, causing tetany: tingling around the mouth and in the fingers, muscle cramps, spasm of the hands and feet (carpopedal spasm) and, in severe cases, spasm of the larynx and seizures. Two bedside signs reveal it: Chvostek's sign (tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear makes the face twitch) and Trousseau's sign (inflating a blood pressure cuff on the arm produces spasm of the wrist and hand). The classic cause is hypoparathyroidism, often after the parathyroids are damaged or removed by accident during thyroid surgery. Low calcium can also cause anxiety, irritability and depression. Example: Riya develops tingling fingers and hand cramps two days after a thyroidectomy; her calcium is low because her parathyroids were injured. Option d is right because parathyroid hormone controls blood calcium, and its lack produces tetany. Exam trap: the thyroid tempts because the trouble often follows thyroid surgery, but it is the neighbouring parathyroid glands that matter. Key note: the stem's 'hypercalcaemic' is an error in the paper; tetany is caused by hypocalcaemia, while high calcium instead causes weakness, kidney stones, bone pain and confusion. Parathyroid hormone controls blood calcium either way, so d stands.", "topic": "Parathyroid and tetany"}, {"q": "The following is a correct term in substance use disorder regarding Psychological Dependence:", "options": {"a": "Kindling", "b": "Craving", "c": "Withdrawal symptoms", "d": "Tolerance"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: craving is the term that belongs to psychological dependence. Dependence on a substance has two faces. Physical (physiological) dependence means the body has adapted to the drug, shown by tolerance (needing more to get the same effect) and withdrawal (unpleasant symptoms, often the opposite of the drug's effect, when it is stopped). Psychological dependence is a compelling need to use the substance for its pleasure or to relieve distress, shown above all by craving: an intense, often overwhelming desire to use. Craving is often set off by cues linked with past use, such as a place, a friend or a time of day, through classical conditioning. The ICD-10 dependence syndrome lists 'a strong desire or sense of compulsion to take the substance' as a criterion, and DSM-5 (2013) added craving as a criterion of substance use disorder while dropping legal problems. Psychological dependence can exist without much physical dependence; cocaine, for example, produces intense craving with relatively mild bodily withdrawal. Example: Arjun has not smoked for a month and no longer has physical withdrawal, yet whenever he drinks tea after lunch he feels a powerful urge to light a cigarette. That urge is craving, the mark of psychological dependence and a major cause of relapse. Option b is right because craving is a mental desire, not a bodily adaptation. Exam trap: tolerance and withdrawal are the best-known dependence terms, so students choose them, but both are signs of physical dependence. Kindling is a neurobiological sensitisation idea, not a dependence term.", "topic": "Craving and psychological dependence"}, {"q": "Identify the correct statement", "options": {"a": "Illusion cannot occur in normal people", "b": "External stimuli are absent in Hallucination", "c": "External stimuli are absent in Illusion", "d": "Hallucination are common in normal persons"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: external stimuli are absent in a hallucination. Perception normally begins with a real stimulus: light, sound or touch reaches a sense organ and the brain interprets it. Descriptive psychopathology sorts abnormal perceptions by whether such a stimulus is present. An illusion is a misinterpretation of a real external stimulus. At dusk Meera sees a coiled rope on the path and takes it for a snake; the rope exists, and only her reading of it is wrong. Illusions are common in healthy people, especially when light is dim, attention is poor or emotion is high. Karl Jaspers (1913) described three kinds: completion illusions (reading a misprint as the correct word), affect illusions (a frightened person sees a coat on a hook as an intruder) and pareidolia (faces seen in clouds). A hallucination, by contrast, is a perception without an adequate external stimulus, experienced as real and located in outer space, like Kabir hearing a voice call his name in an empty room. Auditory hallucinations are commonest in schizophrenia, visual ones suggest delirium or drugs, and olfactory ones temporal lobe epilepsy. In healthy people hallucinations are uncommon; the main normal exceptions are brief hypnagogic (falling asleep) and hypnopompic (waking) experiences. Option b is right because the absence of any real stimulus is the defining feature that separates a hallucination from an illusion. Exam trap: examiners simply swap the two definitions, so option c (stimulus absent in illusion) reverses the rule; option d stretches the fact that hypnagogic hallucinations are normal into the false claim that hallucinations are common.", "topic": "Illusion versus hallucination"}, {"q": "The process of learning involving \"Trial and Error\" was based on work of:", "options": {"a": "Bloom", "b": "Pavlov", "c": "Skinner", "d": "Thorndike"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: trial-and-error learning comes from the work of Edward Thorndike (1898) on cats in puzzle boxes. Thorndike, an American psychologist at Columbia University, shut a hungry cat inside a wooden box closed by a latch, with food in sight outside. The cat clawed, pushed and meowed at random until, by accident, it pressed the lever and escaped. Over repeated trials the useless movements dropped out and the correct one came sooner, so escape time fell gradually rather than suddenly. Thorndike concluded that the cat did not reason its way out; it stamped in the response that worked and stamped out those that failed. He reported this in Animal Intelligence (1898) and stated the law of effect, formally named in 1911: a response followed by a satisfying state is strengthened, and one followed by discomfort is weakened. He added the law of exercise (practice strengthens a bond) and the law of readiness. Because his theory explains learning as stimulus-response bonds, it is called connectionism. An everyday case: Arjun, given a new phone with no instructions, presses buttons until one combination opens the camera, and next time goes straight to it. Option d is right because Thorndike's puzzle-box studies are the origin of the trial-and-error account of learning, and his law of effect later became the base of operant conditioning. Exam trap: B. F. Skinner (1938) is the popular wrong pick because he built directly on the law of effect, but his work concerned reinforcement and schedules in the Skinner box; Wolfgang Köhler (1887 to 1967), by contrast, described sudden insight in chimpanzees, the opposite of gradual trial and error.", "topic": "Thorndike's trial-and-error learning"}, {"q": "In Pavlov's classical conditioning, the ringing of the bell is:", "options": {"a": "Unconditioned stimulus", "b": "Conditioned stimulus", "c": "Neutral stimulus", "d": "Negative conditioning"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: in the classical conditioning experiments of Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936), the ringing bell becomes the conditioned stimulus. Pavlov, a Russian physiologist who won the 1904 Nobel Prize for work on digestion, noticed in the late 1890s that his dogs salivated before food reached them, at the sight of the dish or the sound of the attendant. He turned this into a method. Classical conditioning has four terms. The unconditioned stimulus (UCS) is food, which naturally triggers the unconditioned response (UCR), salivation, with no learning. A neutral stimulus, such as a bell, at first produces no salivation, only an orienting response (the dog pricks up its ears). During acquisition the bell is sounded just before the food on repeated trials. After enough pairings the bell alone makes the dog salivate. The bell is now the conditioned stimulus (CS), and salivation to it is the conditioned response (CR), usually a little weaker than the UCR. Timing matters: forward (delay) conditioning, with the bell slightly before the food, works best, while backward conditioning barely works at all. Example: Riya's phone plays a tone whenever her mother messages that lunch is ready; after a few weeks the tone alone makes her hungry. Option b is right because the question asks the bell's role in the conditioning, and the whole point of the procedure is that a once-neutral bell comes to elicit salivation as a conditioned stimulus. Exam trap: option c (neutral stimulus) is true only before conditioning; once the bell has been paired with food it is no longer neutral.", "topic": "Classical conditioning stimuli"}, {"q": "In a picture of a room, two lines have a similar length in reality BUT appear to have different lengths to the observer, it is called:", "options": {"a": "Visual Hallucination", "b": "Poggendorff illusion", "c": "Muller-Lyer illusion", "d": "Refractory error"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: two lines that are physically equal but look unequal in length is the Müller-Lyer illusion. Franz Carl Müller-Lyer (1889), a German psychiatrist, drew two shafts of the same length. One ends in outward-pointing fins and looks longer; the other ends in inward-pointing arrowheads and looks shorter. Measuring them with a ruler does not remove the effect. The stem's 'picture of a room' points to the best-known explanation. Richard Gregory (1963) argued that the figures copy corners: the arrowheads resemble the near, outside corner of a building jutting towards you, while the fins resemble the far, inside corner of a room receding away. The brain applies size constancy, enlarging whatever it judges to be farther away, so the 'far' corner line is seen as longer. This is called inappropriate constancy scaling. Cross-cultural work by Marshall Segall, Donald Campbell and Melville Herskovits (1966) supports it: people raised in a 'carpentered world' of rectangular rooms are more susceptible than people who grow up in round huts. An illusion is a misperception of a real stimulus; the receptors register the lines correctly and the error lies in interpretation, so illusions distort perception, not sensation. Example: Kabir measures the two vertical lines in a drawing of a room corner, finds both are 5 cm, and still sees one as longer. Option c is right because the Müller-Lyer figure is the classic illusion of equal lengths seen as unequal. Exam trap: the Ponzo illusion (Mario Ponzo, 1911) also makes equal lines look unequal, using converging railway tracks, but it is not offered; Poggendorff is offered, and it distorts the alignment of a diagonal line, not its length.", "topic": "Müller-Lyer illusion"}, {"q": "You shift to a new neighbourhood near an Air Force Base. You meet your new neighbour, and talk to him for some time. When you return your wife asks you, \"What does he do for a living?\" You had not asked his profession. You say, \"He is in the Air Force\". Your interpretation has been based on:", "options": {"a": "Rational thinking", "b": "Heuristics", "c": "Critical thinking", "d": "Hypothesis generation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: guessing that the neighbour is in the Air Force, without asking, is a judgement based on a heuristic. A heuristic is a mental shortcut or rule of thumb that gives a quick, usually good-enough answer without weighing all the evidence. Herbert Simon (1955) argued that people have bounded rationality: limited time, information and memory, so they 'satisfice', settling for an answer that is good enough rather than the best. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1974) then described the main heuristics of judgement and the biases they cause. The representativeness heuristic judges probability by how closely a case resembles a typical member of a category: he lives near the base, so he 'looks like' an Air Force man. The availability heuristic judges likelihood by how easily examples come to mind: near a base, Air Force personnel are the people you picture first. Both fit the stem. The cost is bias. Representativeness ignores base rates, since many people living near a base are teachers, shopkeepers or doctors. Kahneman later placed heuristics in fast, intuitive System 1 thinking, as opposed to slow, deliberate System 2. Example: Meera sees a man in a white coat in a hospital corridor and assumes he is a doctor, though he might be a laboratory technician. Option b is right because the answer came from a rapid shortcut based on resemblance and setting, with no evidence collected and no alternatives considered. Exam trap: hypothesis generation sounds right because a guess is a kind of hypothesis, but in psychology it means deliberately proposing a testable explanation that is then checked, which is exactly what did not happen here.", "topic": "Heuristics in judgement"}, {"q": "The following is a part of a neuron:", "options": {"a": "Sarcolemma", "b": "Axon", "c": "Myoglobin", "d": "Intercalated discs"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the axon is a part of a neuron. A neuron is a nerve cell that receives, integrates and transmits information by electrical and chemical signals. Its main parts are the dendrites, branched fibres that receive messages from other cells; the cell body or soma, which holds the nucleus and keeps the cell alive; the axon hillock, where the action potential is triggered; the axon, a single long fibre that carries the impulse away from the cell body, sometimes for a metre or more; and the axon terminals (terminal buttons), which release neurotransmitter into the synapse. Many axons are wrapped in a fatty myelin sheath, made by oligodendrocytes in the brain and spinal cord and by Schwann cells in peripheral nerves. The small gaps between myelin segments, the nodes of Ranvier, let the impulse jump from node to node (saltatory conduction), which makes it much faster. Santiago Ramón y Cajal (1888 onward) showed that neurons are separate cells, the neuron doctrine, and shared the 1906 Nobel Prize with Camillo Golgi. The direction of flow is easy to remember: dendrite, soma, axon, terminal. When Arjun touches a hot pan, sensory neurons carry the signal along their axons to the spinal cord, and motor neuron axons carry the command back to pull his hand away. Option b is right because the axon is the output fibre that is part of every typical neuron, while the other three options are all parts of muscle. Exam trap: all three distractors come from muscle physiology, and sarcolemma tempts students who know it is a cell membrane; but it is the membrane of a muscle fibre, whereas the axon's own membrane is the axolemma.", "topic": "Structure of the neuron"}, {"q": "The following part of the brain is affected in Parkinson's disease:", "options": {"a": "Thalamus", "b": "Limbic system", "c": "Substantia nigra", "d": "Hypothalamus"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: Parkinson's disease results from the loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra. James Parkinson (1817) described it as the 'shaking palsy'. Its core signs are resting tremor (a pill-rolling tremor present at rest that eases with movement), rigidity (cogwheel or lead-pipe), bradykinesia (slowness) or akinesia (difficulty starting movement), and postural instability, with a masked face, small handwriting and a shuffling gait. The substantia nigra ('black substance', dark because of the pigment neuromelanin) lies in the midbrain. Its pars compacta sends dopamine along the nigrostriatal pathway to the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen), part of the basal ganglia that help start and smooth voluntary movement. Symptoms usually appear only after roughly 60 to 80 percent of these neurons are lost. Oleh Hornykiewicz (1960) showed that the striatum of Parkinson's patients is depleted of dopamine, which led to treatment with L-dopa, the dopamine precursor that can cross the blood-brain barrier. Surviving nigral cells contain Lewy bodies, clumps of the protein alpha-synuclein. Striking support came when young drug users injected a contaminant, MPTP, that destroys nigral neurons and developed sudden severe parkinsonism (J. William Langston, 1983). Example: Kabir's grandfather has a hand tremor at rest that stops when he lifts his cup, walks with short shuffling steps and has a fixed expression. Option c is right because degeneration of the dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta is the defining pathology. Exam trap: students confuse Parkinson's with Huntington's disease, which mainly destroys the caudate nucleus of the striatum; and the thalamus tempts some because surgery for Parkinson's targets nearby nuclei, but the primary lesion is nigral.", "topic": "Parkinson's disease pathology"}, {"q": "Type A and Type B behaviour pattern can be measured by:", "options": {"a": "Jenkins Activity Survey", "b": "CT of the Brain", "c": "Rorschach inkblot test", "d": "Galvanic skin response"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Jenkins Activity Survey is the standard self-report measure of the Type A and Type B behaviour patterns. Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman (1959), two cardiologists in San Francisco, noticed that many of their heart patients were hurried, competitive and impatient. They named this the Type A behaviour pattern: competitive achievement striving, a strong sense of time urgency, and easily aroused hostility and impatience. Type B people are more relaxed and unhurried. In their Western Collaborative Group Study of about 3,000 working men, Type A men developed coronary heart disease about twice as often as Type B men; later research found hostility to be the toxic ingredient. Friedman and Rosenman first measured the pattern with the Structured Interview, in which the interviewer deliberately interrupts and challenges the person to observe loud, fast, impatient speech. Because interviews are slow and need trained raters, C. David Jenkins, working with Stephen Zyzanski and Rosenman from the mid-1960s, built the Jenkins Activity Survey (JAS), a paper-and-pencil questionnaire that gives an overall Type A score and subscales for speed and impatience, job involvement, and hard-driving competitiveness. Example: Arjun eats lunch at his desk, finishes other people's sentences and fumes in traffic; he would score high on the JAS. Option a is right because the JAS was built specifically to classify people as Type A or Type B. Exam trap: galvanic skin response sounds plausible because Type A people are physiologically reactive, but it records momentary arousal in anyone and cannot classify a stable behaviour pattern; the other recognised measure is the Structured Interview, not a brain scan or projective test.", "topic": "Measuring Type A behaviour"}, {"q": "Vygotsky's theory of the zone of proximal development indicates that children can perform at higher level by:", "options": {"a": "Regularly participating in competitive tests", "b": "Support of teachers and capable peers", "c": "Support and encouragement by parents", "d": "Attending extra tuition classes on the subject"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Lev Vygotsky (1896 to 1934) held that children can perform at a higher level with the support of teachers and more capable peers. Vygotsky, a Russian psychologist, saw development as social: higher mental functions first appear between people and are then internalised. He defined the zone of proximal development (ZPD) as the distance between a child's actual developmental level, shown by independent problem solving, and the level of potential development, shown by problem solving under adult guidance or in collaboration with more capable peers. The helper is called the more knowledgeable other. For Vygotsky, good teaching works inside this zone, just ahead of what the child can do alone, so that learning leads development. This contrasts with Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980), for whom development comes first and the child learns largely alone, like a little scientist. The support given within the zone was later named scaffolding by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976): help is adjusted to the child's level and withdrawn as competence grows. The idea also led to dynamic assessment: test, teach, then retest to see how much the child gains from help. Example: Riya, aged 8, can add two-digit numbers alone; with her teacher prompting 'carry the one' and an older classmate showing the steps, she manages three-digit sums. That gap is her ZPD. Option b is right because the ZPD is defined by what the child achieves with guidance from teachers and capable peers. Exam trap: option c (parents) is tempting, since a parent can be the more knowledgeable other, but it stresses support and encouragement, that is, emotional motivation, whereas the ZPD is about skilled guidance in the task itself.", "topic": "Zone of proximal development"}, {"q": "The parenting styles identified by Diana Baumrind include the following term:", "options": {"a": "Democratic", "b": "Secure", "c": "Protective", "d": "Authoritative"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: authoritative is one of the parenting styles described by Diana Baumrind (1966). Baumrind, an American developmental psychologist at Berkeley, observed preschool children at nursery school and at home and interviewed their parents. She described parenting along two broad dimensions, warmth (responsiveness) and control (demandingness), and identified three patterns. Authoritative parents are warm and responsive but also set clear, firm rules, explain the reasons for them and encourage independence; their children tend to be self-reliant, self-controlled, cheerful and achievement oriented. Authoritarian parents are cold and controlling ('because I said so'), rely on punishment and rarely explain; their children tend to be withdrawn, anxious or hostile. Permissive parents are warm but make few demands; their children tend to be impulsive and immature. Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) later split the low-control group into permissive (indulgent) and neglectful (uninvolved), giving four styles in all. Research across many cultures links the authoritative style with the best outcomes, though the advantage varies somewhat by culture. Example: Kabir's mother insists he finish his homework before cricket, explains why, listens when he argues and sometimes agrees to a change; that is authoritative parenting. Option d is right because 'authoritative' is Baumrind's own term for the warm and firm style she found most beneficial. Exam trap: 'democratic' is a near synonym that comes from the leadership studies of Kurt Lewin, Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White (1939), who compared authoritarian, democratic and laissez-faire leaders, so it sounds like a parenting style but is not Baumrind's label; examiners also swap authoritative with authoritarian.", "topic": "Baumrind's parenting styles"}, {"q": "A patient underwent a neurosurgical procedure resulting in cutting of the corpus callosum. He is made to sit in front of a TV monitor with the word PUNJAB written across the screen. On being asked what he sees, he will say", "options": {"a": "PUNJAB", "b": "JAB", "c": "PUN", "d": "UNJA"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: a split-brain patient looking at the centre of PUNJAB will say 'JAB'. Each eye sends information from the left visual field (everything to the left of the fixation point) to the right hemisphere, and from the right visual field to the left hemisphere, because the fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross at the optic chiasm. When the patient fixates the centre of the screen, PUN falls in the left visual field and goes to the right hemisphere, while JAB falls in the right visual field and goes to the left hemisphere. In most right-handers the left hemisphere holds speech (Broca's area). Normally the corpus callosum, the largest band of fibres joining the two hemispheres, would pass PUN across, and the person would read the whole word. Once it is cut (corpus callosotomy, done to stop intractable epilepsy spreading from one side to the other), the speaking left hemisphere knows only JAB. The right hemisphere has still seen PUN: asked to point with his left hand, which the right hemisphere controls, to a card matching what he saw, he could pick PUN while saying he saw only JAB. This is the design of the split-brain studies of Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga in the 1960s, which won Sperry the Nobel Prize in 1981; real tests flash the word for a fraction of a second so the eyes cannot move. In the classic textbook demonstration with HEART, the patient says 'ART' but his left hand points to 'HE'. Option b is right because only the right-visual-field half reaches the hemisphere that can speak. Exam trap: option c (PUN) reverses the crossover; students forget that the left visual field goes to the right hemisphere, which in most people cannot talk.", "topic": "Split-brain visual fields"}, {"q": "The speeding laws In Chandigarh are very strict. No one can drive over 50km/hour. Kamal drives his car so that the speed never exceeds 50km/hour so he never gets a speeding ticket. This is an example of", "options": {"a": "Positive reinforcement", "b": "Negative reinforcement", "c": "Extinction", "d": "Structured reinforcement"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Kamal keeps his speed under 50 km/hour so that he never gets a ticket, which is negative reinforcement. In operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner (1938) from Edward Thorndike's (1898) law of effect, a consequence either strengthens or weakens the behaviour it follows. Reinforcement always strengthens behaviour; punishment always weakens it. 'Positive' means something is added and 'negative' means something is removed or prevented. So positive reinforcement adds a pleasant event (a bonus for good work), while negative reinforcement removes or prevents an unpleasant one (a painkiller ends a headache, so taking painkillers increases). Negative reinforcement comes in two forms. In escape learning the aversive event is already present and the behaviour ends it. In avoidance learning, as here, the behaviour stops the aversive event from ever happening. Kamal's careful driving is kept up because it keeps fines and court visits away. Avoidance is very resistant to extinction, because the person never tests whether the feared event would really come; Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947) used this in his two-factor theory to explain why phobias persist. Example: Meera leaves home ten minutes early every day so that her strict professor never marks her late; her punctuality is maintained by avoidance. Option b is right because the behaviour (driving within the limit) is strengthened and maintained by avoiding an aversive consequence, the speeding ticket. Exam trap: negative reinforcement is not punishment. Punishment weakens a behaviour, whereas here the behaviour is strengthened; a ticket given after speeding would be positive punishment of speeding.", "topic": "Negative reinforcement (avoidance)"}, {"q": "A person sees a snake, runs away and feels afraid. This sequence of stimulus, physiologic response followed by emotional response is based on the following theory of emotion:", "options": {"a": "Cannon-Bard", "b": "Schachter-Singer", "c": "James-Lange", "d": "Opponent process"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: stimulus, then bodily response, then felt emotion is the James-Lange theory of emotion. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) independently proposed that an emotion is our perception of bodily changes. Common sense says we see the snake, feel afraid, and then run. James reversed the order: we see the snake, the body reacts (we run, the heart pounds, we tremble), and our awareness of these changes is the fear. In his famous phrasing, we feel sorry because we cry and afraid because we tremble, not the other way round. The theory implies that each emotion has its own pattern of bodily response. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked it: visceral changes are too slow and too similar across different emotions, and animals whose visceral feedback was cut still showed emotional behaviour. Partial modern support comes from the facial feedback hypothesis, as in the study by Fritz Strack, Leonard Martin and Sabine Stepper (1988), where holding a pen in the teeth (forcing a smile) made cartoons seem funnier. Example: Arjun's car skids on a wet road; he brakes hard, his heart races, and only a moment later does he feel the fear. Option c is right because the stem gives the exact James-Lange sequence: stimulus, then physiological response, then emotion. Exam trap: students who remember the snake example from the same textbook page often pick Cannon-Bard, but Cannon-Bard says arousal and emotion occur at the same time and independently, so there is no 'followed by'; Schachter-Singer adds a cognitive label that the stem never mentions.", "topic": "James-Lange theory of emotion"}, {"q": "A person visits the walk-in-clinic and informs the therapist that he has a fear of heights. This is called:", "options": {"a": "Acrophobia", "b": "Agoraphobia", "c": "Claustrophobia", "d": "Scopophobia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: a fear of heights is called acrophobia. Phobia names join a Greek word for the feared thing to phobos, fear; akron means peak or summit. In DSM-5-TR a specific phobia is a marked fear of a particular object or situation that is out of proportion to the real danger, almost always triggers immediate anxiety, is actively avoided or endured with intense distress, and usually lasts six months or more. Acrophobia belongs to the natural environment subtype, along with fears of storms and water; the other subtypes are animal, blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, flying, enclosed places) and other. People with acrophobia may freeze on a balcony or ladder and avoid flyovers, hill roads or high floors. It is not the same as vertigo, a spinning sensation caused by inner-ear or brain disorders, though the two can coexist. Treatment is graded exposure, from Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation to modern in vivo and virtual reality exposure, which works especially well for heights. Example: Kabir refuses to look down from a fourth-floor window and takes a longer route to avoid a hill road, even though he knows his fear is excessive. Option a is right because acrophobia is the specific name for fear of heights. Exam trap: agoraphobia also starts with 'a' and is often confused with acrophobia, but it means fear of the marketplace, that is, of public places where escape may be hard; claustrophobia is the near-miss space fear, but for enclosed rather than high places.", "topic": "Phobia names"}, {"q": "A person comes to your office. He has a staggering gait, and slurring of speech. He appears like a person under influence of alcohol but on further inquiry you find out that he never drinks alcohol and is not on any other drug. He is likely to be having a disorder of the", "options": {"a": "Thalamus", "b": "Midbrain", "c": "Cerebellum", "d": "Occipital lobe"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: a staggering gait and slurred speech in someone who never drinks point to a disorder of the cerebellum. The cerebellum ('little brain') sits at the back of the hindbrain, behind the pons and medulla. It does not start movement; it coordinates it, comparing the intended movement with what the body is actually doing and correcting the error, so that movement is smooth, accurate and well timed. It also keeps balance and posture, working with the vestibular system, and supports motor learning, such as the practised timing of a cricket stroke. Damage produces ataxia: a broad-based, staggering, 'drunken' gait; dysarthria, slurred or scanning speech; intention tremor, which worsens as the hand nears its target; dysmetria, overshooting in the finger-nose test; dysdiadochokinesia, clumsy rapid alternating movements; and nystagmus. A lesion of one cerebellar hemisphere affects the same side of the body. Alcohol is the classic cause of temporary cerebellar dysfunction, which is why intoxication looks like this and why roadside sobriety tests (walking a straight line, touching the nose) are really cerebellar tests. Other causes include stroke, tumours, multiple sclerosis and inherited spinocerebellar ataxias. Example: Arjun, a lifelong teetotaller, is mistaken for drunk at a bus stop; a scan later shows a small cerebellar stroke. Option c is right because ataxic gait plus dysarthria is the hallmark of cerebellar disease, the same picture alcohol produces. Exam trap: the midbrain tempts students because it contains motor nuclei such as the substantia nigra, but nigral damage gives Parkinson's disease, with slowness, rigidity, tremor at rest and a shuffling rather than staggering gait.", "topic": "Cerebellar ataxia"}, {"q": "The following is NOT a distinctive feature of Obsessive-Compulsive disorder:", "options": {"a": "Impulsive behaviour", "b": "Ritualized behaviour", "c": "Irrational thoughts", "d": "Munchausen syndrome"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: Munchausen syndrome is not a feature of obsessive-compulsive disorder; it is a different disorder altogether. OCD, as defined in DSM-5-TR, has two core features. Obsessions are recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images (fear of contamination, of harming someone, of things not being 'just right') that cause marked anxiety. They are ego-dystonic, felt as alien to the self, and the person tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them. Compulsions are repetitive behaviours or mental acts (washing, checking, counting, ordering, praying) that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or by rigid rules. They aim to reduce distress or prevent a dreaded event, but are not realistically connected to it or are clearly excessive. Many patients recognise that their thoughts are irrational, which DSM-5-TR records as good or fair insight. Example: Meera checks the gas knob twenty times before leaving home, knowing it is off, because the thought 'the house will burn down' will not go away. Munchausen syndrome, named by Richard Asher (1951) after the tall tales of Baron Munchausen, is the severe form of factitious disorder imposed on self: the person deliberately fakes or induces illness, tells dramatic stories and seeks repeated hospital admissions in order to take on the sick role. Option d is right because Munchausen syndrome sits in the somatic symptom and related disorders chapter of DSM-5, not in OCD. Exam trap: option a (impulsive behaviour) also looks shaky, since OCD compulsions are driven by anxiety reduction rather than impulsivity; but compulsions are loosely described as irresistible urges, while d is the clearly correct 'NOT' because it names a separate disorder.", "topic": "Core features of OCD"}, {"q": "The disorder where brain damage results in impairment of a person's ability to recognize faces is:", "options": {"a": "Prosopagnosia", "b": "Auto agnosia", "c": "Anosognosia", "d": "Nominal aphasia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: an inability to recognise faces after brain damage is prosopagnosia, or face blindness. The German neurologist Joachim Bodamer (1947) coined the term from the Greek prosopon (face) and agnosia (not knowing). An agnosia is a failure to recognise things even though basic sensation, memory and language are intact; Heinrich Lissauer (1890) divided visual agnosia into apperceptive (no stable percept is formed) and associative (the percept is formed but not linked to meaning). In prosopagnosia vision is normal and the person can describe the eyes, nose and hair of a face, yet cannot tell whose face it is, even a spouse's or their own in a mirror. They still recognise people by voice, walk, clothes or a distinctive mole. The lesion usually involves the ventral occipitotemporal cortex on both sides or on the right, especially the fusiform gyrus, where Nancy Kanwisher (1997) identified the fusiform face area. Causes include stroke in the posterior cerebral arteries, head injury and encephalitis; a developmental form also exists without obvious brain damage. Oliver Sacks's book The Man Who Mistook His Wife for a Hat (1985) made visual agnosia widely known. Example: after a stroke Kabir's uncle walks straight past his own daughter at the station, but knows her the moment she speaks. Option a is right because prosopagnosia is the specific agnosia for faces. Exam trap: anosognosia sounds similar but means unawareness of one's own illness, classically denial of a paralysed left side after a right parietal stroke (Joseph Babinski, 1914); autotopagnosia is failure to locate one's own body parts.", "topic": "Prosopagnosia (face agnosia)"}, {"q": "According to Miller, the number of pieces of information that can be held briefly in our memory at any given time is:", "options": {"a": "Two", "b": "Three", "c": "Five", "d": "Seven"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: George Miller (1956) put the capacity of short-term memory at about seven items, plus or minus two. In his paper 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two', George Armitage Miller reviewed studies of immediate memory span and absolute judgement and found that people can hold about 5 to 9 units at once. The key word is units: Miller called them chunks, meaningful groups rather than single letters or digits. Chunking stretches capacity a great deal. Riya cannot hold twelve random letters, but I N D I A I I T I S R O becomes three chunks (INDIA, IIT, ISRO) and is easy. In the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), short-term memory holds information for only about 15 to 30 seconds without rehearsal; Lloyd Peterson and Margaret Peterson (1959) showed this rapid forgetting by blocking rehearsal with backward counting. Digit span subtests in the Wechsler scales measure this span directly. Later, Nelson Cowan (2001) argued that when rehearsal and chunking are prevented, the true limit is nearer four chunks, but seven remains the classic exam figure. Option d is right because seven is Miller's famous number for the items held briefly in short-term memory. Exam trap: option c (five) is the lower end of Miller's range (seven minus two) and is close to Cowan's modern estimate of about four, so students who remember either may pick it; the question asks for Miller's number, which is seven.", "topic": "Miller's magical number seven"}, {"q": "You are playing cards and somehow you begin to \"know\" which card will be dealt next accurately. You are displaying the extra-sensory perception of:", "options": {"a": "Telepathy", "b": "Clairvoyance", "c": "Psychokinesis", "d": "Pattern recognition"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: 'knowing' which card will come up is classed here as clairvoyance, extrasensory perception of hidden information. Extrasensory perception (ESP) is the claimed ability to gain information without any of the known senses. Joseph Banks Rhine (1934) popularised the term in his book Extra-Sensory Perception and ran the parapsychology laboratory at Duke University. He used Zener cards, designed by his colleague Karl Zener (early 1930s): a deck of 25 cards with five symbols (circle, cross, wavy lines, square, star), so chance is 5 hits in 25. Rhine named three forms of ESP. Telepathy is mind-to-mind transfer, such as guessing the card another person is looking at. Clairvoyance is perceiving a hidden object or event directly, such as naming a face-down card no one has seen. Precognition is knowing a future event. Psychokinesis, by contrast, is not perception but influencing matter by mind; ESP and psychokinesis together are called psi. Mainstream psychology remains sceptical, because results fail to replicate and early studies suffered from sensory leakage and poor randomisation. Example: Arjun, playing cards at a Diwali party, 'senses' that the next card is the queen of hearts and is right; he forgets the many times he was wrong. Option b is right because, among the choices, clairvoyance is the form of ESP that covers knowledge of a hidden card that no other mind holds. Exam trap: telepathy needs a sender who already knows the card, and nobody knows the next card in a shuffled deck. Key note: strictly, 'knowing' which card will be dealt NEXT is precognition (knowledge of a future event), a term J. B. Rhine used alongside clairvoyance (knowledge of a present hidden object or event) and telepathy (another mind). Precognition is not offered, so b, the extra-sensory perception of hidden information, stands.", "topic": "Forms of extrasensory perception"}, {"q": "In an EEG the following waves are slowest (have the least frequency)", "options": {"a": "Alpha", "b": "Beta", "c": "Delta", "d": "Theta"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: delta waves are the slowest EEG waves, at about 0.5 to 4 cycles per second (hertz). The electroencephalogram (EEG), first recorded in humans by Hans Berger (1929), picks up the summed electrical activity of millions of cortical neurons through electrodes on the scalp. When neurons fire independently the trace is fast and low in voltage (desynchronised); when they fire together it is slow and high in voltage (synchronised). From fastest to slowest the bands are: gamma, above about 30 Hz (focused processing); beta, about 13 to 30 Hz (alert, active thinking, anxiety); alpha, about 8 to 12 Hz (relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed, strongest over the occipital lobe, and the first rhythm Berger described); theta, about 4 to 7 Hz (drowsiness, stage N1 sleep, and normal in children); and delta, below 4 Hz (deep slow-wave sleep, stage N3, and normal in infants). A simple order to remember, slow to fast, is delta, theta, alpha, beta. In an awake adult, delta waves suggest brain dysfunction such as a lesion, encephalopathy or coma. In the sleep scoring manual of Allan Rechtschaffen and Anthony Kales (1968), stages 3 and 4 were defined by the amount of delta activity; the American Academy of Sleep Medicine (2007) merged them into N3. Example: when Meera falls into deep sleep in the first third of the night, her EEG shows large, slow delta waves and she is hardest to wake. Option c is right because delta has the lowest frequency of the four bands offered. Exam trap: theta is the near miss, slow but faster than delta; and students confuse amplitude with frequency, whereas delta has the highest amplitude but the lowest frequency.", "topic": "EEG frequency bands"}, {"q": "A student in a hostel is observed to get up at night and move about while still asleep. This is called:", "options": {"a": "Cataplexy", "b": "Narcolepsy", "c": "Sleep apnoea", "d": "Somnambulism"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: getting up and moving about while still asleep is somnambulism, or sleepwalking. In DSM-5-TR sleepwalking is a type of non-rapid eye movement (NREM) sleep arousal disorder, grouped with sleep terrors. These are incomplete awakenings, usually from deep slow-wave sleep (stage N3), so they occur in the first third of the night, when N3 is most plentiful. The sleepwalker sits up, walks about or performs complex acts (opening doors, eating, even leaving the building) with open, glassy eyes and a blank face. He is hard to wake, confused if woken, and remembers little or nothing in the morning. Sleepwalking cannot arise from REM sleep, because in REM the core muscles are paralysed (atonia); acting out dreams during REM is a different disorder, REM sleep behaviour disorder. Sleepwalking is common in children, usually fades by adolescence and often runs in families. In adults it is triggered by sleep deprivation, stress, fever, alcohol and some sedative drugs. Management is mainly safety (locking doors and windows, clearing obstacles), regular sleep and avoiding triggers; scheduled awakening shortly before the usual episode time can help children. Example: Kabir, a first-year hostel student short of sleep before exams, is found by his roommate standing in the corridor at 1 a.m. and remembers nothing the next day. Option d is right because somnambulism is the term for walking or moving about during sleep. Exam trap: narcolepsy and cataplexy are listed alongside it as sleep disorders, but narcolepsy means falling asleep suddenly in the daytime, and cataplexy is a sudden loss of muscle tone while fully awake, the opposite of moving about in sleep.", "topic": "Sleepwalking (NREM parasomnia)"}, {"q": "If your mother tells you \"You must eat your vegetables before you get Ice-cream\" then she is using the:", "options": {"a": "Parkinson's law", "b": "Peter principle", "c": "Dilbert principle", "d": "Premack principle"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: 'vegetables before ice-cream' is the Premack principle. David Premack (1959) proposed that a more probable (more preferred) activity can be used to reinforce a less probable (less preferred) one. On this view reinforcers are not things but activities, and their power is relative: whatever a person would rather do can reward whatever they would rather not do. Parents know it as 'Grandma's rule': first you work, then you play. In Premack's classic study, children who preferred eating sweets played more pinball when pinball earned them sweets, while children who preferred pinball ate more sweets when eating earned them a turn at the machine. Teachers, parents and behaviour therapists use the principle every day, in classroom management, token systems and activity schedules. Example: Riya's teacher lets the class go out for games only after they finish the arithmetic worksheet; games (high probability) reinforce worksheet completion (low probability). Option d is right because the mother makes the preferred activity, eating ice-cream, depend on first doing the less preferred one, eating vegetables. Exam trap: direction matters. A high-probability behaviour reinforces a low-probability one, never the reverse, so 'you may eat vegetables after the ice-cream' would not work. The other three options are well-known laws of management and office life; they sound like psychological principles because of their names, which is why examiners use them as distractors.", "topic": "Premack principle"}, {"q": "The principle of reinforcement that is applied in games of chance like gambling in a casino is:", "options": {"a": "Continuous reinforcement schedule", "b": "Fixed interval schedule", "c": "Variable ratio schedule", "d": "Fixed ratio schedule"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: casino gambling runs on a variable ratio schedule of reinforcement. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957), in Schedules of Reinforcement, mapped how the pattern of rewards shapes behaviour. In continuous reinforcement every response is rewarded; this is best for teaching a new behaviour but extinguishes quickly once rewards stop. In partial (intermittent) reinforcement only some responses are rewarded, and there are four basic schedules. A ratio schedule depends on the number of responses made; an interval schedule depends on time passed. Fixed ratio (FR) rewards after a set number of responses, as in piece-rate pay, and gives a high rate with a short pause after each reward. Variable ratio (VR) rewards after an unpredictable number of responses that averages out to some value. A slot machine may pay on average once every 20 pulls, but the next win could come on the second pull or the fiftieth. Because any response might be the winner, VR produces the highest, steadiest rate of responding and the greatest resistance to extinction, so the gambler keeps playing through long losing runs. Fixed interval (FI) produces a scalloped pattern, a pause and then a speed-up; variable interval (VI) gives a moderate, steady rate. Example: Arjun keeps buying a lottery ticket every week although he has won only twice in three years. Option c is right because games of chance pay off after an unpredictable number of plays, which is the definition of a variable ratio schedule. Exam trap: variable interval is the near miss because it is also unpredictable, but in gambling the payoff depends on the number of bets, not on the passage of time; checking messages for a reply is the textbook VI example.", "topic": "Variable ratio schedule"}, {"q": "The following is true regarding management of Somatic Symptom disorder (somatization disorder):", "options": {"a": "Medication is the mainstay of treatment", "b": "Biofeedback is a useful approach", "c": "Hypnosis has no role", "d": "Group therapy is not effective"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: biofeedback is a useful approach in managing somatic symptom disorder. In DSM-5-TR, somatic symptom disorder means one or more distressing bodily symptoms (pain, fatigue, stomach upset) together with excessive thoughts, feelings or behaviour about them: persistent worry about their seriousness, high anxiety about health, or too much time and energy devoted to them, usually for more than six months. It replaced the older somatisation disorder, descended from Briquet's syndrome (Paul Briquet, 1859), which required many medically unexplained symptoms. Management is mainly psychological and rests on a few principles. First, one primary doctor sees the patient at regular, scheduled short visits that do not depend on new complaints; this reduces doctor shopping and needless tests. Second, cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT), individual or in groups, has the strongest evidence: it challenges catastrophic readings of bodily sensations and reduces checking, reassurance seeking and avoidance. Third, adjuncts such as biofeedback, relaxation training, mindfulness and hypnosis teach control over muscle tension and arousal and show the patient that mind and body interact. Antidepressants help when depression or anxiety coexist, but medication is not the mainstay. Example: Meera, with years of headaches and normal scans, learns through EMG biofeedback to watch her forehead muscle tension fall on a screen as she relaxes, and her headaches ease. Option b is right because biofeedback is a recognised adjunct that lowers physiological arousal and shifts the patient's focus from disease to self-control. Exam trap: students pick a because the patient feels physically ill, but drugs mainly treat the coexisting depression or anxiety; options c and d are absolute statements ('no role', 'not effective') that the evidence contradicts.", "topic": "Managing somatic symptom disorder"}, {"q": "The following is a Primary reinforcer:", "options": {"a": "Money", "b": "Food", "c": "Status", "d": "Praise"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: food is a primary reinforcer. A reinforcer is any consequence that makes the behaviour it follows more likely to happen again. A primary (unconditioned) reinforcer works without any learning, because it satisfies a biological need: food, water, warmth, sleep, relief from pain and sexual contact. A secondary (conditioned) reinforcer is a stimulus that was once neutral but became rewarding through repeated pairing with primary reinforcers, so classical conditioning is at work inside operant learning. Praise and status are secondary reinforcers. Money is a special case, a generalised reinforcer, because it is linked with many different primary reinforcers (food, shelter, comfort) and so stays powerful whatever need is active. B. F. Skinner (1953) set out these ideas in Science and Human Behavior, and Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) described the token economy on a psychiatric ward, where tokens, a secondary reinforcer, could be exchanged for snacks and privileges. Primary reinforcers lose force when the need is met (satiation), which is why animal experiments use hungry rats. Example: baby Arjun stops crying the moment he is fed; nobody taught him that milk is rewarding, whereas over the years he will learn that a gold star or his teacher's 'well done' is worth working for. Option b is right because food satisfies a biological drive and reinforces behaviour without any prior learning. Exam trap: money is the commonest wrong pick because it feels so basic to adult life, but its value is learned; a toddler will happily swap a coin for a sweet.", "topic": "Primary versus secondary reinforcers"}, {"q": "A conditioned reinforcer is:", "options": {"a": "Money", "b": "Food", "c": "Water", "d": "Sexual pleasure"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: money is a conditioned (secondary) reinforcer, a stimulus that strengthens behaviour only because it has been linked with other reinforcers. A reinforcer is any consequence that makes the behaviour before it more likely. Reinforcers come in two kinds. Primary (unconditioned) reinforcers satisfy biological needs and work without any learning: food, water, warmth, sexual pleasure, relief from pain. Conditioned or secondary reinforcers start out neutral and gain their power by being paired with primary ones, through the same associative process as classical conditioning. Money is the textbook case. A ten rupee note means nothing to an infant, but as a child learns that it buys sweets, toys and food, the note itself becomes rewarding. Because money is linked with so many different primary reinforcers, B. F. Skinner (1953), in Science and Human Behavior, called it a generalised reinforcer, one that works whatever need is active at the moment. John Wolfe (1936) showed the same process in chimpanzees, who learned to work for poker chips that they could later exchange for food; the idea grew into the token economy of Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968). Example: Arjun's son works for stars on a chart while they can be traded for treats, and stops once they buy nothing; a conditioned reinforcer fades if it is never backed by a primary one. Option a is right because money is reinforcing only through learned association with things that meet biological needs. Exam trap: sexual pleasure seems social and learned in humans, so students pick it, but it is a primary reinforcer like food and water. Also keep a conditioned reinforcer (which follows a response) apart from a conditioned stimulus (which comes before one).", "topic": "Conditioned (secondary) reinforcers"}, {"q": "In clinical psychology, the importance of external stimuli on our responses is the underlying principle of:", "options": {"a": "Phenomenological theory", "b": "Psychodynamic theory", "c": "Behavioural theory", "d": "Gestalt theory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: behavioural theory holds that behaviour, normal or abnormal, is shaped by external stimuli in the environment and by the consequences that follow it. John B. Watson (1913), in 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It', argued that psychology should study observable behaviour, not the mind. Two learning processes explain how the environment controls us. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927), a neutral stimulus paired with a meaningful one comes to trigger a response by itself. In operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner (1938), a behaviour becomes more or less frequent depending on whether it is reinforced or punished. Clinical psychology turned these ideas into behaviour therapy. Joseph Wolpe (1958) treated phobias by systematic desensitisation, and behaviour therapists still begin with a functional analysis of antecedents, behaviour and consequences (the ABC model): what happens just before the problem, and what follows it. The problem is treated as learned behaviour that can be unlearned, not as a sign of hidden conflict. Example: four-year-old Kabir throws tantrums at the shop counter. A behavioural clinician notices that his parents buy him chocolate to stop the screaming, so the tantrum is being reinforced; the plan is to stop the payoff and praise calm behaviour instead. Option c is right because only the behavioural model places the causes of behaviour in external stimuli and their consequences. Exam trap: Gestalt theory also studies how we respond to stimuli, but it is about how the mind organises them into wholes, an inner process; the phenomenological and psychodynamic models both look inward too, to subjective experience and to unconscious conflict respectively.", "topic": "Behavioural model of behaviour"}, {"q": "The highest level attained based on Maslow's hierarchy is of:", "options": {"a": "Security", "b": "Self-actualization", "c": "Self-esteem", "d": "Prestige"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: self-actualisation is the highest level of Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs. Abraham Maslow (1943), in 'A Theory of Human Motivation', and later in Motivation and Personality (1954), arranged human needs as a pyramid of five levels. From bottom to top they are physiological needs (food, water, sleep), safety needs (security, order, freedom from danger), belongingness and love, esteem (self-respect plus recognition, status and prestige from others), and self-actualisation. Lower needs are prepotent: while they are unmet they dominate behaviour, and they must be largely satisfied before higher needs take charge. The first four are deficiency needs, which stop motivating once they are met. Self-actualisation is a growth or being need: the drive to become everything one is capable of becoming, which grows stronger as it is satisfied. Maslow studied people he saw as self-actualisers, such as Abraham Lincoln and Albert Einstein, and described them as accepting, spontaneous, problem-centred and prone to peak experiences. Example: Meera has a secure job, a loving family and respect at work, yet she feels restless until she starts painting seriously; that pull is self-actualisation. Option b is right because self-actualisation sits at the top of the classic five-level pyramid. Late in life Maslow wrote of self-transcendence beyond self-actualisation, but the standard five-level model that exams test ends with self-actualisation. Exam trap: security, self-esteem and prestige all belong to lower levels; prestige in particular sounds lofty, but Maslow placed it inside the esteem needs, the fourth level.", "topic": "Maslow's hierarchy of needs"}, {"q": "The term Clinical Psychology was first proposed by:", "options": {"a": "Witmer", "b": "Binet", "c": "Cattell", "d": "Weber"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: Lightner Witmer first proposed the term clinical psychology. Lightner Witmer (1867 to 1956) studied with James McKeen Cattell at the University of Pennsylvania, took his doctorate under Wilhelm Wundt (founder of the Leipzig laboratory, 1879) in 1892, and then returned to Pennsylvania. In March 1896 he opened the first psychological clinic there, after a schoolteacher brought him a boy who could not learn to spell. Later that year he described his 'clinical method' to the American Psychological Association, and in 1907 he founded the journal The Psychological Clinic, whose first issue carried his article 'Clinical Psychology', the first printed use of the name. His clinic worked mainly with children who had learning and behaviour problems, combining assessment with remedial teaching, so early clinical psychology was closer to what we now call school psychology and special education than to adult psychotherapy. For founding both the name and the first clinic, Witmer is called the father of clinical psychology. Option a is right because Witmer coined the term and set up the first clinic in which it was practised. Exam trap: Alfred Binet and James McKeen Cattell are famous for mental testing, which clinical psychologists later relied on heavily, so they look like founders of the field; but neither of them named it. Ernst Heinrich Weber is a psychophysicist with no link to clinics. Remember the pair: 1896, the first clinic; 1907, the name in print.", "topic": "Founder of clinical psychology"}, {"q": "A student tells the counsellor \"I do not hate the teacher, he hates me\" is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Projection", "b": "Assimilation", "c": "Regression", "d": "Denial"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: saying 'I do not hate the teacher, he hates me' is projection, attributing one's own unacceptable feeling to someone else. A defence mechanism is an unconscious strategy that protects the ego from anxiety by distorting reality. Sigmund Freud (1896) first described projection in paranoia, and Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, set it among the ego's standard defences. In projection, an impulse that would make the person feel guilty or ashamed, such as hostility, envy or sexual desire, is pushed out of awareness and then 'seen' in another person. The person gains twice: he no longer has to own the feeling, and his own anger now feels justified as self-defence. In his analysis of the Schreber case (1911), Freud traced persecutory delusions to a chain whose last step is exactly this: 'I hate him' is turned into 'he hates me'. That is the student's sentence. George Vaillant (1977) classed projection as an immature defence, common in adolescence and in paranoid and prejudiced thinking. Example: Arjun, who quietly resents his elder brother's success, keeps telling friends that his brother is jealous of him. Option a is right because the student disowns his own hatred and places it in the teacher. Exam trap: the stem opens with a denial ('I do not hate'), which pulls students towards option d; but pure denial would stop there. The defining move is the second half, relocating the feeling onto the teacher, and that relocation is projection.", "topic": "Defence mechanism: projection"}, {"q": "On the basis of Piaget's concept, children develop knowledge by:", "options": {"a": "Constructing reality out of their experiences", "b": "Participating in traditional learning environments", "c": "Responding to physiological changes", "d": "Modelling the cultural influences around them"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: in Jean Piaget's theory, children develop knowledge by constructing reality out of their own experiences with the world. Jean Piaget (1936), in The Origins of Intelligence in Children, described the child as a 'little scientist' who learns by acting on objects and noticing what happens, not by passively absorbing what adults say. This view is called constructivism. Knowledge is organised into schemes, mental patterns of action or thought. When a new experience fits an existing scheme, the child assimilates it; when it does not fit, the scheme is changed or a new one is made, which is accommodation. The tension between the two is resolved by equilibration, a drive to regain a balanced understanding, and each new balance is more advanced than the last. Over years this produces the four stages: sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational and formal operational. Example: two-year-old Riya calls every four-legged animal 'dog' (assimilation). At a farm she meets a cow that moos and is far too big, so she builds a new scheme for 'cow' (accommodation). No one taught her the rule; she built it from her own encounters. Option a is right because Piaget saw knowledge as actively built by the child, not received, inherited or copied. Exam trap: Lev Vygotsky (1978) is also called a constructivist, but his social constructivism stresses learning through culture, language and more skilled partners. 'Modelling the cultural influences around them' is therefore closer to Vygotsky and Albert Bandura than to Piaget's individual, self-directed construction.", "topic": "Piaget's constructivism"}, {"q": "The traditional scientific approach to understand \"everyday life experiences of people\" is:", "options": {"a": "Anthropologic", "b": "Phenomenological", "c": "Ethnographic", "d": "Ecologic"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the phenomenological approach is the traditional method for understanding the everyday lived experience of people. Phenomenology began with the philosopher Edmund Husserl (1913), who wanted to describe experience exactly as it appears to the person living it, before any theory is imposed on it. His tool was bracketing (epoche): the researcher sets aside his own assumptions so that the participant's experience can show itself. Husserl later called the taken-for-granted world of daily life the lifeworld (Lebenswelt), and Alfred Schutz (1932) carried the idea into the study of everyday social life. Martin Heidegger (1927) added that all understanding is interpretation, which led to hermeneutic phenomenology. In psychology the approach became a qualitative method: the researcher interviews a small group of people who have lived through the same phenomenon, reads their accounts closely and distils the essential structure of that experience. Interpretative phenomenological analysis (IPA), introduced by Jonathan Smith (1996), is a widely used modern form. Example: Meera interviews eight mothers about what it is like to care for a child with autism and describes the shared core of their experience: exhaustion, guilt and fierce protectiveness. Option b is right because phenomenology alone makes the person's own lived, everyday experience the object of study. Exam trap: ethnography also studies daily life, but its unit is a culture or group and its method is long participant observation of shared practices; phenomenology studies how individuals experience a phenomenon. 'Ecologic' in epidemiology means studying whole populations, not anyone's experience.", "topic": "Phenomenological research approach"}, {"q": "While swimming in the ocean, Richie got frightened by a dark shadow even before he could identify it. The neurologic basis of this frightening experience is explained by messages being sent from:", "options": {"a": "Thalamus to amygdala", "b": "Parasympathetic nervous system to cerebral cortex", "c": "Frontal lobe to pituitary", "d": "Occipital lobe to parietal lobe"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: Richie's instant fright comes from the fast route that sends sensory messages from the thalamus straight to the amygdala. The thalamus is the brain's relay station: almost all sensory information (smell is the exception) passes through it before reaching the cortex. The amygdala, a small almond-shaped structure in the temporal lobe, detects threat and triggers fear. Joseph LeDoux (1996), in The Emotional Brain, showed from fear conditioning work in rats that sensory signals leave the thalamus by two roads. The low road runs directly from the sensory thalamus to the amygdala. It is fast but crude: it carries only a rough sketch such as 'large dark shape', yet that is enough to set off fear. The high road runs from the thalamus to the sensory cortex and only then to the amygdala. It is slower but detailed, and it tells you whether the shape is a shark, a rock or a cloud's shadow. The amygdala then drives the hypothalamus (racing heart, sympathetic arousal) and the brainstem (freezing or startle). The low road is adaptive: jumping back from what may be a shark costs little if it proves to be a rock, while waiting for certainty could cost your life. This is why fear can arrive before recognition, as it did for Richie. Option a is right because only the thalamus to amygdala pathway explains fear arising before the object is identified. Exam trap: students reach for cortical routes because seeing the shadow seems to need the visual lobes; but conscious identification is the high road, and Richie was frightened before it had finished.", "topic": "LeDoux's low road of fear"}, {"q": "The WRONG statement regarding the EEG is that:", "options": {"a": "Beta waves occur during alert awake state", "b": "Alpha waves occur when you switch off the light to sleep", "c": "Delta waves occur during the awake state", "d": "Theta activity is seen in early sleep"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the wrong statement is that delta waves occur during the awake state, because delta is the mark of deep sleep. The electroencephalogram (EEG) records the summed electrical activity of cortical neurons through electrodes on the scalp; Hans Berger (1929) made the first human recordings and named alpha and beta waves. Waves are described by frequency, in cycles per second (hertz, Hz). Beta waves, 13 to 30 Hz and low in amplitude, appear when a person is alert, eyes open, thinking or anxious. Alpha waves, 8 to 12 Hz, appear in relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed, strongest over the back of the head; they are what you see when someone switches off the light and lies down to sleep. Theta waves, 4 to 7 Hz, mark stage N1, the drowsy transition into early sleep. Stage N2 adds sleep spindles and K-complexes. Delta waves, 0.5 to 4 Hz and high in amplitude, define N3, slow-wave sleep, the deepest stage, concentrated early in the night. In a healthy awake adult, delta activity is abnormal and points to brain dysfunction such as a lesion, encephalopathy or coma. Example: as Kabir lies in bed with the lights off his EEG shows alpha; as he dozes it slows to theta; an hour later, in deep sleep, delta dominates. Option c is right because delta belongs to deep sleep, not to the waking state. Exam trap: REM sleep is the stage whose EEG looks awake (fast and low voltage, hence 'paradoxical sleep'); do not let that reversal tempt you into thinking slow delta can also appear while awake.", "topic": "EEG waves and sleep stages"}, {"q": "Research analysis was done on the various meanings of newspaper editorial cartoons which appeared during a period of 4 years. This is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Hermeneutics", "b": "Discourse analysis", "c": "Ethnography", "d": "Semiotics"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: analysing the meanings of newspaper editorial cartoons is semiotics, the study of signs and how they create meaning. A sign is anything that stands for something else: a word, a picture, a colour, a gesture. Ferdinand de Saussure (1916), in his Course in General Linguistics, split the sign into the signifier (the form, such as a drawing of a crow) and the signified (the concept it calls up). Charles Sanders Peirce, writing from the 1860s, sorted signs into icons (which resemble their object), indexes (which are caused by it, as smoke indicates fire) and symbols (linked by convention, as a dove stands for peace). Roland Barthes (1957), in Mythologies, applied semiotics to advertisements, photographs and popular culture, showing that images carry a literal meaning (denotation) and a deeper cultural or ideological one (connotation). Cartoons are built almost entirely from such signs: exaggerated features, symbolic animals, labels and captions. A semiotic analysis asks what each sign means and how together they build the cartoon's message. Example: Arjun studies four years of R. K. Laxman's 'You Said It' cartoons in The Times of India and asks what the silent Common Man, in his checked coat, signifies about ordinary citizens and their rulers. Option d is right because the research is about the meanings carried by visual signs and symbols. Exam trap: hermeneutics and discourse analysis also deal with meaning, so they tempt; but hermeneutics is the general interpretation of texts as wholes, and discourse analysis studies how language in use constructs social reality. Meanings of signs and images point to semiotics.", "topic": "Semiotics in qualitative research"}, {"q": "The following is TRUE regarding transcranial magnetic stimulation:", "options": {"a": "It is useful for psychotic disorders and not for depression", "b": "It requires general anaesthesia", "c": "It is given over the occipital cortex", "d": "It has no cognitive side effects"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) is free of the memory and thinking problems that electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) can cause. TMS passes a brief, strong magnetic pulse from a coil held against the scalp. By electromagnetic induction the pulse creates a small electric current in the cortex just beneath the coil, which can excite or quieten the neurons there. Anthony Barker (1985) and his team in Sheffield built the first modern TMS device. Given in repeated trains (rTMS), it changes activity in mood circuits, and the US Food and Drug Administration cleared rTMS for depression in 2008. For depression the coil is placed over the left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex, an area that is underactive in many depressed patients. The patient is awake and needs no anaesthesia or muscle relaxant; he sits in a chair for each session, usually on weekdays for four to six weeks, and can go straight back to work. The main side effects are scalp discomfort and headache; a seizure is rare. Unlike ECT, TMS does not set out to produce a seizure and does not cause memory loss. Example: Kabir's depression has not improved with two antidepressants; he has rTMS sessions before work and drives himself home afterwards. Option d is right because, among these statements, the absence of cognitive side effects is the one true feature, and it is what sets TMS apart from ECT. Exam trap: options b and c borrow from ECT and from the wrong brain area; anaesthesia is needed for ECT, and the target for depression is prefrontal, not occipital. Key note: 'no cognitive side effects' is the intended contrast with ECT; strictly, TMS has no significant cognitive side effects, so d stands.", "topic": "Transcranial magnetic stimulation"}, {"q": "When a car driver fails to notice a pedestrian who has just stepped onto the road, it is an example of", "options": {"a": "Anton's syndrome", "b": "Amaurosis fugax", "c": "Inattentional blindness", "d": "Divided attention"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: a driver who fails to see a pedestrian in plain view is showing inattentional blindness. Inattentional blindness is the failure to notice an unexpected object that is fully visible, because attention is focused elsewhere. Arien Mack and Irvin Rock (1998) named it after finding that many people busy judging the arms of a cross on a screen did not see an extra shape that appeared right beside it. The best-known demonstration is by Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris (1999): when viewers counted basketball passes in a short film, about half failed to notice a person in a gorilla suit who walked through the scene and thumped her chest. The eyes take in the image, but without attention it never reaches awareness. On the road this is the 'looked but failed to see' accident: the driver is watching the signal or the car ahead, or talking on the phone, and a pedestrian is not expected, so he goes unseen. David Strayer and William Johnston (2001) showed in a simulated driving task that phone conversations make drivers miss more signals. Option c is right because the pedestrian was visible yet not perceived for lack of attention, which is the definition of inattentional blindness. Exam trap: divided attention may be the cause (attention split between the road and a call), so it tempts; but the question asks for the name of the failure to notice, which is inattentional blindness. Also keep it apart from change blindness, which is missing a change between two views of the same scene.", "topic": "Inattentional blindness"}, {"q": "A housewife is cooking food on the stove and also participating in a videoconference of her department. This is possible due to:", "options": {"a": "Alternating attention", "b": "Divided attention", "c": "Selective attention", "d": "Sustained attention"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: cooking while taking part in a videoconference is divided attention, attending to two tasks at the same time. Attention is limited, so the question is how we share it. Daniel Kahneman (1973), in Attention and Effort, described attention as a single pool of mental effort that can be split between tasks as long as their total demand stays within capacity. Christopher Wickens (1984) refined this in his multiple resource theory: tasks interfere less when they use different senses, codes and responses. Cooking uses the hands and eyes; the meeting uses the ears and voice, so the two compete only a little. Practice helps too. Walter Schneider and Richard Shiffrin (1977) showed that well-practised tasks become automatic and need almost no attention, which is why an experienced cook can stir the dal while following a discussion. Two hard, unfamiliar tasks using the same channel, such as writing a report while listening to a lecture, are very hard to divide. Clinicians use the model of McKay Sohlberg and Catherine Mateer (1987), which ranks attention from focused through sustained, selective and alternating to divided, the most demanding level. Option b is right because the woman keeps both tasks going at once rather than shutting one out or taking them in turns. Exam trap: alternating attention means switching back and forth, giving each task full attention in turn (stop cooking, speak, then return to the stove). Research suggests that much everyday multitasking involves rapid switching, but the textbook label for doing two things at once is divided attention.", "topic": "Divided attention"}, {"q": "The following is true regarding suicide:", "options": {"a": "It is more prevalent in general population than in schizophrenics", "b": "Women are more likely to be successful in a suicide attempt", "c": "The lifting of depression may indicate decision to commit suicide", "d": "There are no scales to assess suicide risk"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: when a severe depression begins to lift, the apparent improvement can mean the person has decided to end his life, so suicide risk may rise rather than fall. Severe depression brings psychomotor slowing, exhaustion and indecision; a person may wish to die yet lack the energy to plan or act. In the early weeks of recovery, for example soon after starting an antidepressant or on discharge from hospital, energy and drive often return before hopelessness and low mood have eased. That gap gives the person the means to act on thoughts that are still there. A second pattern is the sudden calm that follows a decision: the inner struggle stops, the person looks peaceful, settles his affairs and gives away belongings, and relatives think he is better. Clinicians therefore increase monitoring in early recovery and after discharge. Other well-established facts sit alongside this one: the strongest single predictor of suicide is a previous attempt, and hopelessness predicts suicide better than the severity of depression. Example: Arjun, depressed for months, suddenly seems relaxed and cheerful, returns borrowed books to friends and writes a long letter to his sister; his psychologist reads this as a warning sign and reassesses his risk at once. Option c is right because the lifting of depression is a recognised period of raised suicide risk. Exam trap: option b reverses the gender paradox. Women attempt suicide more often, but men die by suicide more often because they use more lethal methods such as hanging and firearms; 'successful' in the option means completed suicide, which is commoner in men.", "topic": "Suicide risk facts"}, {"q": "The repetition or prolongation of a visual phenomenon beyond the time limit of the physiological image is:", "options": {"a": "Akinetopsia", "b": "Paraprosopia", "c": "Palinopsia", "d": "Teleopsia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: palinopsia is the persistence or recurrence of a visual image after the object that produced it has gone, beyond the time a normal afterimage lasts. The word comes from the Greek palin (again) and opsis (seeing). Everyone sees brief physiological afterimages: stare at a bright bulb and a dark or complementary coloured blob lingers for a few seconds, then fades. Palinopsia is different in kind. In the hallucinatory form, a clear, formed copy of a real object persists or reappears, sometimes minutes or hours later, often in another part of the visual field. In the illusory form, moving objects leave trails or images smear and linger. Palinopsia is a disorder of visual perception linked with lesions of the occipital or parieto-occipital cortex, migraine, seizures, some medicines, and hallucinogen persisting perception disorder (HPPD) after drugs such as LSD. Example: after a small stroke at the back of her brain, Meera keeps seeing the nurse's face on the wall a minute after the nurse has left the room. Option c is right because the stem describes a visual image repeated or prolonged beyond the normal physiological image, which is the definition of palinopsia. Exam trap: all four options are rare, similar-sounding '-opsia' or '-prosopia' terms. Fix each by its root meaning: akinetopsia is motion blindness, teleopsia makes objects look far away, paraprosopia distorts faces, and only palinopsia means 'seeing again'.", "topic": "Palinopsia and visual distortions"}, {"q": "Sometimes we are able to read the article and understand its content despite the fact that there are many misprints. This is called:", "options": {"a": "Learned illusion", "b": "Completion illusion", "c": "Similarity perception", "d": "Pareidolia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: reading an article correctly despite many misprints is a completion illusion, the tendency to perceive an incomplete or faulty pattern as complete and correct. Perception is not a passive copy of the page. The brain combines bottom-up input (the letters actually printed) with top-down knowledge (vocabulary, grammar, the topic and what we expect to read). When a word is misspelt or a letter is missing, expectation fills the gap and we 'see' the right word, usually without noticing the error at all. The principle behind it is closure, one of the Gestalt laws of organisation set out by Max Wertheimer (1923): we complete broken or partial figures into whole, familiar forms. The same process explains why proofreading our own writing is so hard, and why most people read the classic sign 'PARIS IN THE THE SPRING' without noticing the doubled 'THE'. It is an illusion in the strict sense, because a real stimulus is misperceived: the page says one thing and we perceive another. Example: Kabir reads a college notice saying 'Studnets must sumbit fomrs by Fridya' and understands it at once, noticing the four spelling errors only when a friend points them out. Option b is right because the reader completes and corrects the faulty text from expectation, which is what a completion illusion means. Exam trap: pareidolia also involves the mind adding meaning, but it is seeing a meaningful pattern, such as a face, in a random or vague stimulus like clouds or a stain on a wall; a misprinted article is not random, it is a nearly complete pattern that gets filled in.", "topic": "Completion illusion"}, {"q": "A histrionic (hysterical) personality could have the following clinical features:", "options": {"a": "Superstitions", "b": "Suicidal tendency", "c": "Seductive", "d": "Shy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: seductive behaviour is a core feature of histrionic personality disorder. Histrionic personality disorder is a Cluster B ('dramatic, emotional, erratic') pattern of excessive emotionality and attention seeking that begins by early adulthood and shows across many settings. The older label, hysterical personality, came from the psychoanalytic idea of hysteria; DSM-III (1980) made 'histrionic', meaning theatrical, the official name. DSM-5-TR (2022) lists eight criteria, of which five are needed: discomfort when not the centre of attention; interaction with others that is often characterised by inappropriate sexually seductive or provocative behaviour; rapidly shifting and shallow expression of emotions; consistent use of physical appearance to draw attention; speech that is impressionistic and lacking in detail; self-dramatisation, theatricality and exaggerated emotion; suggestibility; and seeing relationships as more intimate than they really are. The seductiveness is usually less about sexual desire than about winning attention and approval. Example: Riya, 26, flirts with every new colleague, dresses provocatively for routine meetings, sulks when someone else is praised, and calls a film 'absolutely life-changing' but cannot say a single thing about its story. Option c is right because seductive or provocative behaviour is one of the formal diagnostic criteria for histrionic personality. Exam trap: dramatic suicidal gestures sound attention-seeking and therefore 'histrionic', but recurrent suicidal behaviour, gestures, threats or self-harm is a criterion of borderline personality disorder, not of histrionic personality disorder.", "topic": "Histrionic personality disorder"}, {"q": "Ramu was initially conditioned to be fearful of brown rats. Over time he began to fear all brown animals like dogs. This is indicative of:", "options": {"a": "Higher order conditioning", "b": "Stimulus dispersion", "c": "Stimulus generalization", "d": "Stimulus discrimination"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Ramu's fear spreading from brown rats to all brown animals is stimulus generalisation. In classical conditioning, once a neutral stimulus has become a conditioned stimulus (CS), other stimuli that resemble it also come to trigger the conditioned response (CR). Ivan Pavlov (1927) found that a dog conditioned to salivate to one tone also salivated to tones of nearby pitch, and that the response grew weaker as the new tone became less similar. This falling curve is the generalisation gradient. The classic human case is Little Albert. John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) paired a white rat with a sudden loud noise until the infant feared the rat; his fear then generalised to a rabbit, a dog, a fur coat and a Santa Claus mask with a white cotton beard, all things that shared the rat's furry, light-coloured look. Ramu's case works the same way: the shared feature, brown colour, carries the fear to dogs he was never conditioned with. Generalisation is adaptive, because a dangerous thing rarely looks exactly the same twice, but it also explains why phobias and traumatic fears can spread far beyond their original trigger. Option c is right because a conditioned fear has spread to new stimuli similar to the original CS. Exam trap: stimulus discrimination is the opposite process (responding only to the original stimulus), and 'stimulus dispersion' is not a conditioning term at all; it simply borrows the idea of spreading to sound plausible.", "topic": "Stimulus generalisation"}, {"q": "In an interview for this post, you are asked \"Who was the first President of India?\" and you promptly answer correctly \"Dr. Rajendra Prasad\". This is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Episodic memory", "b": "Working memory", "c": "Semantic memory", "d": "Implicit memory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: recalling that Dr Rajendra Prasad was the first President of India is semantic memory, memory for general facts and knowledge. Endel Tulving (1972) divided long-term declarative memory into two systems. Episodic memory holds personal events tied to a particular time and place, the 'what, where and when' of your own life, such as your first day at college. Semantic memory holds facts, word meanings and concepts that make up your general knowledge of the world, stored without any record of when or where you learned them. You know that Rajendra Prasad held office from 1950 to 1962, that Delhi is the capital and that a triangle has three sides, but you probably cannot recall the day you learned each fact. Both systems are explicit (declarative) memory, which Larry Squire (1987) contrasted with implicit (non-declarative) memory such as skills and priming. Brain evidence supports the split: Tulving's patient K. C., injured in a motorcycle accident in 1981, could not recall a single personal event from his past, yet kept much of his general knowledge. Option c is right because the answer is a general fact retrieved from stored knowledge, not a relived personal experience. Exam trap: the interview itself is an episode in your life, which pulls students towards episodic memory; but the question asks what you retrieved, and that is a fact. Describing the day you visited Rashtrapati Bhavan on a school trip would be episodic memory.", "topic": "Semantic versus episodic memory"}, {"q": "Brainstorming is a form of:", "options": {"a": "Convergent thinking", "b": "Non-directed thinking", "c": "Divergent thinking", "d": "Heuristic approach"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: brainstorming is a form of divergent thinking, generating many different possible ideas or solutions to one problem. Joy Paul Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, distinguished two kinds of productive thinking. Convergent thinking narrows down to the single correct answer, as in an arithmetic sum or a multiple-choice question. Divergent thinking spreads outward to many possible answers, as in 'List all the uses of a brick'. Guilford scored divergent output for fluency (how many ideas), flexibility (how many categories), originality (how rare) and elaboration (how detailed), and he linked it closely with creativity. Brainstorming was devised by the advertising executive Alex Osborn (1953), in Applied Imagination, as a group method for producing creative ideas. Its rules are to defer judgement, aim for quantity, welcome wild ideas and build on other people's ideas, exactly the conditions that free divergent thinking from early criticism. Ideas are judged only afterwards, which is the convergent step. Later research by Michael Diehl and Wolfgang Stroebe (1987) found that people who brainstorm alone and then pool their ideas often produce more than interacting groups, partly because group members block each other while waiting to speak. Example: in fifteen minutes Meera's team lists forty ideas for a college mental health week, from street plays to a poster contest, before choosing any. Option c is right because brainstorming aims at many varied ideas, the defining feature of divergent thinking. Exam trap: brainstorming feels free-flowing, so 'non-directed thinking' tempts; but brainstorming is aimed at a set problem, while non-directed thinking is aimless, like daydreaming.", "topic": "Brainstorming and divergent thinking"}, {"q": "You teach students Hydroelectricity. You ask a question \"Due to monsoons, there is a dangerous rise in level of water in Bhakra Dam. Formulate a plan to release the water gradually and avoid flooding in the plains\". This type of question is most likely to be at Bloom's level of:", "options": {"a": "Analysis", "b": "Knowledge", "c": "Synthesis", "d": "Evaluation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: asking students to formulate a plan for releasing the water is a synthesis question in Bloom's taxonomy. Benjamin Bloom (1956) and his colleagues published the Taxonomy of Educational Objectives, Handbook I: Cognitive Domain, which arranges learning goals in six levels from simple to complex: knowledge (recall facts), comprehension (explain in one's own words), application (use a rule in a new case), analysis (break material into parts and see how they relate), synthesis (put parts together to form a new whole) and evaluation (judge value against criteria). Each level builds on those below it. The handbook lists 'production of a plan, or proposed set of operations' as one of the forms of synthesis, so a question asking students to design a release schedule for Bhakra Dam fits it almost word for word. The students must combine what they know about reservoir level, monsoon inflow, spillway gates and the plains downstream on the Sutlej into a new plan of their own; there is no single answer to recall. In the revised taxonomy of Lorin Anderson and David Krathwohl (2001), the levels became verbs (remember, understand, apply, analyse, evaluate, create), and synthesis was renamed 'create' and moved to the top, above evaluation. Option c is right because formulating a plan means building something new from parts, which is synthesis. Exam trap: in the original 1956 order the top level is evaluation, so students who equate 'hardest' with 'highest' pick d; but judging a plan is evaluation, while producing one is synthesis.", "topic": "Bloom's taxonomy: synthesis"}, {"q": "Thorndike suggested that the ability to deal with skilled trades and scientific appliances is a form of", "options": {"a": "Abstract intelligence", "b": "Social intelligence", "c": "Concrete intelligence", "d": "Fluid intelligence"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Edward Thorndike called the ability to deal with skilled trades, tools and scientific appliances concrete (mechanical) intelligence. Edward Thorndike (1920), in an article titled 'Intelligence and its Uses', proposed that intelligence is not one general ability but at least three broad kinds. Abstract intelligence is the ability to handle ideas and symbols: words, numbers, formulas and concepts, the kind measured by most school and IQ tests. Concrete or mechanical intelligence is the ability to understand and manage things: tools, machines, instruments and practical materials, as in skilled trades and laboratory work. Social intelligence is the ability to understand and manage people and to act wisely in human relations; it later fed into the idea of emotional intelligence. Thorndike's wider view is called the multifactor or 'anarchic' theory: intelligence is the sum of many specific connections in the nervous system, with no single general factor like Charles Spearman's (1904) g. His CAVD test (1927) sampled completion, arithmetic, vocabulary and directions. Example: Kabir left school early and scores modestly on verbal tests, yet he can strip and rebuild a motorcycle engine and set up a laboratory centrifuge without a manual; he has high concrete intelligence. Option c is right because skilled trades and scientific appliances are dealings with things, which is Thorndike's concrete intelligence. Exam trap: 'concrete' here has nothing to do with Jean Piaget's concrete operational stage, and 'mechanical' and 'concrete' are two names for the same category; fluid intelligence belongs to a different theorist and a different scheme.", "topic": "Thorndike's three intelligences"}, {"q": "On the basis of Gardner's theory of multiple intelligence, the potential career choice for a person with Spatial intelligence should be a:", "options": {"a": "Airline Pilot", "b": "Lawyer", "c": "Teacher", "d": "Psychologist"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in Howard Gardner's theory, spatial intelligence suits careers such as airline pilot. Howard Gardner (1983), in Frames of Mind, argued that intelligence is not one general ability but several relatively independent intelligences. He identified them from evidence such as brain damage that destroys one ability and spares others, prodigies and savants, and separate developmental histories. The original seven were linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal and intrapersonal; naturalist was added in 1999. Spatial intelligence is the ability to perceive the visual and spatial world accurately, form mental images, rotate and transform them, and find one's way through space. Damage to the right parietal region tends to impair it, which supported Gardner's claim that it is a separate ability. Gardner's examples include navigators, sculptors, architects and chess players; he described the Puluwat sailors of the Caroline Islands in the Pacific, who navigate between islands without instruments by holding a mental map of stars, swells and islands. A pilot must constantly picture the aircraft's position in three dimensions, judge distances and angles on approach and read spatial displays. Example: Arjun, who could always rebuild a Lego model from memory and never gets lost in a new city, chooses pilot training. Option a is right because flying depends above all on visual and spatial judgement. Exam trap: teacher and psychologist sound like intelligence-heavy jobs, but in Gardner's scheme they rest mainly on interpersonal (and linguistic) intelligence, not spatial; a lawyer relies on linguistic and logical-mathematical intelligence.", "topic": "Gardner's spatial intelligence"}, {"q": "Among the biological and psycho-social human needs, the following is an example of a biological need:", "options": {"a": "The need for independence", "b": "The need for sexual satisfaction", "c": "The need for achievement", "d": "The need for self-assertion"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the need for sexual satisfaction is a biological need, while independence, achievement and self-assertion are psychosocial needs. Motivation textbooks divide motives into two broad classes. Biological (primary, physiological) needs are unlearned and rooted in the body: hunger, thirst, sleep, breathing, avoidance of pain, temperature regulation, the maternal drive and sex. Most are homeostatic, driven by a bodily deficit that the behaviour corrects. Psychosocial (secondary) needs are learned through experience and culture: achievement, affiliation, power, approval, independence and self-assertion. Henry Murray (1938), in Explorations in Personality, drew the same line between viscerogenic (bodily) needs, among which he listed sex, and psychogenic (mental) needs such as achievement, autonomy and dominance. Abraham Maslow (1943) also placed sex among the physiological needs at the base of his hierarchy. Sex is a slightly unusual biological motive: it is not needed for the survival of the individual, only of the species, and it is not homeostatic in the strict sense; but it rests on hormones, the hypothalamus and inborn reflexes, so it is classed as biological, even though culture shapes how it is expressed. Example: Riya's hunger after a long fast and her sex drive are biological needs; her wish to top the class, to live on her own and to speak up firmly in meetings are psychosocial. Option b is right because sex is the only listed need based on a bodily, unlearned drive. Exam trap: self-assertion sounds instinctive, and William McDougall (1908) did list it among his instincts, but in the modern biological versus psychosocial split it is a learned social motive.", "topic": "Biological versus psychosocial needs"}, {"q": "The Theory formulated by Bandura that human behaviour is learned by observation and imitation is called:", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalytic", "b": "Social Learning", "c": "Optimum Level", "d": "Incentive"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Albert Bandura's theory that behaviour is learned by watching and imitating others is social learning theory. Albert Bandura (1977), in Social Learning Theory, argued that people do not have to perform a behaviour and be reinforced in order to learn it; they can learn simply by observing a model and the consequences the model receives. His Bobo doll studies with Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961) showed this. Nursery school children who watched an adult punch, kick and hit an inflatable Bobo doll with a mallet later attacked the doll in much the same ways, often copying the adult's exact actions and words, while children who had seen a calm model rarely did. Later work showed vicarious reinforcement: children who saw the model rewarded imitated more than those who saw the model punished, yet all of them could reproduce the acts when offered a reward, so learning and performance are separate. Bandura named four processes in observational learning: attention to the model, retention in memory, reproduction of the act and motivation to perform it. In 1986 he renamed the approach social cognitive theory, adding self-efficacy and reciprocal determinism. Example: Kabir learns to roll chapatis by watching his grandmother, though no one ever rewarded him for trying. Option b is right because learning by observation and imitation is the heart of Bandura's social learning theory. Exam trap: Neal Miller and John Dollard (1941) wrote Social Learning and Imitation, and Julian Rotter (1954) also used the label social learning; but the theory of observational learning and modelling named in the stem is Bandura's.", "topic": "Bandura's social learning theory"}, {"q": "According to Bronfenbrenner's ecological Systems theory, the workplace of the spouse is part of:", "options": {"a": "Microsystem", "b": "Mesosystem", "c": "Exo-system", "d": "Macrosystem"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: the spouse's workplace belongs to the exosystem in Urie Bronfenbrenner's ecological systems theory. Urie Bronfenbrenner (1979), in The Ecology of Human Development, pictured a person at the centre of nested layers of environment, like a set of rings. The microsystem holds the settings the person is directly in, with face-to-face, two-way relationships: home, school, their own workplace, friends. The mesosystem is the set of links between two microsystems, such as a parent-teacher meeting that connects home and school. The exosystem holds settings the person never enters but which still reach them indirectly: a spouse's or parent's workplace, the local school board, the mass media. The macrosystem is the overarching culture: values, laws, customs and the economy. He later added the chronosystem, the dimension of time: life transitions such as divorce and historical events such as a pandemic. Example: Meera never sets foot in her husband Arjun's office. Yet when his manager moves him to night shifts, Arjun comes home tired and irritable, the family routine changes, and Meera's own days become harder. The office touches her only through him, which is exactly what an exosystem does. Option c is right because the spouse's workplace is a setting in which the person is not an active participant, but whose events still affect the person through the spouse. Exam trap: students pick microsystem because a workplace sounds like an immediate setting. It is a microsystem only for the person who works there (Arjun); for Meera it lies outside her direct experience, so it is exosystem. Also do not confuse the mesosystem, a link between two settings the person is in, with the exosystem, a setting the person is not in.", "topic": "Bronfenbrenner's ecological systems"}, {"q": "The Thematic Apperception Test is designed to measure:", "options": {"a": "Motivation", "b": "Scholarly ability", "c": "Emotional Quotient", "d": "Workplace Assessment"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was designed to measure motivation, that is, a person's needs, drives and inner conflicts. The TAT was published by Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935) at the Harvard Psychological Clinic. It is a projective test: the person is shown ambiguous pictures of people in uncertain situations and asked to tell a story about each one, saying what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters think and feel, and how it ends. The full set has 31 cards, including one blank card, and examiners usually use a selection of about 10 to 20. The theory behind it is Murray's need-press model from Explorations in Personality (1938). A need is an inner motive (achievement, affiliation, dominance, aggression); a press is a force in the environment that helps or blocks a need. Because the pictures are vague, the person 'apperceives' them through their own needs, and the hero of each story tends to carry those needs. David McClelland (1953) later scored TAT-style stories for the need for achievement. Example: shown a card of a boy looking at a violin, Kabir tells a story in which the boy practises day and night to win a national prize. The theme of striving and success suggests a strong need for achievement. Option a is right because the TAT reads needs, motives and conflicts from story themes, which is what motivation means here. Exam trap: the TAT is a personality and motivation test, not an ability test. Do not confuse it with the Rorschach (Hermann Rorschach, 1921), which uses inkblots and reads perception and thought, not story themes.", "topic": "Thematic Apperception Test"}, {"q": "The conflict that arises when a person gets a good job opportunity BUT he will have to go to a place he does not like is called:", "options": {"a": "Affective-Subjective conflict", "b": "Opportunity-Issue based conflict", "c": "Value based conflict", "d": "Approach-Avoidance conflict"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: a single goal that both attracts and repels creates an approach-avoidance conflict. Kurt Lewin (1935) described conflicts as clashes between forces that pull a person towards goals or push the person away from them. In an approach-approach conflict there are two attractive goals (two good job offers); it is the easiest to resolve, because moving towards one makes it look even better. In an avoidance-avoidance conflict there are two unpleasant options (a painful operation or continued pain); people waver and may try to escape the choice altogether. In an approach-avoidance conflict one goal has both a good and a bad side. A fourth type, double approach-avoidance, has two options that each have good and bad sides; it is the commonest in real life. Neal Miller (1944) showed with rats why approach-avoidance produces wavering: both tendencies grow stronger as the goal comes closer, but the avoidance gradient is steeper. So from far away the person wants the goal, close to it the fear wins, and he hovers where the two gradients cross. Example: Arjun is offered a well-paid job, but it is in a city he dislikes. While the joining date is months away he is keen; as it comes near, the dislike grows sharper and he starts to doubt. Option d is right because one opportunity carries both an attractive feature (the job) and an aversive feature (the place). Exam trap: if the stem had two different jobs, each with good and bad sides, the answer would be double approach-avoidance. Here there is one job, so it is plain approach-avoidance.", "topic": "Lewin's types of conflict"}, {"q": "In conflict management, the approach that engenders a \"losing-losing\" situation is", "options": {"a": "Collaborating", "b": "Accommodating", "c": "Competing", "d": "Compromising"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: in the conflict management model, compromising is the approach usually labelled 'lose-lose' among the options given. Kenneth Thomas and Ralph Kilmann (1974) built their Conflict Mode Instrument on two dimensions: assertiveness (how far you try to satisfy your own concerns) and cooperativeness (how far you try to satisfy the other person's). Crossing them gives five modes. Competing is high assertiveness and low cooperativeness: I win, you lose. Accommodating is the reverse: I give in so that you win (lose-win). Avoiding is low on both: the issue is sidestepped and nobody gains (lose-lose). Collaborating is high on both: the parties dig into the problem until both are fully satisfied (win-win). Compromising sits in the middle: each side gives up part of what it wanted, 'splitting the difference', so neither is fully satisfied. The model grew out of Robert Blake and Jane Mouton's (1964) managerial grid. Example: Riya wants the team outing in Goa and Kabir wants Manali. They settle on a nearby resort that neither of them really wanted. Each has partly lost, which is why compromise is often called lose-lose. Option d is right because, of the four modes offered, compromising is the only one in which both parties give up something they wanted and neither gets a full win. Exam trap: accommodating also involves losing, but only one party loses (lose-win); competing has one clear winner (win-lose); collaborating is the classic win-win. Key note: many textbooks reserve the purest 'lose-lose' label for avoiding and describe compromise as partial win, partial loss. Avoiding is not among the options, so compromising, the mode in which both sides give something up, is the answer used here, and it stands.", "topic": "Thomas-Kilmann conflict modes"}, {"q": "The test designed to assess ability to predict future performance is:", "options": {"a": "Achievement test", "b": "Aptitude test", "c": "Intelligence Quotient", "d": "NEO-Personality Inventory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: an aptitude test is built to predict how well a person will learn or perform in a specific area in future. Psychologists separate ability tests by the direction in which they look. An achievement test looks back: it measures what a person has already learnt in a subject, usually through teaching, such as a final mathematics examination. An aptitude test looks ahead: it estimates the capacity to acquire a skill with training, such as mechanical, clerical, musical, verbal or numerical aptitude. An intelligence test samples broad general ability and gives one overall score. Because their jobs differ, achievement tests are judged mainly by content validity (do the items cover the syllabus?), while aptitude tests are judged mainly by predictive validity (do today's scores correlate with later success?). A well-known example is the Differential Aptitude Tests of George Bennett, Harold Seashore and Alexander Wesman (1947), used in school and career guidance. Anne Anastasi (1954), in Psychological Testing, pointed out that the difference is one of degree, since all ability tests measure developed ability, but the purpose still differs. Example: Meera, aged 15, is unsure whether to take science. Her counsellor gives an aptitude battery; high mechanical reasoning and numerical scores suggest she is likely to do well in engineering even though she has never studied it. Option b is right because prediction of future performance is the defining purpose of an aptitude test. Exam trap: achievement and aptitude tests can contain similar items, so students mix them up. Ask whether the test checks past learning (achievement) or forecasts future learning (aptitude).", "topic": "Aptitude versus achievement tests"}, {"q": "The following test is a projective test for measurement of personality:", "options": {"a": "Rorschach", "b": "Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory", "c": "Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory", "d": "Positron Emission Tomography"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Rorschach inkblot test is a projective test of personality. Projective tests rest on the projective hypothesis: faced with an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, people fill it with meaning drawn from their own needs, conflicts and ways of thinking. The term 'projective methods' was popularised by Lawrence Frank (1939). Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published Psychodiagnostik with ten symmetrical inkblots: five in black and grey, two in black and red, and three multicoloured. In the free association phase the person says what each blot might be; in the inquiry phase the examiner asks where it was seen and what made it look that way. Responses are scored for location (whole blot or a detail), determinants (form, colour, shading, movement), content and popularity. John Exner (1974) standardised scoring in his Comprehensive System, and the newer R-PAS continues that work. Example: Arjun sees 'two people fighting over something' in several cards; a clinician would note the human movement and aggressive content and check it against other evidence before drawing any conclusion. Option a is right because the Rorschach uses an ambiguous stimulus with no right answer, the hallmark of a projective test. Exam trap: the MMPI and MCMI are personality tests too, but they are objective self-report inventories with fixed items and standard scoring. 'Personality test' does not mean 'projective test'.", "topic": "Projective versus objective tests"}, {"q": "The socio-cultural factors that affect personality development are:", "options": {"a": "Alcoholism", "b": "Diet", "c": "Peer group", "d": "Illness"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: the peer group is a socio-cultural factor in personality development. Psychologists usually sort the determinants of personality into biological and environmental factors. Biological factors include heredity, temperament, physique, the endocrine glands, nutrition and health; twin and adoption studies suggest genes account for up to about half of the variation in traits. The environment shapes the rest, and its socio-cultural part covers the family (parenting style, birth order), the school and teachers, the peer group, the neighbourhood, social class, religion, the mass media and the wider culture. Peers matter because children and adolescents spend much of their day with them and want to be accepted by them. Through peers a child learns cooperation, competition, sharing, leadership and the norms of the group, and peer approval shapes self-esteem and identity. Judith Rich Harris (1995), in her group socialisation theory, argued that peers may shape personality even more than parents do; that claim is debated, but peer influence itself is well established. Example: Riya was shy at home, but after joining a theatre group in Class 8 she began to speak up, took a lead role and grew more confident. The group's norms and approval drove the change. Option c is right because a peer group is a social grouping that passes on cultural norms and values, which is what 'socio-cultural' means. Exam trap: diet, illness and alcoholism can all change behaviour and mood, but they act through the body, so they are classed as physical or biological influences, not socio-cultural ones.", "topic": "Determinants of personality"}, {"q": "Carl Jung classified personality on the basis of certain characteristics; one of the terms in his classification was:", "options": {"a": "Pyknic", "b": "Ambivert", "c": "Mesomorphic", "d": "Phlegmatic"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: 'ambivert' is the term that belongs on Carl Jung's introversion-extraversion dimension. Carl Jung (1921), in Psychological Types, described two attitudes, the directions in which a person's psychic energy flows. In extraversion energy flows outward to people, objects and events; the extravert is sociable, active and quickly at ease. In introversion energy flows inward to thoughts, feelings and the inner world; the introvert is reflective, reserved and prefers small groups. Jung combined the two attitudes with four functions: thinking and feeling (ways of judging) and sensation and intuition (ways of perceiving), giving eight psychological types, the basis of the later Myers-Briggs Type Indicator of Katharine Cook Briggs and Isabel Briggs Myers (1940s). Jung stressed that both attitudes exist in everyone and that most people are not pure types. The middle of the dimension, where a person shows introverted and extraverted features fairly equally, is called the ambivert. Example: Kabir loves leading his college quiz team and chatting with strangers at events, yet he needs a quiet evening alone with a book to recharge. He is neither clearly introverted nor clearly extraverted; he is an ambivert. Option b is right because ambiversion names the middle position on Jung's introversion-extraversion attitude, and it is the only option linked with his typology. Exam trap: the other three are also 'type' words, but they belong to body-type and temperament theories, not to Jung; and pyknic is often wrongly attributed to William Sheldon, when it is Ernst Kretschmer's. Key note: the word 'ambivert' itself was coined after Jung, usually credited to the sociologist Kimball Young (1927). It is still the only option on Jung's dimension, so b stands.", "topic": "Jung's attitude types"}, {"q": "On the basis of Allport's trait theory of personality development, a rich person who gives away all his property to society is", "options": {"a": "Central traits", "b": "Cardinal traits", "c": "Secondary traits", "d": "Temperamental traits"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: a single ruling passion, such as giving away all one's wealth to society, is what Gordon Allport called a cardinal trait. Gordon Allport (1937), in Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, treated traits as real neuropsychological structures that guide behaviour across situations. He sorted a person's individual traits (later renamed personal dispositions) into three levels by how much of the life they govern. A cardinal trait is so dominant that almost every action can be traced to it; it is a master motive. Few people have one, and those who do often become a byword for it: Mother Teresa for selfless service, Gandhi for non-violence, Machiavelli for cunning. Central traits are the five to ten outstanding qualities you would mention in a reference letter, such as honest, friendly or hard-working. Secondary traits are narrow and situation-bound: preferences, attitudes and habits that show only in certain settings, like disliking crowds or loving mangoes. Example: Arjun, a wealthy businessman, spends his later life building schools and finally donates all his property to a trust for village children. His generosity is not one quality among many; it organises his whole life. That is a cardinal trait. Option b is right because giving away all one's property reflects a single, all-pervasive disposition that dominates the person's life, the definition of a cardinal trait. Exam trap: central traits are also important and stable, so students choose them. The test is breadth: central traits describe a person; a cardinal trait rules a person.", "topic": "Allport's cardinal traits"}, {"q": "In Borderline Personality disorders, the Behavioral approach includes:", "options": {"a": "Biofeedback", "b": "Behavioural activation", "c": "Desensitization", "d": "Dialectical behavioural therapy"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) is the behavioural treatment designed for borderline personality disorder. Marsha Linehan (1993) built DBT for chronically suicidal women with borderline personality disorder; her first randomised trial appeared in 1991 and the treatment manual in 1993. Her biosocial theory says borderline problems arise when a child with high emotional sensitivity grows up in an invalidating environment that dismisses or punishes her feelings, so she never learns to regulate emotion. DBT is a cognitive behavioural therapy with a dialectical core: the therapist balances acceptance (validating the person as she is, drawn partly from Zen practice) with change (behavioural skills and problem solving). Standard DBT has four parts: weekly individual therapy, a weekly skills group, telephone coaching in crises, and a consultation team for therapists. The skills group teaches four modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Trials show DBT reduces self-harm, suicide attempts and days in hospital. Example: Meera, 23, cuts herself after rows with her boyfriend. In DBT she keeps a diary card, learns distress tolerance skills such as holding ice or paced breathing to ride out the urge, and practises asking for what she needs without threats. Option d is right because DBT is the specific, evidence-based behavioural programme built for borderline personality disorder. Exam trap: biofeedback, desensitisation and behavioural activation are all genuine behavioural techniques, so they look plausible, but each was built for a different problem: physiological arousal and stress, phobias, and depression respectively.", "topic": "DBT for borderline personality"}, {"q": "On the basis of Jean Piaget's theory of personality development, the stage in which a child can do simple arithmetic but not algebraic manipulation is called:", "options": {"a": "Formal Operations", "b": "Concrete Operations", "c": "Intuitive Pre-Operational Phase", "d": "Role-Conformity phase"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: a child who can do simple arithmetic but not algebra is in Jean Piaget's concrete operational stage. Jean Piaget (writing from the 1920s to the 1960s) described four stages of cognitive development. In the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) the infant learns through action and acquires object permanence. In the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) the child uses symbols and language but is egocentric and fails conservation; its later intuitive phase (about 4 to 7) relies on how things look. In the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11) the child gains operations, mental actions that can be reversed. She can conserve number, mass and volume, classify, put objects in order (seriation) and add, subtract, multiply and divide, because these work on real or easily pictured quantities. What she cannot yet do is reason about abstract symbols and hypothetical possibilities. That arrives in the formal operational stage (about 11 or 12 onward), with hypothetico-deductive reasoning, the handling of variables such as x and y, and systematic testing of hypotheses, as in the pendulum task of Bärbel Inhelder and Piaget (1958). Example: Kabir, aged 9, quickly solves 'Meera has 12 sweets and gives away 5; how many are left?' but is baffled by 'if x plus 5 equals 12, what is x?', because x stands for nothing he can picture. Option b is right because arithmetic on concrete quantities is a concrete operation, while algebra demands the abstract, symbolic reasoning of formal operations. Exam trap: the stem calls this 'personality development', but Piaget's theory is about cognitive development; do not let that wording pull you towards a personality or moral stage.", "topic": "Piaget's concrete operations"}, {"q": "The personality of a person who exhibits extreme \"Black-and-White\" thinking and has instability in relationships will be classified as:", "options": {"a": "Paranoid", "b": "Borderline", "c": "Histrionic", "d": "Avoidant"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: black-and-white thinking together with unstable relationships points to borderline personality disorder. DSM-5-TR (2022) defines borderline personality disorder as a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood; five of nine criteria are needed. They include frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; unstable, intense relationships that swing between extremes of idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; marked mood reactivity; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and brief stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. The black-and-white style is called splitting. Otto Kernberg (1975) described it as a primitive defence in which people, including oneself, are seen as all good or all bad, with no blend of the two. Because the label can flip overnight, relationships lurch between adoration and rage. Example: in the first week Riya tells her new flatmate Meera that she is 'the best friend I have ever had'. A month later Meera forgets to reply to a message, and Riya decides she is cruel and selfish, shouts at her and threatens to move out, then begs her not to leave. Option b is right because splitting and a pattern of intense, unstable relationships are core features of borderline personality disorder, and no other option combines them. Exam trap: histrionic personality disorder also shows shifting emotions and dramatic relationships, but its core is attention-seeking and shallow emotion, not all-or-nothing splitting driven by fear of abandonment.", "topic": "Borderline personality and splitting"}, {"q": "The psychology of a caregiver of a sick person and that of a person grieving for the loss of a family member (Kubler-Ross stages) have the following in common:", "options": {"a": "Blaming", "b": "Denial", "c": "Aggression", "d": "Displacement"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: denial is the shared reaction, the first of Elisabeth Kubler-Ross's stages. Elisabeth Kubler-Ross (1969), a Swiss-American psychiatrist who interviewed dying patients at a Chicago hospital, described five stages in On Death and Dying: denial ('this cannot be happening to me'), anger ('why me?'), bargaining (trying to buy time, often with God), depression (sadness at what is being lost) and acceptance (calm readiness). She noted that the families of dying patients pass through similar reactions, and the model was later applied to grief after any major loss. Denial comes first because it acts as a buffer: it gives the mind time to absorb shocking news before it has to be faced in full. Later research shows the stages are not fixed or universal; people may skip, repeat or mix them, so they are best treated as common reactions, not a timetable. Example: when Arjun's father is diagnosed with advanced cancer, Arjun keeps saying 'the reports must be wrong' and books a third opinion. Months later, after his father dies, Arjun's sister Riya keeps setting a plate for him at dinner and says she half expects him to walk in. Caregiver and mourner share the same first move: refusing, for a while, to believe the loss. Option b is right because denial is the opening stage of the Kubler-Ross model, seen both in those caring for the seriously ill and in the bereaved. Exam trap: anger is a Kubler-Ross stage, so 'aggression' and 'blaming' look tempting, but those are behaviours within anger, not stage names; displacement is a defence mechanism, not a stage.", "topic": "Kubler-Ross stages of grief"}, {"q": "The correct statement regarding Primordial prevention is:", "options": {"a": "It aims to prevent the development of disease risk factors", "b": "It aims to modify existing risk factors to prevent disease in future", "c": "Its target population is the susceptible population", "d": "Its target population is the symptomatic patient"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: primordial prevention aims to stop disease risk factors from developing in the first place. Public health describes prevention in levels that match the natural history of disease. Primordial prevention, a term introduced by Toma Strasser (1978) for heart disease, acts before risk factors even appear in a population. It works on the whole population and its way of life: healthy diet, physical activity, no tobacco, and sound social and environmental conditions. Primary prevention acts in the stage of susceptibility, when risk factors exist but disease has not begun; it targets susceptible people and modifies existing risk factors through health promotion and specific protection (immunisation, treating high blood pressure, quitting smoking). Secondary prevention acts in early disease: early diagnosis and prompt treatment, such as screening for cervical cancer or diabetes. Tertiary prevention acts in established disease in symptomatic patients: limiting disability and rehabilitation. Gerald Caplan (1964) brought the primary, secondary and tertiary levels into mental health. Example: a school in Pune removes junk food from its canteen and runs daily sport so that its pupils never become obese or start smoking; that is primordial. Giving a diet plan to Kabir, an overweight 40-year-old with high cholesterol, is primary; screening him for diabetes is secondary; cardiac rehabilitation after his heart attack is tertiary. Option a is right because preventing the emergence of risk factors themselves, not just disease, is the definition of primordial prevention. Exam trap: option b sounds similar, but modifying risk factors that already exist is primary prevention. The word 'existing' is the giveaway.", "topic": "Levels of prevention"}, {"q": "A stimulus that does not initially on its own elicit a response from the organism is called:", "options": {"a": "Unconditioned stimulus", "b": "Neutral stimulus", "c": "Conditioned stimulus", "d": "Negative stimulus"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: a stimulus that does not on its own produce the response under study is a neutral stimulus. Ivan Pavlov (1927), a Russian physiologist who had won the 1904 Nobel Prize for work on digestion, noticed that his dogs began to salivate before food reached their mouths, and he worked out the parts of classical conditioning in Conditioned Reflexes. The unconditioned stimulus (UCS), such as food, produces a response naturally, without learning. That response, salivation to food, is the unconditioned response (UCR). A neutral stimulus (NS), such as the sound of a metronome or bell, produces no salivation at first; at most it causes an orienting response, the dog pricking up its ears. After the neutral stimulus is repeatedly presented just before the food, it becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that triggers salivation on its own, now called the conditioned response (CR). Timing matters: learning is best when the neutral stimulus comes slightly before the unconditioned stimulus (forward conditioning). Example: Meera's mother always puts the pressure cooker on before serving dinner. At first the whistle means nothing to Meera's stomach (neutral stimulus). After weeks of whistles followed by food, Meera's mouth waters the moment it whistles (conditioned stimulus). Option b is right because a stimulus that does not initially elicit the response is, by definition, neutral. Exam trap: the same physical stimulus changes its label over time. The bell is a neutral stimulus before conditioning and a conditioned stimulus after it, so check whether the question describes it before or after pairing.", "topic": "Neutral stimulus in conditioning"}, {"q": "The Neurotic defence mechanism by which a person who cheats on his own Income tax return but also criticizes the government for corruption is called:", "options": {"a": "Displacement", "b": "Dissociation", "c": "Compartmentalization", "d": "Projection"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: holding two contradictory positions in separate mental boxes, cheating on tax while condemning government corruption, is compartmentalisation. Defence mechanisms, first described by Sigmund Freud (1894) and catalogued by his daughter Anna Freud (1936), are unconscious ways in which the ego protects itself from anxiety. Compartmentalisation keeps conflicting attitudes, beliefs or behaviours apart in separate mental compartments, so that the person never feels the contradiction between them. Each attitude is switched on in its own setting and never set beside the other, so there is no guilt and no dissonance to resolve. It is usually described as a neurotic-level defence: it distorts reality less than psychotic defences such as delusional projection, but more than mature ones such as humour or suppression. It is close to isolation of affect and rationalisation, and it is familiar in everyday hypocrisy: a doctor who smokes, or a strict vegetarian who happily wears leather. Example: Kabir under-reports his income every year and is proud of 'saving' on tax. At dinner he speaks passionately about how corrupt politicians are ruining the country. He sees no conflict, because his own tax return and 'public corruption' sit in different compartments of his mind. Option c is right because the man holds two incompatible standards at once without ever connecting them, which is exactly what compartmentalisation does. Exam trap: projection is the closest distractor. In projection a person denies his own impulse and sees it in others instead. This man does not deny his cheating or locate it in the government; he simply keeps his cheating and his criticism apart.", "topic": "Compartmentalisation as a defence"}, {"q": "A mature defence mechanism where a painter who has obsessive sexual ideas redirects them into his paintings is:", "options": {"a": "Introjection", "b": "Sublimation", "c": "Anticipation", "d": "Compensation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: channelling forbidden sexual ideas into painting is sublimation, a mature defence. Sigmund Freud (1905), in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, described sublimation as redirecting sexual or aggressive drive energy away from its original aim towards a socially valued, non-sexual goal such as art, science or sport. He saw it as the healthiest defence and a source of much of civilisation, and Anna Freud (1936) kept it in her list of defences. George Vaillant (1977), in Adaptation to Life, drew on the Harvard Study of Adult Development and ranked defences from psychotic through immature and neurotic to mature. His mature defences are altruism, anticipation, humour, sublimation and suppression, and men who used them more had better health, careers and marriages. Sublimation counts as mature because the impulse is neither denied nor acted out; it gets real but acceptable expression, and the person gains from it. Example: Kabir, a young painter, is troubled by intense sexual thoughts that he finds shameful. Instead of acting on them or forcing them out of mind, he pours their energy into vivid, sensual paintings of the human form that win him an exhibition. The drive is kept; only its outlet has changed. Option b is right because the stem describes an unacceptable drive transformed into socially valued creative work, the textbook definition of sublimation. Exam trap: anticipation is also on Vaillant's list of mature defences, so the word 'mature' alone does not decide the answer. The deciding feature is the redirection of a forbidden impulse into a valued activity.", "topic": "Sublimation as a mature defence"}, {"q": "The Psychological approach in Anxiety is:", "options": {"a": "Biofeedback", "b": "Acceptance and Commitment therapy", "c": "Peer support", "d": "Masters and Johnson technique"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT) is the psychological treatment for anxiety in this list. Steven Hayes, Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson (1999) set out ACT, one of the 'third wave' cognitive behavioural therapies. It grows from relational frame theory, an account of how language lets thoughts take on the power of the things they describe. ACT does not try to argue thoughts away or remove anxiety. It holds that much suffering comes from experiential avoidance: struggling to get rid of unwanted feelings, which narrows life and often makes the feelings stronger. Its goal is psychological flexibility, built through six processes (the 'hexaflex'): acceptance, cognitive defusion (seeing a thought as just a thought), contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values and committed action. Trials and meta-analyses support ACT for anxiety disorders, depression and chronic pain. Example: Riya avoids presentations because she fears her voice will shake. Her ACT therapist helps her notice 'I will look stupid' as a passing thought, make room for the racing heart, and step towards what she values, a career in teaching, by volunteering to present anyway. Option b is right because ACT is a structured psychotherapy that changes the person's relationship with anxious thoughts and feelings. Exam trap: do not confuse ACT with the dialectical behaviour therapy of Marsha Linehan (1993), which teaches four skill modules, or with the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (1976), which changes the content of thoughts; ACT changes how a person relates to them. Key note: biofeedback is also used for anxiety and is taught by psychologists as a behavioural self-regulation technique, so option a is arguable. But it trains a physiological signal rather than being a full psychological therapy, so b stands.", "topic": "ACT for anxiety"}, {"q": "The following is NOT a principle in the Counselling process:", "options": {"a": "Include client's family members if needed", "b": "Respecting the bizarre feelings of the client", "c": "Helping client develop a positive attitude", "d": "Making decisions for the client"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: making decisions for the client breaks a basic principle of counselling. Counselling is a helping relationship in which a trained person helps a client understand a problem and find his or her own way through it. Carl Rogers (1951), in Client-Centered Therapy, argued that the client, not the counsellor, is the expert on his own life and has an inborn drive towards growth; the counsellor's job is to provide empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness. Felix Biestek (1957) set out seven principles of the casework relationship that counselling shares: individualisation, purposeful expression of feelings, controlled emotional involvement, acceptance, a non-judgemental attitude, client self-determination and confidentiality. Client self-determination means the client makes the final choices. Deciding for him breeds dependence, removes his ownership of the outcome and lets the counsellor's values take over. The other options fit accepted practice: accept and respect all of the client's feelings, even odd or 'bizarre' ones; involve the family when the problem or its solution lies partly in the family; and help the client build a hopeful, positive attitude. Example: Arjun, 22, cannot decide whether to take a job in Bengaluru or stay home to care for his ageing parents. A good counsellor helps him weigh the pros and cons, explore his values and feelings, and perhaps meets the parents too. She does not tell him 'take the job'. Option d is right because counselling respects client self-determination; decisions belong to the client. Exam trap: advice-giving and directive guidance are older styles, so 'deciding for the client' can sound helpful. In counselling it is a violation, not a technique.", "topic": "Principles of counselling"}, {"q": "The following statement is true regarding isolated adjustment disorders:", "options": {"a": "Emphasis should be on strengthening coping mechanisms", "b": "Prolonged in-depth psychotherapy is essential", "c": "Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors are needed for many months", "d": "Sedatives do not provide relief in the acute phase"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: in an isolated adjustment disorder, treatment centres on strengthening the person's coping. An adjustment disorder is an emotional or behavioural reaction to an identifiable stressor, such as a divorce, job loss, exam failure or move, that is out of proportion to the stressor or impairs functioning. In DSM-5-TR the symptoms begin within 3 months of the stressor and do not last more than 6 months after the stressor or its consequences have ended. Subtypes include with depressed mood, with anxiety, with mixed anxiety and depressed mood, and with disturbance of conduct. 'Isolated' means it stands alone, without another disorder such as major depression. Because it is by definition a time-limited response to a specific stress, the main treatment is brief: supportive counselling or crisis intervention, problem solving, reducing or removing the stressor where possible, mobilising family and social support, and teaching coping skills such as relaxation and structured problem solving. Medicines are not the core; a short course of a sedative or anxiolytic may be used for a few nights of severe insomnia or acute distress. Example: after failing her final-year exam, Meera, 21, stops eating properly, cries daily and skips classes for six weeks. Her counsellor helps her draw up a revision plan, rebuild her routine, lean on friends and practise breathing exercises; within two months she is back to normal. Option a is right because adjustment disorder is a short reaction to stress, and building coping skills and support is the main aim. Exam trap: options that sound more intensive (long in-depth therapy, months of SSRIs) are tempting because they seem thorough, but adjustment disorder usually resolves with brief support.", "topic": "Managing adjustment disorder"}, {"q": "In a case of Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder, once the diagnosis is made, the following is true regarding management:", "options": {"a": "Prolonged Psychotherapy", "b": "Cognitive processing therapy is useful", "c": "Clonazepam is very useful", "d": "Thought stopping is useful"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: cognitive processing therapy (CPT) is a useful, first-line, evidence-based treatment once PTSD is diagnosed. Post-traumatic stress disorder follows exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence; in DSM-5-TR it has four symptom clusters (intrusions, avoidance, negative changes in thoughts and mood, and changes in arousal and reactivity) lasting more than a month. Patricia Resick (1988 onward) developed CPT, first for survivors of rape. It is a structured cognitive therapy of about 12 sessions. The person writes an impact statement about what the trauma means to them and works on 'stuck points', distorted beliefs about blame, safety, trust, power, esteem and intimacy, using Socratic questioning and worksheets; an optional written account of the trauma adds exposure. Major guidelines name trauma-focused therapies first: CPT, prolonged exposure (Edna Foa, 1980s onward), eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing (Francine Shapiro, 1989) and trauma-focused CBT. Example: after an assault, Riya, 26, believes 'it was my fault for going out at night' and 'nowhere is safe'. In CPT she examines the evidence for the self-blame, comes to see that responsibility lies with the attacker, and her shame and nightmares ease. Option b is right because CPT is a specific trauma-focused therapy with strong trial support for PTSD. Exam trap: 'prolonged psychotherapy' sounds like Prolonged Exposure therapy, a real and specific PTSD treatment, but the option only describes long, vague therapy, which is not a recommended plan.", "topic": "Treatment of PTSD"}, {"q": "The following is true regarding management of Generalized Anxiety Disorder:", "options": {"a": "Antidepressants are more useful than benzodiazepines", "b": "Exposure and Response prevention approach is very useful", "c": "Emotive imagery is not useful", "d": "Deep Brain Stimulation is effective"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: for generalised anxiety disorder (GAD), antidepressants are more useful than benzodiazepines as long-term treatment. GAD is excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters, more days than not for at least 6 months, with three or more of six symptoms: restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension and sleep disturbance. It tends to be chronic, so treatment must be safe for months or years. First-line medicines are selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (escitalopram, sertraline, paroxetine) and serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors (venlafaxine, duloxetine); pregabalin and buspirone are alternatives. They take 2 to 4 weeks to work but treat both the anxiety and the depression that often comes with it, and they do not cause dependence. Benzodiazepines (diazepam, alprazolam, clonazepam) act within an hour by boosting GABA, but tolerance, dependence, withdrawal, sedation and memory problems mean they are kept for short-term or bridging use, usually 2 to 4 weeks. Among psychological treatments, cognitive behavioural therapy (worry exposure, cognitive restructuring, applied relaxation) is first-line. Example: Kabir, 35, has worried constantly about money, his children and his health for a year. His psychiatrist starts escitalopram, adds clonazepam only to cover the first few weeks and then tapers it off, while a psychologist begins CBT. Option a is right because SSRIs and SNRIs are first-line long-term drugs for GAD, while benzodiazepines are limited by dependence. Exam trap: benzodiazepines work faster and feel more 'anti-anxiety', so students rate them higher. Speed of relief is not the same as long-term usefulness.", "topic": "Drug treatment of GAD"}, {"q": "In a patient with obsessive compulsive disorder, the Psychological Cognitive Behaviour Therapy approach is:", "options": {"a": "Thought stopping", "b": "Vocational rehabilitation", "c": "Relaxation technique", "d": "Emotive imagery"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: among these options, thought stopping is the cognitive behavioural technique aimed at the obsessions of OCD. Obsessive-compulsive disorder has obsessions, which are intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety, and compulsions, which are repetitive acts or mental rituals done to reduce that anxiety. Thought stopping was first used for obsessional thinking by James Taylor in the 1950s and popularised by Joseph Wolpe (1960s) as a behaviour therapy technique. The person deliberately brings on the obsessive thought, and the therapist (later the person, silently) says 'Stop!' firmly to interrupt it; snapping a rubber band on the wrist or switching to a pleasant scene may be added. With practice the person learns to cut the thought off early. Example: Arjun keeps getting the thought 'my hands are contaminated'. In session he lets the thought come, then says 'Stop!' and turns his attention to a planned task. Option a is right because thought stopping is the only option that is a cognitive behavioural method directed at obsessional thoughts. Exam trap: today the first-line psychological treatment for OCD is exposure and response prevention, which grew from the work of Victor Meyer (1966) and was refined by Edna Foa; if ERP were offered, it would win. Key note: modern guidelines put exposure and response prevention first because suppressing a thought can make it rebound, as Daniel Wegner showed in his 'white bear' studies (1987). Thought stopping is now seen as weak for OCD, but among these options it is the only OCD-directed cognitive behavioural technique, so a stands.", "topic": "Thought stopping in OCD"}, {"q": "The development of new onset diabetes mellitus is most likely with the use of atypical antipsychotic:", "options": {"a": "Clozapine", "b": "Lurasidone", "c": "Aripiprazole", "d": "Ziprasidone"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: among atypical antipsychotics, clozapine carries the highest risk of new-onset diabetes mellitus. Atypical (second-generation) antipsychotics block serotonin 5-HT2A as well as dopamine D2 receptors, so they cause fewer movement side effects than older drugs such as haloperidol. Their price is metabolic: weight gain, raised blood sugar, insulin resistance, raised lipids and, in some patients, new type 2 diabetes, occasionally with diabetic ketoacidosis. The risk varies sharply by drug. Clozapine and olanzapine are highest; quetiapine and risperidone are intermediate; aripiprazole, ziprasidone and lurasidone are lowest. The mechanisms include histamine H1 and serotonin 5-HT2C blockade, which increase appetite and weight, and direct effects on insulin action that can appear even before weight rises. Clozapine is kept for treatment-resistant schizophrenia, where it is the most effective drug, so patients on it need regular checks of weight, waist, fasting glucose or HbA1c and lipids, along with blood counts for agranulocytosis. Example: Kabir, 30, with treatment-resistant schizophrenia, finally improves on clozapine, but within a year he gains 12 kilograms and his fasting sugar rises into the diabetic range. His psychiatrist adds lifestyle advice and metformin rather than stopping the one drug that works. Option a is right because clozapine, together with olanzapine, has the strongest link with weight gain and new-onset diabetes among the atypicals. Exam trap: clozapine's famous side effect is agranulocytosis, so students forget its metabolic risk. Remember both: clozapine tops the list for agranulocytosis and for diabetes.", "topic": "Antipsychotics and diabetes"}, {"q": "A Client goes to bed at 10PM and is still unable to sleep till 11PM. The next step is:", "options": {"a": "Take a cup of coffee", "b": "See a programme on TV", "c": "Put on the light and try to sleep", "d": "Do meditation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: meditation, a calming relaxation method, is the right next step for someone still awake an hour after going to bed. Sleep needs low arousal. Anything that stimulates the body or mind delays sleep, and lying in bed awake and frustrated teaches the brain to link the bed with wakefulness. Cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia. Its key part is stimulus control, from Richard Bootzin (1972): go to bed only when sleepy; use the bed only for sleep and sex; if not asleep within about 20 minutes, get up, go to another room and do something quiet and relaxing in dim light, returning only when sleepy; get up at the same time every day; avoid daytime naps. Other parts include sleep restriction (Arthur Spielman, 1987), relaxation training, cognitive work on sleep worries and sleep hygiene (no caffeine late in the day, no screens before bed). Meditation and relaxation lower heart rate, muscle tension and racing thoughts, which lets sleep come. Example: Riya lies awake from 10 to 11 pm worrying about work. Rather than scrolling her phone or making tea, she sits in a dim chair and does 15 minutes of slow-breathing meditation, then returns to bed when her eyes feel heavy. Option d is right because meditation lowers arousal, and it is the only option that does. The paper printed 'mediation', which means meditation. Exam trap: 'trying' to sleep feels sensible but backfires, because effort raises arousal; and television, though passive, brings stimulating content and bright screen light that suppresses melatonin.", "topic": "Insomnia and stimulus control"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"gmch-2023": [{"q": "You hypothesize that paediatric cases of Autism in your clinic are due to maternal vaccination with a Vaccine which was popular few years ago. The design of your study which is ethically acceptable and provides the information in a reasonable time is:", "concept": "The answer is b: a case-control study is the design that is both ethical and quick enough to test this hypothesis. A case-control study starts from the outcome and looks backwards. The researcher selects cases (children who already have autism) and controls (similar children without autism), then compares how often each group was exposed in the past, here to the mother's vaccination. Nobody is followed forward for years and nobody is exposed on purpose, so it is fast, cheap and ethically clean. It is also the best design for a rare outcome such as autism, because it begins with the cases a clinic already has instead of waiting for new ones to appear. The result is expressed as an odds ratio: the odds of exposure among cases divided by the odds of exposure among controls. Example: Dr Meera takes 50 children with autism from her clinic and 100 children without autism from the same area, matched for age and sex, and checks their mothers' vaccination records. If exposure is no more common among the cases, the odds ratio sits near 1 and the hypothesis fails. The best-known vaccine scare, Andrew Wakefield's 1998 paper linking the MMR vaccine to autism, was retracted in 2010, and large later studies found no link. Option b is right because it tests the link using existing cases, without harming anyone and without waiting years. Exam trap: the randomised controlled trial is the strongest design for cause and effect, so students pick it; but deliberately vaccinating mothers to see whether their children develop autism would be unethical. A prospective cohort is ethical but would need thousands of pregnancies followed for years.", "traps": {"a": "A prospective cohort starts with mothers grouped by vaccination status and follows their children forward until autism appears. It is ethical, but autism is rare and diagnosed years after birth, so it needs a huge sample and a long wait, failing the 'reasonable time' condition.", "c": "A descriptive study only describes who has the disorder, where and when (time, place and person); with no comparison group it can raise a hypothesis but cannot test the link between the vaccine and autism.", "d": "A randomised controlled trial assigns the exposure by chance, so mothers would have to be vaccinated on purpose to see whether their children became autistic; deliberately exposing people to a suspected harm is unethical."}}, {"q": "In a Cohort Study design the following is TRUE:", "concept": "The answer is d: a cohort study provides information on incidence rates. A cohort is a group of people who share something, such as being born in the same year or working in the same factory. In a cohort study the researcher starts with people who are free of the disease, classifies them by exposure (exposed versus not exposed), and follows them forward in time, counting the new cases that appear in each group. Counting new cases over a period in a population at risk is exactly what incidence means. So a cohort study gives the incidence among the exposed and the unexposed, and from these the relative risk (incidence in exposed divided by incidence in unexposed) and the attributable risk. A classic example is the British Doctors Study of Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill (1951 onward), which followed thousands of British doctors by smoking habit and showed far higher lung cancer death rates among smokers. Example: Arjun, a public health researcher in Pune, enrols 5,000 healthy adults, records who chews tobacco, and checks every year for new cases of oral cancer. Cohort studies need large samples, long follow-up and much money, and people drop out over time, but they show clearly that the exposure came before the disease. Option d is right because only a design that follows a disease-free group forward can count new cases. Exam trap: students mix up the two observational designs. 'Starts with people who have the disease', 'small sample' and 'good for rare diseases' all describe the case-control study. Cohort studies suit rare exposures, not rare diseases.", "traps": {"a": "Selecting people who already have the disease is the starting point of a case-control study; a cohort must begin with disease-free people so that new cases can be counted.", "b": "A small study population is a feature of the case-control design; cohort studies usually need thousands of people because only a few will develop the disease during follow-up.", "c": "Rare disorders are best studied with a case-control design, which starts from the existing cases; a cohort would have to follow huge numbers for years to catch a handful of cases. Cohorts suit rare exposures instead."}}, {"q": "The term sensitivity in statistics while using measurement instruments is defined as:", "concept": "The answer is a: sensitivity is the ability of a test or instrument to identify a case correctly. Imagine every person tested falls into one of four boxes: true positive (has the disorder, test says yes), false negative (has it, test says no), false positive (does not have it, test says yes) and true negative (does not have it, test says no). Sensitivity = true positives divided by all who truly have the disorder (true positives plus false negatives); it is the true positive rate. Specificity is its partner: true negatives divided by all who are truly free of the disorder. Jacob Yerushalmy (1947) introduced the two terms while evaluating chest X-ray screening for tuberculosis. Example: Riya gives a depression screening questionnaire to 100 patients whom psychiatrists have already diagnosed with depression. If it flags 90 of them, its sensitivity is 90 per cent; the 10 it misses are false negatives. A highly sensitive test rarely misses a case, so a negative result helps rule the disorder out (memory aid SnNout: Sensitive test, Negative result, rules out). That is why screening tests are chosen for high sensitivity and confirmatory tests for high specificity (SpPin). Sensitivity and specificity belong to the test itself and do not change with prevalence; predictive values do. Option a is right because sensitivity asks: of the people who really have the condition, what share does the test catch? Exam trap: examiners reverse the pair, so option b (identifying those without disease) is specificity. They also offer the predictive values, which start from the test result rather than from the person's true disease status.", "traps": {"b": "Correctly identifying those without the disease is specificity: true negatives divided by all disease-free people (true negatives plus false positives). It is the mirror image of sensitivity.", "c": "Positive predictive value is true positives divided by all who test positive: the chance that a person with a positive result really has the disorder. It starts from the test result and rises or falls with prevalence, unlike sensitivity.", "d": "Negative predictive value is true negatives divided by all who test negative: the chance that a person with a negative result is really free of the disorder. It also depends on prevalence and is a different index from sensitivity."}}, {"q": "The example of a continuous variable is:", "concept": "The answer is a: height is a continuous variable. A variable is continuous when it can take any value within a range, including fractions, so that between any two values another is always possible. Height can be 152 cm, 152.5 cm or 152.53 cm; the only limit is the precision of the measuring tape. Continuous variables are measured rather than counted: height, weight, age, blood pressure, temperature and reaction time are typical. A discrete variable can take only separate values, usually whole-number counts with gaps between them, such as the number of children in a family or the number of hospital admissions in a month. Categorical (qualitative) variables place people in named groups with no numerical value, such as gender or blood group. On Stanley Smith Stevens's (1946) levels of measurement these are nominal scales, while height is a ratio scale because it has a true zero. Example: Kabir records four facts about each patient on a ward: height (continuous), number of children (discrete), blood group (nominal) and gender (nominal). Only height can be measured to any decimal place and sensibly summarised with a mean and standard deviation of individual values. Option a is right because height varies smoothly and can be measured to any fineness. Exam trap: 'number of children' is numerical, so it looks continuous, but it is a count. A family can have 2 or 3 children, never 2.4; an average of 2.4 children per family is a summary statistic, not a value the variable itself can take. Numerical does not mean continuous.", "traps": {"b": "Gender is a categorical (nominal) variable: it sorts people into named groups that have no numerical size or order, so it cannot be continuous.", "c": "Blood group (the ABO system described by Karl Landsteiner, 1901) is a nominal categorical variable; the groups A, B, AB and O are labels, not amounts.", "d": "Number of children is a discrete quantitative variable: it is counted in whole numbers with gaps between possible values, so it cannot take values such as 2.4."}}, {"q": "The following is not a measure of central tendency in statistics:", "concept": "The answer is d: variance is not a measure of central tendency; it is a measure of dispersion. A measure of central tendency gives a single typical value around which the data cluster, and there are three main ones. The mean is the arithmetic average (the sum of scores divided by their number); it uses every score but is pulled by extreme values. The median is the middle score when the data are put in order; it resists outliers, so it suits skewed data such as income. The mode is the most frequent score and the only one usable with nominal data. Variance answers a different question: how spread out are the scores around the mean? It is the average of the squared deviations from the mean, and its square root is the standard deviation. The term was introduced by Ronald Fisher (1918). Other measures of dispersion are the range, the quartile deviation and the mean deviation. Example: Meera's two tuition batches both have a mean score of 60. In batch one every student scored between 58 and 62; in batch two scores ran from 30 to 90. The central tendency is identical, but the variance of batch two is far larger. Two sets of data can share a centre and differ completely in spread, which is why both kinds of measure are reported together. Option d is right because variance describes scatter, not the centre. Exam trap: 'NOT' questions list three members of one family and one from a neighbouring family. Variance, standard deviation and range often hide among mean, median and mode; anything that measures spread belongs to variability, not central tendency.", "traps": {"a": "The mean (arithmetic average) is the most widely used measure of central tendency; it uses every score but is distorted by extreme values.", "b": "The median is the middle value of ordered data, a measure of central tendency preferred for skewed distributions because outliers barely move it.", "c": "The mode is the most frequently occurring value, a measure of central tendency and the only one that works for nominal data such as blood group."}}, {"q": "The frequency distribution of continuous variables can be depicted by a graphic representation called:", "concept": "The answer is a: a histogram depicts the frequency distribution of a continuous variable. In a histogram the class intervals of the variable (for example, heights of 140 to 145 cm, 145 to 150 cm and so on) run along the horizontal axis, and the frequency of each interval is shown by a bar. The bars touch each other with no gaps, because the variable is continuous: one class ends exactly where the next begins. Strictly, it is the area of each bar that is proportional to the frequency, which matters when class intervals are unequal. Karl Pearson (1895) coined the word 'histogram'. Joining the midpoints of the tops of the bars gives a frequency polygon, and a cumulative frequency curve is called an ogive; both are also used for continuous data. Example: Dr Arjun weighs 200 newborns in a district hospital and groups the weights into intervals of 250 g. A histogram instantly shows whether most babies cluster near 3 kg and whether there is a long tail of low birth weight babies on the left. Option a is right because the histogram is the standard graph built for grouped continuous data. Exam trap: bar charts and histograms look alike. Bar charts (simple, multiple or composite) have gaps between the bars and suit discrete or categorical data, such as the number of patients in each diagnosis. If the variable is continuous, think histogram, frequency polygon or ogive; if it is categorical, think bar chart or pie chart.", "traps": {"b": "A composite (component) bar chart divides each separate bar into segments, for example male and female patients within each year; its bars have gaps and it is meant for categorical or discrete data, not a continuous frequency distribution.", "c": "A 'correlation line' is not a frequency graph; the relationship between two variables is shown on a scatter diagram, and a line drawn through it shows association, not how often values occur.", "d": "Linear regression is a statistical method, not a type of graph: it fits the line Y = a + bX to predict one variable from another (an idea going back to Francis Galton, 1886). It does not show the frequency of values of one variable."}}, {"q": "Find the mode and median in the set of data provided for weights of 8 children: 30, 35, 40, 45, 50, 50, 55, 60", "concept": "The answer is c: the mode is 50 and the median is 47.5. The mode is the value that occurs most often. In the set 30, 35, 40, 45, 50, 50, 55, 60, every weight appears once except 50, which appears twice, so the mode is 50. The median is the middle value once the data are arranged in order. With an odd number of values it is simply the middle one; with an even number it is the mean of the two middle values. Here n = 8, so the middle positions are the 4th and 5th, that is the (n/2)th and (n/2 + 1)th values. The 4th value is 45 and the 5th is 50. Median = (45 + 50) / 2 = 95 / 2 = 47.5. The data are already in ascending order, but always check this before finding a median, or you will pick the wrong middle. For comparison, the mean is 365 / 8, about 45.6. Example: Riya, a school counsellor, records these weights for eight children in her group. If a ninth child weighing 70 kg joins, n becomes 9, the median moves to the single middle (5th) value, which is 50, and the mode stays 50. Option c is right because 50 is the only repeated value and 47.5 is the average of the two central values. Exam trap: option b gives the same two numbers with their roles reversed, catching anyone who calculates correctly but mislabels. Another trap is to take 50 as the median because it is the 5th of 8 values; with an even count there is no single middle value.", "traps": {"a": "Mode 30 picks the smallest value, but 30 occurs only once; and median 50 wrongly takes the 5th value alone instead of averaging the 4th and 5th.", "b": "This reverses the labels: 47.5 is the median (the average of 45 and 50), and a mode must be a value that actually occurs in the data, which 47.5 does not.", "d": "60 is the largest value, not the most frequent one, so it is not the mode; and median 50 again ignores that an even-numbered set needs the mean of the two middle values."}}, {"q": "From the list below, the highest level of evidence in scientific literature is generated by", "concept": "The answer is c: of these options, randomised controlled trials generate the highest level of evidence. Evidence-based medicine ranks study designs by how well they protect against bias. The usual hierarchy, from top to bottom, is: systematic reviews and meta-analyses of randomised controlled trials (RCTs); single well-run RCTs; cohort studies; case-control studies; case series and case reports; and finally expert opinion and committee recommendations. RCTs rank high because randomisation spreads both known and unknown confounding factors equally between groups, so a difference in outcome can be credited to the treatment; blinding and concealed allocation reduce bias further. The first modern RCT was the Medical Research Council streptomycin trial for tuberculosis (1948), designed by Austin Bradford Hill. Archie Cochrane (1972), in Effectiveness and Efficiency, argued that health care should be judged by such trials, and the Cochrane Collaboration (1993) now pools them. Example: Kabir wants to know whether a yoga programme reduces anxiety. Twenty observational studies showing that people who do yoga are less anxious cannot rule out that calmer people choose yoga; one RCT that randomly assigns 200 patients to yoga or a waiting list can. Option c is right because it is the only option based on randomised, experimental evidence. Exam trap: option d contains the magic words 'systematic review', but a review is only as strong as the studies it pools. A systematic review of correlation (observational) studies inherits their confounding, so it sits below a well-run RCT. Only a systematic review of RCTs sits above a single RCT.", "traps": {"a": "Expert committee recommendations sit at the very bottom of the evidence hierarchy because they rest on opinion and experience rather than on systematically collected data.", "b": "Observational studies (cohort, case-control, cross-sectional) record exposures without assigning them, so confounding cannot be ruled out; they rank below RCTs.", "d": "A systematic review is only as strong as its ingredients; pooling correlation (observational) studies combines their confounding instead of removing it, so it ranks below a single well-conducted RCT. Only a review or meta-analysis of RCTs sits above the RCT."}}, {"q": "The mean temperature of 25 patients is 100°F and the Standard deviation 0.5. Calculate the standard error of the mean:", "concept": "The answer is c: the standard error of the mean is 0.1. The standard error of the mean (SE or SEM) tells us how much a sample mean would vary if we drew many samples of the same size from the same population. The standard deviation (SD) describes the spread of individual scores; the standard error describes the spread of sample means, which is always smaller because averaging cancels out individual extremes. The formula is SE = SD / square root of n. Working: n = 25, so the square root of n is 5. SD = 0.5. SE = 0.5 / 5 = 0.1. The mean temperature itself (100°F) is not needed for the calculation; it is there to distract. The SE is used to build confidence intervals: the 95 per cent confidence interval is the mean plus or minus 1.96 SE, here 100 plus or minus about 0.2, so roughly 99.8 to 100.2°F. Example: Meera measures the temperature of 25 patients in one ward. If she measured 100 patients instead, the SE would fall to 0.5 / 10 = 0.05; quadrupling the sample only halves the error. Larger samples give more precise estimates of the population mean, but with diminishing returns. Option c is right because dividing the SD by the square root of the sample size gives exactly 0.1. Exam trap: dividing by n instead of by its square root gives 0.02 (option a), the most common slip. Remember too that the SE can never be larger than the SD when n is more than 1, which rules out option d at a glance.", "traps": {"a": "0.02 comes from dividing the SD by n (0.5 / 25) instead of by the square root of n; it is the classic formula error.", "b": "0.05 would be the SE if the sample were 100 (0.5 / 10); with 25 patients the divisor is 5, not 10.", "d": "2.0 is larger than the SD itself, which is impossible for a standard error when n is more than 1; it comes from inverting the numbers (1 / 0.5) and has no meaning here."}}, {"q": "Cognitive behaviour therapy was first developed by", "concept": "The answer is a: cognitive behaviour therapy was first developed by Aaron Beck. Aaron Temkin Beck (1921 to 2021), an American psychiatrist at the University of Pennsylvania who trained in psychoanalysis at the Philadelphia Psychoanalytic Institute, set out in the late 1950s to test Sigmund Freud's (1917) idea that depression is anger turned inward. Instead he found that his depressed patients had a stream of quick, automatic negative thoughts about themselves, their world and their future: the cognitive triad. He described this cognitive model in Depression (1967) and turned it into a structured, short-term therapy, set out in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979). In Beck's model, early experiences create core beliefs and schemas ('I am unlovable'); in stressful situations these produce automatic thoughts shaped by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, catastrophising and all-or-nothing thinking. Therapy teaches the patient to catch these thoughts, test them against evidence and replace them with balanced ones, using homework, thought records and behavioural experiments. Joined with behaviour therapy techniques, this became what is now called CBT, the most researched psychotherapy. Example: Kabir fails one test and thinks 'I am a failure; I will never get a job'. His therapist asks him to list the evidence for and against that thought and to rate his belief in it before and after. Option a is right because Beck founded cognitive therapy, the core of CBT. Exam trap: Albert Ellis (1955) developed rational emotive behaviour therapy slightly earlier and is a co-pioneer of the cognitive approach; if Ellis and Beck both appear, read the stem carefully, but when only Beck is offered, Beck is the answer.", "traps": {"b": "Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) founded psychoanalysis, which works through free association, dream analysis and the interpretation of unconscious conflict; Beck developed cognitive therapy partly as a reaction against it.", "c": "'Erickson' points either to Milton Erickson (1901 to 1980), known for indirect hypnotherapy and strategic therapy, or to Erik Erikson (1950), who proposed the eight psychosocial stages; neither developed CBT.", "d": "Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) studied cognitive development in children (sensorimotor to formal operational stages); 'cognitive' in his work refers to how thinking develops, not to a therapy."}}, {"q": "The symptom that is more suggestive of Mania than Severe depression is:", "concept": "The answer is a: overconfidence points to mania rather than severe depression. A manic episode in DSM-5-TR is a distinct period, lasting at least one week (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed), of abnormally elevated, expansive or irritable mood with increased energy or activity. Its symptoms include inflated self-esteem or grandiosity, decreased need for sleep, pressure of speech, flight of ideas, distractibility, increased goal-directed activity and risky behaviour such as spending sprees. Overconfidence is the everyday face of inflated self-esteem: the patient believes he can do anything, has special talents or will soon make a fortune. Severe depression shows the opposite pole: low self-esteem, guilt, worthlessness, hopelessness, slowed movement and thought, poor appetite and weight loss. Example: Arjun, a 24-year-old clerk, has slept three hours a night for a week, tells everyone he will soon be India's richest film producer, and has taken a large loan to fund the film. His overconfidence and grandiose plans mark a manic episode. Psychotic features can appear at both poles, so their presence does not separate the two; their content does. Manic delusions are usually grandiose (special powers, a mission), while depressive delusions are of guilt, poverty, illness or nihilism. Option a is right because inflated self-esteem is a core criterion of mania and runs counter to the self-blame of depression. Exam trap: persecutory delusions sound dramatic and so feel 'manic', but they occur in severe depression too (the patient may feel he deserves to be punished) and in schizophrenia, so they do not discriminate between the two mood states.", "traps": {"b": "Persecutory delusions can occur in mania, in psychotic depression (often as deserved punishment) and in schizophrenia, so they do not point to mania rather than depression.", "c": "Weight loss with poor appetite is a DSM-5-TR criterion for a major depressive episode; a manic patient may lose weight through overactivity, but it is not a distinguishing sign of mania.", "d": "Psychomotor retardation (slowed speech, thought and movement) is a classic sign of severe, often melancholic, depression; mania shows the opposite, agitation and increased activity."}}, {"q": "An accident victim loses his right hand and this results in his being unemployed. This situation is best explained by the term", "concept": "The answer is a: losing his job because of the lost hand is a handicap. The World Health Organization's International Classification of Impairments, Disabilities and Handicaps (ICIDH, 1980) described the consequences of disease or injury in three linked steps. Impairment is any loss or abnormality of body structure or function, at the level of the body: here, the loss of the right hand. Disability is any restriction or lack of ability to perform an activity in the normal way, at the level of the person: he cannot write, lift or use tools with that hand. Handicap is the disadvantage the person then faces in society, which stops him filling a role that is normal for his age, sex and culture: he loses his job and income. The chain runs disease, then impairment, then disability, then handicap. In 2001 the WHO replaced the ICIDH with the International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health (ICF), which renamed these levels body functions and structures, activity limitations and participation restrictions, and stressed that the environment (ramps, adapted jobs) decides how far an impairment disadvantages someone. Example: Meera, a tailor, loses the sight of one eye after an injury (impairment), cannot thread needles quickly (disability) and loses her customers (handicap). Option a is right because the stem ends in unemployment, a social disadvantage, the defining mark of handicap. Exam trap: examiners offer the first link in the chain (the lost hand, impairment) and the second (cannot do tasks with it, disability); always look at where the stem ends. Loss of a body part is impairment; loss of a social role is handicap.", "traps": {"b": "Disability (ICIDH, 1980) is the restriction in performing activities, such as being unable to write or grip; it sits between impairment and handicap, but the stem goes further, to the social result of job loss.", "c": "Impairment is the loss or abnormality at the body level, here the missing right hand itself; it does not by itself describe the unemployment that followed.", "d": "Illness is the person's subjective experience of being unwell (as opposed to disease, the objective pathology); it is not a term in the WHO impairment, disability and handicap sequence."}}, {"q": "In Psychological Rehabilitation, the main aim is to", "concept": "The answer is c: psychological rehabilitation aims to restore personal confidence. Rehabilitation is the combined use of medical, social, educational and vocational measures to train or retrain a person to the highest possible level of functional ability after disease or injury. It is the main work of tertiary prevention, once disability has already set in, and it aims at function and a valued life rather than cure. The community-medicine textbook of K. Park (Preventive and Social Medicine) divides it into four areas, each with its own aim. Medical rehabilitation restores function (for example, physiotherapy and an artificial limb). Vocational rehabilitation restores the capacity to earn a livelihood (job training, sheltered workshops, supported employment). Social rehabilitation restores family and social relationships. Psychological rehabilitation restores personal dignity and confidence, helping the person accept the change and regain self-esteem and a sense of worth. Example: Kabir, a 30-year-old driver, loses a leg in a road accident. Surgery and a prosthesis are medical rehabilitation; training as a mobile phone repair technician is vocational; helping his family and friends include him again is social; counselling that helps him stop seeing himself as a burden and believe in himself again is psychological. The four overlap in practice, but each has one defining aim. Option c is right because confidence and dignity are inner, psychological states, the specific target of psychological rehabilitation. Exam trap: all four options sound like good things a rehabilitation team does, so students choose the broadest. Match each aim to its named type instead: earning goes with vocational, relationships with social, confidence with psychological.", "traps": {"a": "Restoring family and social relationships is the aim of social rehabilitation, not psychological rehabilitation.", "b": "Financial support, such as a disability pension or allowance, is a welfare measure that may accompany rehabilitation; it is not the defining aim of psychological rehabilitation.", "d": "Restoring the capacity to earn a livelihood is the aim of vocational rehabilitation (job training, sheltered workshops, supported employment)."}}, {"q": "The fluctuation of a disease trend that occurs over a long period of time (years) is defined by:", "concept": "The answer is c: a change in disease frequency over a long period, years or decades, is a secular trend. Epidemiologists describe how disease occurrence varies with time in a few standard patterns. A secular trend is a slow, consistent rise or fall over many years: the long decline of tuberculosis in Europe even before effective drugs, the rise of coronary heart disease, diabetes and obesity in India over recent decades, or the fall in the age at menarche over the last century. Cyclic trends are rises and falls that recur at intervals of a few years, often because the pool of susceptible people builds up and is then exhausted; measles epidemics every two to three years before mass vaccination are the classic example. Seasonal trends repeat within the year, linked to climate or vectors: dengue and malaria peaking during and after the monsoon. Short-term fluctuations, such as epidemics, form a further category. Example: Riya plots district data on hypertension from 1990 to 2020 and sees a steady climb across thirty years; that is a secular trend. If she plotted monthly dengue cases instead, she would see a peak every September or October, a seasonal trend. Option c is right because 'secular' means belonging to an age or long period, which matches 'over a long period of time (years)'. Exam trap: 'propagated' sounds like a time pattern but belongs to the types of epidemic. A propagated epidemic spreads from person to person in successive waves, unlike a common-source epidemic that comes from one shared source such as contaminated water.", "traps": {"a": "A seasonal trend repeats within each year, tied to weather or vectors, such as dengue after the monsoon; it is a short, yearly pattern, not a change over many years.", "b": "A cyclic trend is a rise and fall that recurs every few years, such as measles epidemics every two to three years before vaccination; it repeats rather than moving steadily in one direction.", "d": "'Propagated' describes a type of epidemic that spreads person to person in successive generations of cases (in contrast to a common-source epidemic); it is not a time trend."}}, {"q": "Confounding Bias in a case-control scientific study can be removed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: confounding in a case-control study can be removed by matching. A confounder is a third variable that is linked to the exposure and is itself a cause of the outcome, so it can create a false association or hide a true one. The classic textbook example: alcohol drinkers seem to get more lung cancer, but drinkers also smoke more, and smoking causes lung cancer, so smoking confounds the alcohol and lung cancer link. In an experiment, randomisation spreads confounders evenly, but a case-control study cannot randomise because the outcome has already happened. Matching is the main remedy at the design stage: for each case, controls are chosen who are similar on the suspected confounder (age, sex, smoking, social class), so that it cannot differ between the groups. Matching can be individual (pair matching, one or more controls per case) or by group (frequency matching). At the analysis stage, confounding can also be handled by stratification and multivariable analysis. Example: Dr Arjun studies whether chewing tobacco causes oral cancer. Because both are commoner in older men, he matches each patient with a control of the same age and sex, so any remaining difference in tobacco use cannot be due to age or sex. A caution: do not match on the exposure itself or on something closely tied to it (overmatching), or the real association is hidden. Option a is right because matching makes cases and controls equal on the confounder, removing its influence. Exam trap: the other options fix different biases. Accurate recall tackles recall bias, an information bias; spreading the study across hospitals tackles selection bias. Neither removes confounding.", "traps": {"b": "Purposive sampling is a non-probability method in which the researcher hand-picks participants; it can introduce selection bias and does nothing to equalise confounders between cases and controls.", "c": "Accurate recall reduces recall bias, an information bias common in case-control studies because cases search their memories harder than controls do; it does not deal with confounding.", "d": "Running the study in several hospitals helps with hospital-admission selection bias (described by Joseph Berkson, 1946) and with generalisability, but cases and controls can still differ on the confounder in every hospital."}}, {"q": "A study on the experiences of 100 slum-dwelling post-partum women in the first 4 weeks after delivery of live-born baby by use of a questionnaire is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is c: a study of the experiences of slum-dwelling post-partum women is best classed as a qualitative study. Qualitative research asks what people experience and what it means to them, rather than measuring how much or how often something happens. It collects words, stories and observations through in-depth interviews, focus groups, open-ended questionnaires and participant observation, and analyses them for recurring themes. Its roots lie in phenomenology, the study of lived experience associated with Edmund Husserl (1913), and in later traditions such as grounded theory (Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, 1967) and ethnography. Samples are usually purposive, chosen because they have lived through the experience, and findings are judged by trustworthiness rather than statistical significance. Quantitative research, by contrast, counts, measures and tests hypotheses with numbers. Example: Riya, a health psychologist, asks 100 new mothers in a Mumbai slum open questions during their first four weeks after birth: how they feel, who helps them, what worries them about feeding and money. She codes the answers into themes such as isolation, family support and fear for the baby's health. Option c is right because the stem's focus on 'experiences' signals qualitative inquiry, and nothing in it involves an intervention or the follow-up of exposed and unexposed groups. Exam trap: the 4-week time frame tempts students towards a cohort study, but a cohort compares the incidence of an outcome between exposed and unexposed groups; here there is no exposure comparison at all. Key note: a questionnaire given to 100 women is, strictly, closer to a descriptive cross-sectional survey; but among the options the study of lived experiences is the qualitative one, and the other three need an intervention or the follow-up of an exposure, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "An experimental study needs the researcher to manipulate an independent variable under controlled conditions, usually with random assignment; nothing is manipulated here, the women are simply asked about their experiences.", "b": "A cohort study follows disease-free people grouped by an exposure and compares the incidence of an outcome; the stem has no exposure groups and no outcome being counted, only a description of experiences.", "d": "An interventional study (such as a clinical or community trial) delivers a treatment or programme and measures its effect; no intervention is given to these mothers."}}, {"q": "The first experimental psychology laboratory was established in:", "concept": "The answer is b: the first experimental psychology laboratory was established at the University of Leipzig, Germany. Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920), a physician and physiologist who held a chair of philosophy, set it up in 1879, the year usually given as the birth of psychology as an independent science. It began in a small room where Wundt had stored the apparatus he used in lecture demonstrations; by 1879 students there were running original experiments, and the first dissertation from it was Max Friedrich's study of the duration of apperception. Wundt used controlled introspection by trained observers, together with reaction-time instruments such as the Hipp chronoscope, to study sensations, feelings and attention. He also founded the first psychology journal, Philosophische Studien (1881), and trained many pioneers, including Edward Titchener (1867 to 1927), James McKeen Cattell (1860 to 1944) and G. Stanley Hall (1844 to 1924), who carried the laboratory model to Britain and America. Titchener later systematised the approach as structuralism, which aimed to break conscious experience into its basic elements. Example: if Arjun is asked in an interview why 1879 matters, the answer is that a laboratory devoted to psychological experiments, rather than to physiology or philosophy, first opened in Leipzig that year. Option b is right because Leipzig housed the first formal laboratory built for psychological research. Exam trap: William James (1875) had a small demonstration room at Harvard even earlier, but it was for teaching, not research; and Hall's 1883 laboratory at Johns Hopkins was the first in America, not the first in the world.", "traps": {"a": "Johns Hopkins University is where G. Stanley Hall opened the first psychology research laboratory in America, in 1883, four years after Leipzig.", "c": "King's College, London, is not linked with the first laboratory; Britain's early landmark was Francis Galton's anthropometric laboratory (1884) in London, which measured physical and sensory abilities, and British university psychology laboratories came only in the 1890s.", "d": "The Sorbonne in Paris gained France's first psychology laboratory in 1889 under Henri Beaunis, later directed by Alfred Binet; it came a decade after Leipzig."}}, {"q": "The part of the brain that is involved in speech production is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: Broca's area is the part of the brain involved in speech production. It lies in the left inferior frontal gyrus (Brodmann areas 44 and 45), just in front of the part of the motor cortex that controls the face, tongue and larynx. Paul Broca (1861) described a patient, Louis Victor Leborgne, nicknamed 'Tan' because it was almost the only syllable he could say. He understood speech but could not produce it, and at autopsy Broca found a lesion in the left frontal lobe. After more cases he concluded (1865) that 'we speak with the left hemisphere', the first strong evidence for the localisation and lateralisation of language. Damage to the area produces Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia: slow, effortful, telegraphic speech that drops small grammatical words ('Want... water... now'), with comprehension relatively preserved. Patients are aware of their errors and frustrated by them. Example: after a left frontal stroke, Kabir understands his doctor's questions but can answer only in halting two-word phrases. Option a is right because Broca's area plans the articulation and grammar of spoken language. Exam trap: Wernicke's area is its partner and the commonest distractor. Wernicke's area serves comprehension; damage gives fluent but meaningless speech and poor understanding. Memory aid: Broca means broken speech, Wernicke means wordy nonsense. The arcuate fasciculus links the two, and damage to it causes conduction aphasia, in which repetition is especially poor.", "traps": {"b": "Wernicke's area, described by Carl Wernicke (1874), lies in the posterior superior temporal gyrus and handles language comprehension; damage gives fluent but meaningless speech, not a loss of speech production.", "c": "The foramen of Monro (the interventricular foramen, named after Alexander Monro secundus, 1783) is a channel through which cerebrospinal fluid flows from each lateral ventricle into the third ventricle; it has no language function.", "d": "The Sylvian fissure (lateral sulcus, named after the 17th-century anatomist Franciscus Sylvius) is a deep groove separating the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes; the language areas lie near it, but the fissure itself produces nothing."}}, {"q": "The following system is responsible for the \"fight or flight\" response:", "concept": "The answer is b: the sympathetic nervous system is responsible for the fight or flight response. The autonomic nervous system, which runs the internal organs without conscious control, has two branches that usually work in opposition. The sympathetic branch mobilises the body for emergencies; Walter Cannon (1915), in Bodily Changes in Pain, Hunger, Fear and Rage, called this the 'fight or flight' reaction. When danger is detected, sympathetic nerves, which leave the spinal cord from its thoracic and lumbar segments, raise heart rate and blood pressure, dilate the pupils and the airways, release glucose from the liver, divert blood from the gut and skin to the muscles, slow digestion and increase sweating. They also stimulate the adrenal medulla to pour adrenaline and noradrenaline into the blood, which prolongs the arousal. The parasympathetic branch (cranial and sacral outflow, largely through the vagus nerve) does the opposite, 'rest and digest': it slows the heart, constricts the pupils and stimulates digestion, restoring calm once the threat passes. Example: Meera is walking home at night when a dog leaps at her; her heart pounds, her mouth goes dry and she runs faster than she thought possible. Ten minutes later, safe at home, her breathing slows as parasympathetic activity takes over. Option b is right because the sympathetic system is the effector system that actually produces the bodily changes of fight or flight. Exam trap: the limbic system, and the amygdala within it, detects threat and triggers the response through the hypothalamus, so it tempts students; but the bodily response itself is carried out by the sympathetic system.", "traps": {"a": "The limbic system (amygdala, hippocampus, hypothalamus, cingulate cortex) handles emotion and memory; the amygdala detects threat and alerts the hypothalamus, but the bodily fight or flight changes are carried out by the sympathetic nervous system.", "c": "The hippocampus, a limbic structure in the medial temporal lobe, is mainly for forming new memories and also helps switch off the stress hormone response; it does not produce fight or flight.", "d": "The parasympathetic nervous system is the 'rest and digest' branch: it slows the heart, constricts the pupils and promotes digestion, the opposite of fight or flight."}}, {"q": "If you are analysing causes of rebirth, then the field of this study is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the study of rebirth belongs to parapsychology. Parapsychology is the scientific study of claimed paranormal phenomena, events that seem to go beyond known physical and psychological explanation. Its main topics are extrasensory perception (telepathy, clairvoyance and precognition), psychokinesis (mind affecting matter), and survival research: near-death experiences, mediumship and claims of reincarnation or rebirth. Joseph Banks Rhine (1934), in Extra-Sensory Perception, made the field experimental at Duke University using Zener cards, where chance is 5 hits in 25. Research on rebirth is linked above all with the psychiatrist Ian Stevenson (1966), whose book Twenty Cases Suggestive of Reincarnation reported children, several of them in India, who claimed memories of a previous life; he tried to check their statements against the lives of people who had died. Most scientists remain sceptical because such findings have not been reliably replicated under controlled conditions. Example: Kabir, a psychology student, hears of a four-year-old girl in a village who says she was a woman from a nearby town in her past life. A study of whether her statements can be verified, or explained without rebirth, falls squarely within parapsychology. Option b is right because rebirth is a claimed paranormal phenomenon, the subject matter of parapsychology. Exam trap: precognition is a tempting distractor because it is a parapsychological term, but it names one phenomenon (knowledge of future events), not a field of study. Developmental psychology studies change across one lifespan, from conception to death, not across lives.", "traps": {"a": "Developmental psychology studies physical, cognitive and social change across a single lifespan, from conception to death; it does not investigate past lives.", "c": "Social psychology studies how the real or imagined presence of others shapes thoughts, feelings and behaviour (conformity, attitudes, prejudice); rebirth is outside its scope.", "d": "Precognition is a phenomenon, the claimed knowledge of future events and one of the three forms of extrasensory perception studied by parapsychology; it is not a field of study."}}, {"q": "The basic Laws of Gestalt Psychology include", "concept": "The answer is b: perceptual grouping is one of the basic laws of Gestalt psychology. Gestalt psychology began with Max Wertheimer's (1912) study of the phi phenomenon, the apparent movement seen when two lights flash in quick succession, and was developed with Wolfgang Kohler (1887 to 1967) and Kurt Koffka (1886 to 1941). 'Gestalt' is German for form or whole, and the school's slogan is that the whole is different from the sum of its parts. Against structuralism, which broke experience into elements, the Gestaltists argued that the brain organises sensations into meaningful wholes automatically. Wertheimer (1923) set out the laws of perceptual organisation, or grouping: proximity (things close together are seen as a group), similarity (similar items group together), closure (we fill gaps to see complete figures), continuity or good continuation (we follow smooth lines), common fate (things moving together belong together) and, over all of them, the law of Pragnanz: we see the simplest, most stable organisation possible. Figure and ground, shown by Edgar Rubin's (1915) vase that turns into two faces, is a closely related principle. Example: Riya looks at a row of dots spaced in pairs and sees 'pairs' rather than single dots; that is proximity at work. Option b is right because grouping is the core of the Gestalt laws of perception. Exam trap: every option is a real psychological term, but the other three belong elsewhere. Rationalisation is a psychoanalytic defence, and psychoanalysis and behaviourism are rival schools that the Gestalt psychologists argued against, not laws within Gestalt psychology.", "traps": {"a": "Rationalisation is an ego defence mechanism from psychoanalysis (a term introduced by Ernest Jones, 1908): giving a plausible but false reason for one's behaviour. It is not a law of perception.", "c": "Psychoanalysis is the school founded by Sigmund Freud (who coined the term in 1896), centred on unconscious conflict; it is a separate school, not a Gestalt law.", "d": "Behaviourism is the school launched by John B. Watson (1913) that studies only observable behaviour; Gestalt psychologists criticised it for ignoring how experience is organised."}}, {"q": "The following is a part of the Peripheral Nervous System:", "concept": "The answer is c: the intercostal nerves are part of the peripheral nervous system. The nervous system has two main divisions. The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord, protected by bone, the meninges and cerebrospinal fluid. The peripheral nervous system (PNS) is everything outside them: the 12 pairs of cranial nerves, the 31 pairs of spinal nerves with their branches, and the ganglia. The PNS carries sensory information in and motor commands out, and is itself divided into the somatic nervous system (skin, skeletal muscle, voluntary movement) and the autonomic nervous system (internal organs, with sympathetic and parasympathetic branches). The intercostal nerves are the front branches (anterior rami) of the thoracic spinal nerves T1 to T11, running in the spaces between the ribs. They supply the intercostal muscles used in breathing and the skin of the chest and abdominal wall. Because they lie outside the spinal cord, they belong to the PNS, mainly to its somatic division. Example: Arjun develops shingles in one band across his chest. The virus has reactivated in the dorsal root ganglion of a thoracic spinal nerve and spread along its intercostal nerve, a peripheral structure. Option c is right because it is the only named structure that lies outside the brain and spinal cord. Exam trap: the three distractors come from different levels of the brain (midbrain from the brainstem, cerebellum from the hindbrain, thalamus from the diencephalon), so students who think 'not the cerebrum, therefore peripheral' fall into them. Every part of the brain, however deep or low, is CNS.", "traps": {"a": "The midbrain (mesencephalon) is part of the brainstem, containing the tectum and the substantia nigra; as a part of the brain it belongs to the central nervous system.", "b": "The cerebellum, in the hindbrain, coordinates balance, posture and smooth movement; it is a brain structure and so part of the CNS.", "d": "The thalamus, in the diencephalon, is the relay station for most sensory information on its way to the cortex; it is a brain structure and so part of the CNS."}}, {"q": "The neurotransmitter that is decreased in anxiety disorders is:", "concept": "The answer is b: gamma aminobutyric acid (GABA) is the neurotransmitter classically described as decreased in anxiety disorders. GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. When it binds to the GABA-A receptor, a chloride channel opens, negative chloride ions flow into the neuron, and the neuron becomes less likely to fire. It is the brain's brake, balancing glutamate, the main excitatory transmitter. The GABA theory of anxiety grew from drug evidence. Benzodiazepines such as diazepam and alprazolam relieve anxiety within minutes by binding to their own site on the GABA-A receptor and making the channel open more often when GABA is present; barbiturates and alcohol also enhance GABA and also calm anxiety. Drugs that block the receptor provoke anxiety and even seizures, and imaging studies have reported fewer benzodiazepine binding sites or lower GABA levels in some patients with panic disorder. Too little GABA activity is therefore thought to leave the fear circuit, centred on the amygdala, poorly restrained. Noradrenaline from the locus coeruleus drives the alarm, and serotonin modulates it, which is why SSRIs are first-line long-term treatment. Example: Meera, who has panic disorder, is given clonazepam in the emergency room and feels calm within the hour, because the drug strengthens GABA's inhibition. Option b is right because the GABA-benzodiazepine system is the classic anxiety system, and reduced GABA activity is its standard explanation. Exam trap: serotonin is famous from depression, and SSRIs are used in anxiety, so students choose it; but the textbook 'decreased in anxiety' transmitter is GABA. Decreased acetylcholine belongs to Alzheimer's disease.", "traps": {"a": "Glutamate is the brain's main excitatory transmitter; anxiety is linked with too much excitation, so less glutamate would calm rather than cause it. Glutamate excess is tied to excitotoxicity and seizures.", "c": "Serotonin modulates anxiety and SSRIs treat it in the long term, but low serotonin is the classic monoamine theory of depression; the transmitter named as decreased in anxiety is GABA.", "d": "Acetylcholine works at the neuromuscular junction, in the parasympathetic system and in memory; its loss is the hallmark of Alzheimer's disease (the basis of cholinesterase inhibitors), not anxiety."}}, {"q": "Hypercalcaemic tetany can occur with disorder of:", "concept": "The answer is d: tetany occurs with disorder of the parathyroid glands. The parathyroids are four small glands, each about the size of a grain of rice, on the back of the thyroid in the neck. They secrete parathyroid hormone (PTH), which raises blood calcium by releasing calcium from bone, reducing its loss in urine and, through activated vitamin D, increasing its absorption from the gut. Calcium stabilises nerve membranes. When blood calcium falls, nerves become hyperexcitable and fire spontaneously, causing tetany: tingling around the mouth and in the fingers, muscle cramps, spasm of the hands and feet (carpopedal spasm) and, in severe cases, spasm of the larynx and seizures. Two bedside signs reveal it: Chvostek's sign (tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear makes the face twitch) and Trousseau's sign (inflating a blood pressure cuff on the arm produces spasm of the wrist and hand). The classic cause is hypoparathyroidism, often after the parathyroids are damaged or removed by accident during thyroid surgery. Low calcium can also cause anxiety, irritability and depression. Example: Riya develops tingling fingers and hand cramps two days after a thyroidectomy; her calcium is low because her parathyroids were injured. Option d is right because parathyroid hormone controls blood calcium, and its lack produces tetany. Exam trap: the thyroid tempts because the trouble often follows thyroid surgery, but it is the neighbouring parathyroid glands that matter. Key note: the stem's 'hypercalcaemic' is an error in the paper; tetany is caused by hypocalcaemia, while high calcium instead causes weakness, kidney stones, bone pain and confusion. Parathyroid hormone controls blood calcium either way, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "The pituitary, the 'master gland', controls other glands through hormones such as growth hormone, ACTH and TSH; its disorders cause gigantism, acromegaly or Cushing's disease, and it does not regulate calcium.", "b": "The adrenal glands make cortisol, aldosterone, adrenaline and noradrenaline; their classic disorders are Cushing's syndrome, Addison's disease and phaeochromocytoma, and they do not regulate blood calcium, so they are not the textbook cause of tetany.", "c": "The thyroid makes thyroxine, which sets metabolic rate, and calcitonin, which lowers calcium only weakly; tetany after thyroid surgery is actually due to accidental damage to the parathyroid glands beside it."}}, {"q": "The following is a correct term in substance use disorder regarding Psychological Dependence:", "concept": "The answer is b: craving is the term that belongs to psychological dependence. Dependence on a substance has two faces. Physical (physiological) dependence means the body has adapted to the drug, shown by tolerance (needing more to get the same effect) and withdrawal (unpleasant symptoms, often the opposite of the drug's effect, when it is stopped). Psychological dependence is a compelling need to use the substance for its pleasure or to relieve distress, shown above all by craving: an intense, often overwhelming desire to use. Craving is often set off by cues linked with past use, such as a place, a friend or a time of day, through classical conditioning. The ICD-10 dependence syndrome lists 'a strong desire or sense of compulsion to take the substance' as a criterion, and DSM-5 (2013) added craving as a criterion of substance use disorder while dropping legal problems. Psychological dependence can exist without much physical dependence; cocaine, for example, produces intense craving with relatively mild bodily withdrawal. Example: Arjun has not smoked for a month and no longer has physical withdrawal, yet whenever he drinks tea after lunch he feels a powerful urge to light a cigarette. That urge is craving, the mark of psychological dependence and a major cause of relapse. Option b is right because craving is a mental desire, not a bodily adaptation. Exam trap: tolerance and withdrawal are the best-known dependence terms, so students choose them, but both are signs of physical dependence. Kindling is a neurobiological sensitisation idea, not a dependence term.", "traps": {"a": "Kindling, first shown by Graham Goddard (1967) in rats, is the process by which repeated small stimuli make the brain progressively more excitable; in addiction it explains why alcohol withdrawal grows worse, with seizures, after repeated detoxifications, and Robert Post (1992) applied it to recurrent mood episodes. It is not a term for psychological dependence.", "c": "Withdrawal symptoms, the unpleasant bodily reaction when a drug is stopped (such as tremor, sweating and seizures in alcohol withdrawal), are a sign of physical dependence.", "d": "Tolerance, needing increasing doses to get the same effect, reflects the body's adaptation to the drug and is a sign of physical dependence."}}, {"q": "Identify the correct statement", "concept": "The answer is b: external stimuli are absent in a hallucination. Perception normally begins with a real stimulus: light, sound or touch reaches a sense organ and the brain interprets it. Descriptive psychopathology sorts abnormal perceptions by whether such a stimulus is present. An illusion is a misinterpretation of a real external stimulus. At dusk Meera sees a coiled rope on the path and takes it for a snake; the rope exists, and only her reading of it is wrong. Illusions are common in healthy people, especially when light is dim, attention is poor or emotion is high. Karl Jaspers (1913) described three kinds: completion illusions (reading a misprint as the correct word), affect illusions (a frightened person sees a coat on a hook as an intruder) and pareidolia (faces seen in clouds). A hallucination, by contrast, is a perception without an adequate external stimulus, experienced as real and located in outer space, like Kabir hearing a voice call his name in an empty room. Auditory hallucinations are commonest in schizophrenia, visual ones suggest delirium or drugs, and olfactory ones temporal lobe epilepsy. In healthy people hallucinations are uncommon; the main normal exceptions are brief hypnagogic (falling asleep) and hypnopompic (waking) experiences. Option b is right because the absence of any real stimulus is the defining feature that separates a hallucination from an illusion. Exam trap: examiners simply swap the two definitions, so option c (stimulus absent in illusion) reverses the rule; option d stretches the fact that hypnagogic hallucinations are normal into the false claim that hallucinations are common.", "traps": {"a": "False: illusions occur in nearly everyone, from the rope seen as a snake at dusk to geometric figures like the Müller-Lyer; they are not a sign of illness.", "c": "This reverses the definitions: an illusion always needs a real stimulus that is misread, while the absence of a stimulus defines a hallucination.", "d": "Only hypnagogic and hypnopompic hallucinations (and some bereavement experiences) are normal; in general hallucinations are uncommon in healthy people and point to psychosis, delirium or drugs."}}, {"q": "The process of learning involving \"Trial and Error\" was based on work of:", "concept": "The answer is d: trial-and-error learning comes from the work of Edward Thorndike (1898) on cats in puzzle boxes. Thorndike, an American psychologist at Columbia University, shut a hungry cat inside a wooden box closed by a latch, with food in sight outside. The cat clawed, pushed and meowed at random until, by accident, it pressed the lever and escaped. Over repeated trials the useless movements dropped out and the correct one came sooner, so escape time fell gradually rather than suddenly. Thorndike concluded that the cat did not reason its way out; it stamped in the response that worked and stamped out those that failed. He reported this in Animal Intelligence (1898) and stated the law of effect, formally named in 1911: a response followed by a satisfying state is strengthened, and one followed by discomfort is weakened. He added the law of exercise (practice strengthens a bond) and the law of readiness. Because his theory explains learning as stimulus-response bonds, it is called connectionism. An everyday case: Arjun, given a new phone with no instructions, presses buttons until one combination opens the camera, and next time goes straight to it. Option d is right because Thorndike's puzzle-box studies are the origin of the trial-and-error account of learning, and his law of effect later became the base of operant conditioning. Exam trap: B. F. Skinner (1938) is the popular wrong pick because he built directly on the law of effect, but his work concerned reinforcement and schedules in the Skinner box; Wolfgang Köhler (1887 to 1967), by contrast, described sudden insight in chimpanzees, the opposite of gradual trial and error.", "traps": {"a": "Benjamin Bloom (1956) built the taxonomy of educational objectives and later mastery learning; he did not study animal learning or trial and error.", "b": "Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936) studied classical conditioning, in which a neutral stimulus paired with food comes to elicit salivation; the animal's own trial-and-error responses play no part.", "c": "B. F. Skinner (1938) developed operant conditioning and reinforcement schedules in the Skinner box, extending Thorndike's law of effect; he did not originate trial-and-error learning."}}, {"q": "In Pavlov's classical conditioning, the ringing of the bell is:", "concept": "The answer is b: in the classical conditioning experiments of Ivan Pavlov (1849 to 1936), the ringing bell becomes the conditioned stimulus. Pavlov, a Russian physiologist who won the 1904 Nobel Prize for work on digestion, noticed in the late 1890s that his dogs salivated before food reached them, at the sight of the dish or the sound of the attendant. He turned this into a method. Classical conditioning has four terms. The unconditioned stimulus (UCS) is food, which naturally triggers the unconditioned response (UCR), salivation, with no learning. A neutral stimulus, such as a bell, at first produces no salivation, only an orienting response (the dog pricks up its ears). During acquisition the bell is sounded just before the food on repeated trials. After enough pairings the bell alone makes the dog salivate. The bell is now the conditioned stimulus (CS), and salivation to it is the conditioned response (CR), usually a little weaker than the UCR. Timing matters: forward (delay) conditioning, with the bell slightly before the food, works best, while backward conditioning barely works at all. Example: Riya's phone plays a tone whenever her mother messages that lunch is ready; after a few weeks the tone alone makes her hungry. Option b is right because the question asks the bell's role in the conditioning, and the whole point of the procedure is that a once-neutral bell comes to elicit salivation as a conditioned stimulus. Exam trap: option c (neutral stimulus) is true only before conditioning; once the bell has been paired with food it is no longer neutral.", "traps": {"a": "The unconditioned stimulus is the food (meat powder), which produces salivation without any learning; the bell has no natural power to do that.", "c": "The bell is a neutral stimulus only before pairing; in the conditioning itself it has become the conditioned stimulus.", "d": "'Negative conditioning' is not a term in Pavlov's scheme; it vaguely suggests aversive conditioning or the operant idea of negative reinforcement, neither of which describes the bell."}}, {"q": "In a picture of a room, two lines have a similar length in reality BUT appear to have different lengths to the observer, it is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: two lines that are physically equal but look unequal in length is the Müller-Lyer illusion. Franz Carl Müller-Lyer (1889), a German psychiatrist, drew two shafts of the same length. One ends in outward-pointing fins and looks longer; the other ends in inward-pointing arrowheads and looks shorter. Measuring them with a ruler does not remove the effect. The stem's 'picture of a room' points to the best-known explanation. Richard Gregory (1963) argued that the figures copy corners: the arrowheads resemble the near, outside corner of a building jutting towards you, while the fins resemble the far, inside corner of a room receding away. The brain applies size constancy, enlarging whatever it judges to be farther away, so the 'far' corner line is seen as longer. This is called inappropriate constancy scaling. Cross-cultural work by Marshall Segall, Donald Campbell and Melville Herskovits (1966) supports it: people raised in a 'carpentered world' of rectangular rooms are more susceptible than people who grow up in round huts. An illusion is a misperception of a real stimulus; the receptors register the lines correctly and the error lies in interpretation, so illusions distort perception, not sensation. Example: Kabir measures the two vertical lines in a drawing of a room corner, finds both are 5 cm, and still sees one as longer. Option c is right because the Müller-Lyer figure is the classic illusion of equal lengths seen as unequal. Exam trap: the Ponzo illusion (Mario Ponzo, 1911) also makes equal lines look unequal, using converging railway tracks, but it is not offered; Poggendorff is offered, and it distorts the alignment of a diagonal line, not its length.", "traps": {"a": "A visual hallucination is seeing something with no external stimulus at all; here the lines are real and only misjudged, which makes it an illusion.", "b": "The Poggendorff illusion (Johann Poggendorff, 1860) makes a straight diagonal line passing behind a rectangle look broken and misaligned; it is about alignment, not length.", "d": "A refractive error (myopia, hypermetropia, astigmatism) is an optical fault of the eye that blurs the image; it is a sensory problem and would not make equal lines look unequal to everyone."}}, {"q": "You shift to a new neighbourhood near an Air Force Base. You meet your new neighbour, and talk to him for some time. When you return your wife asks you, \"What does he do for a living?\" You had not asked his profession. You say, \"He is in the Air Force\". Your interpretation has been based on:", "concept": "The answer is b: guessing that the neighbour is in the Air Force, without asking, is a judgement based on a heuristic. A heuristic is a mental shortcut or rule of thumb that gives a quick, usually good-enough answer without weighing all the evidence. Herbert Simon (1955) argued that people have bounded rationality: limited time, information and memory, so they 'satisfice', settling for an answer that is good enough rather than the best. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1974) then described the main heuristics of judgement and the biases they cause. The representativeness heuristic judges probability by how closely a case resembles a typical member of a category: he lives near the base, so he 'looks like' an Air Force man. The availability heuristic judges likelihood by how easily examples come to mind: near a base, Air Force personnel are the people you picture first. Both fit the stem. The cost is bias. Representativeness ignores base rates, since many people living near a base are teachers, shopkeepers or doctors. Kahneman later placed heuristics in fast, intuitive System 1 thinking, as opposed to slow, deliberate System 2. Example: Meera sees a man in a white coat in a hospital corridor and assumes he is a doctor, though he might be a laboratory technician. Option b is right because the answer came from a rapid shortcut based on resemblance and setting, with no evidence collected and no alternatives considered. Exam trap: hypothesis generation sounds right because a guess is a kind of hypothesis, but in psychology it means deliberately proposing a testable explanation that is then checked, which is exactly what did not happen here.", "traps": {"a": "Rational thinking means drawing conclusions logically from evidence; you had no evidence about his job, so the answer was not rationally derived.", "c": "Critical thinking means questioning assumptions and evaluating evidence (for instance asking what else he might do); it is the very process the snap guess skipped.", "d": "Hypothesis generation is the deliberate first step of the scientific method, proposing a testable explanation to be checked; here the guess was stated as fact and never tested."}}, {"q": "The following is a part of a neuron:", "concept": "The answer is b: the axon is a part of a neuron. A neuron is a nerve cell that receives, integrates and transmits information by electrical and chemical signals. Its main parts are the dendrites, branched fibres that receive messages from other cells; the cell body or soma, which holds the nucleus and keeps the cell alive; the axon hillock, where the action potential is triggered; the axon, a single long fibre that carries the impulse away from the cell body, sometimes for a metre or more; and the axon terminals (terminal buttons), which release neurotransmitter into the synapse. Many axons are wrapped in a fatty myelin sheath, made by oligodendrocytes in the brain and spinal cord and by Schwann cells in peripheral nerves. The small gaps between myelin segments, the nodes of Ranvier, let the impulse jump from node to node (saltatory conduction), which makes it much faster. Santiago Ramón y Cajal (1888 onward) showed that neurons are separate cells, the neuron doctrine, and shared the 1906 Nobel Prize with Camillo Golgi. The direction of flow is easy to remember: dendrite, soma, axon, terminal. When Arjun touches a hot pan, sensory neurons carry the signal along their axons to the spinal cord, and motor neuron axons carry the command back to pull his hand away. Option b is right because the axon is the output fibre that is part of every typical neuron, while the other three options are all parts of muscle. Exam trap: all three distractors come from muscle physiology, and sarcolemma tempts students who know it is a cell membrane; but it is the membrane of a muscle fibre, whereas the axon's own membrane is the axolemma.", "traps": {"a": "The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of a muscle fibre, which carries the muscle action potential; it is not part of a neuron.", "c": "Myoglobin is an oxygen-storing protein found in muscle cells (it makes red muscle red); neurons do not contain it as a structural part.", "d": "Intercalated discs are the specialised junctions that join cardiac muscle cells so the heart contracts as one unit; they belong to heart muscle, not nerve."}}, {"q": "The following part of the brain is affected in Parkinson's disease:", "concept": "The answer is c: Parkinson's disease results from the loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra. James Parkinson (1817) described it as the 'shaking palsy'. Its core signs are resting tremor (a pill-rolling tremor present at rest that eases with movement), rigidity (cogwheel or lead-pipe), bradykinesia (slowness) or akinesia (difficulty starting movement), and postural instability, with a masked face, small handwriting and a shuffling gait. The substantia nigra ('black substance', dark because of the pigment neuromelanin) lies in the midbrain. Its pars compacta sends dopamine along the nigrostriatal pathway to the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen), part of the basal ganglia that help start and smooth voluntary movement. Symptoms usually appear only after roughly 60 to 80 percent of these neurons are lost. Oleh Hornykiewicz (1960) showed that the striatum of Parkinson's patients is depleted of dopamine, which led to treatment with L-dopa, the dopamine precursor that can cross the blood-brain barrier. Surviving nigral cells contain Lewy bodies, clumps of the protein alpha-synuclein. Striking support came when young drug users injected a contaminant, MPTP, that destroys nigral neurons and developed sudden severe parkinsonism (J. William Langston, 1983). Example: Kabir's grandfather has a hand tremor at rest that stops when he lifts his cup, walks with short shuffling steps and has a fixed expression. Option c is right because degeneration of the dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta is the defining pathology. Exam trap: students confuse Parkinson's with Huntington's disease, which mainly destroys the caudate nucleus of the striatum; and the thalamus tempts some because surgery for Parkinson's targets nearby nuclei, but the primary lesion is nigral.", "traps": {"a": "The thalamus is the sensory relay station of the diencephalon; thalamic lesions cause sensory loss and pain syndromes, not the dopamine loss of Parkinson's disease.", "b": "The limbic system (hippocampus, amygdala, cingulate gyrus) serves emotion and memory; its damage gives amnesia or emotional change, not resting tremor and rigidity.", "d": "The hypothalamus controls hunger, thirst, temperature and the pituitary; loss of its orexin neurons causes narcolepsy, not Parkinson's disease."}}, {"q": "Type A and Type B behaviour pattern can be measured by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Jenkins Activity Survey is the standard self-report measure of the Type A and Type B behaviour patterns. Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman (1959), two cardiologists in San Francisco, noticed that many of their heart patients were hurried, competitive and impatient. They named this the Type A behaviour pattern: competitive achievement striving, a strong sense of time urgency, and easily aroused hostility and impatience. Type B people are more relaxed and unhurried. In their Western Collaborative Group Study of about 3,000 working men, Type A men developed coronary heart disease about twice as often as Type B men; later research found hostility to be the toxic ingredient. Friedman and Rosenman first measured the pattern with the Structured Interview, in which the interviewer deliberately interrupts and challenges the person to observe loud, fast, impatient speech. Because interviews are slow and need trained raters, C. David Jenkins, working with Stephen Zyzanski and Rosenman from the mid-1960s, built the Jenkins Activity Survey (JAS), a paper-and-pencil questionnaire that gives an overall Type A score and subscales for speed and impatience, job involvement, and hard-driving competitiveness. Example: Arjun eats lunch at his desk, finishes other people's sentences and fumes in traffic; he would score high on the JAS. Option a is right because the JAS was built specifically to classify people as Type A or Type B. Exam trap: galvanic skin response sounds plausible because Type A people are physiologically reactive, but it records momentary arousal in anyone and cannot classify a stable behaviour pattern; the other recognised measure is the Structured Interview, not a brain scan or projective test.", "traps": {"b": "A CT scan of the brain images structure (bleeds, tumours, atrophy); it tells nothing about competitiveness, time urgency or hostility.", "c": "The Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) is a projective test of perception and personality organisation; it was never designed to classify Type A or Type B.", "d": "Galvanic skin response measures sweat-gland activity as an index of momentary autonomic arousal (used in lie detection and biofeedback); it is a physiological signal, not a measure of a behaviour pattern."}}, {"q": "Vygotsky's theory of the zone of proximal development indicates that children can perform at higher level by:", "concept": "The answer is b: Lev Vygotsky (1896 to 1934) held that children can perform at a higher level with the support of teachers and more capable peers. Vygotsky, a Russian psychologist, saw development as social: higher mental functions first appear between people and are then internalised. He defined the zone of proximal development (ZPD) as the distance between a child's actual developmental level, shown by independent problem solving, and the level of potential development, shown by problem solving under adult guidance or in collaboration with more capable peers. The helper is called the more knowledgeable other. For Vygotsky, good teaching works inside this zone, just ahead of what the child can do alone, so that learning leads development. This contrasts with Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980), for whom development comes first and the child learns largely alone, like a little scientist. The support given within the zone was later named scaffolding by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976): help is adjusted to the child's level and withdrawn as competence grows. The idea also led to dynamic assessment: test, teach, then retest to see how much the child gains from help. Example: Riya, aged 8, can add two-digit numbers alone; with her teacher prompting 'carry the one' and an older classmate showing the steps, she manages three-digit sums. That gap is her ZPD. Option b is right because the ZPD is defined by what the child achieves with guidance from teachers and capable peers. Exam trap: option c (parents) is tempting, since a parent can be the more knowledgeable other, but it stresses support and encouragement, that is, emotional motivation, whereas the ZPD is about skilled guidance in the task itself.", "traps": {"a": "Competitive tests measure what a child can already do alone, the actual developmental level; Vygotsky argued such static testing misses the zone that guided help reveals.", "c": "Parents can act as the more knowledgeable other, but 'support and encouragement' describes emotional motivation; the ZPD concerns skilled guidance in the task, and its classic definition names adults such as teachers and more capable peers.", "d": "Extra tuition only adds more instruction time; it says nothing about collaborative help pitched just beyond the child's independent level, and may be far above or below the zone."}}, {"q": "The parenting styles identified by Diana Baumrind include the following term:", "concept": "The answer is d: authoritative is one of the parenting styles described by Diana Baumrind (1966). Baumrind, an American developmental psychologist at Berkeley, observed preschool children at nursery school and at home and interviewed their parents. She described parenting along two broad dimensions, warmth (responsiveness) and control (demandingness), and identified three patterns. Authoritative parents are warm and responsive but also set clear, firm rules, explain the reasons for them and encourage independence; their children tend to be self-reliant, self-controlled, cheerful and achievement oriented. Authoritarian parents are cold and controlling ('because I said so'), rely on punishment and rarely explain; their children tend to be withdrawn, anxious or hostile. Permissive parents are warm but make few demands; their children tend to be impulsive and immature. Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) later split the low-control group into permissive (indulgent) and neglectful (uninvolved), giving four styles in all. Research across many cultures links the authoritative style with the best outcomes, though the advantage varies somewhat by culture. Example: Kabir's mother insists he finish his homework before cricket, explains why, listens when he argues and sometimes agrees to a change; that is authoritative parenting. Option d is right because 'authoritative' is Baumrind's own term for the warm and firm style she found most beneficial. Exam trap: 'democratic' is a near synonym that comes from the leadership studies of Kurt Lewin, Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White (1939), who compared authoritarian, democratic and laissez-faire leaders, so it sounds like a parenting style but is not Baumrind's label; examiners also swap authoritative with authoritarian.", "traps": {"a": "'Democratic' comes from Kurt Lewin, Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White's (1939) leadership styles (authoritarian, democratic, laissez-faire); it resembles authoritative but is not Baumrind's term.", "b": "'Secure' is an attachment classification from Mary Ainsworth's (1978) Strange Situation, describing the child's bond with the carer, not a parenting style.", "c": "'Protective' (or overprotective) describes a parental attitude studied by others, such as David Levy's (1943) work on maternal overprotection; it is not one of Baumrind's styles."}}, {"q": "A patient underwent a neurosurgical procedure resulting in cutting of the corpus callosum. He is made to sit in front of a TV monitor with the word PUNJAB written across the screen. On being asked what he sees, he will say", "concept": "The answer is b: a split-brain patient looking at the centre of PUNJAB will say 'JAB'. Each eye sends information from the left visual field (everything to the left of the fixation point) to the right hemisphere, and from the right visual field to the left hemisphere, because the fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross at the optic chiasm. When the patient fixates the centre of the screen, PUN falls in the left visual field and goes to the right hemisphere, while JAB falls in the right visual field and goes to the left hemisphere. In most right-handers the left hemisphere holds speech (Broca's area). Normally the corpus callosum, the largest band of fibres joining the two hemispheres, would pass PUN across, and the person would read the whole word. Once it is cut (corpus callosotomy, done to stop intractable epilepsy spreading from one side to the other), the speaking left hemisphere knows only JAB. The right hemisphere has still seen PUN: asked to point with his left hand, which the right hemisphere controls, to a card matching what he saw, he could pick PUN while saying he saw only JAB. This is the design of the split-brain studies of Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga in the 1960s, which won Sperry the Nobel Prize in 1981; real tests flash the word for a fraction of a second so the eyes cannot move. In the classic textbook demonstration with HEART, the patient says 'ART' but his left hand points to 'HE'. Option b is right because only the right-visual-field half reaches the hemisphere that can speak. Exam trap: option c (PUN) reverses the crossover; students forget that the left visual field goes to the right hemisphere, which in most people cannot talk.", "traps": {"a": "PUNJAB would be the answer with an intact corpus callosum, which lets the right hemisphere pass PUN across to the speech areas; after the cut that transfer is lost.", "c": "PUN reaches only the right hemisphere, which in most people cannot speak; the patient could point to it with his left hand but cannot say it.", "d": "UNJA is just the middle letters and has no basis in visual-field projection; the split falls at the fixation point, dividing the word into PUN and JAB."}}, {"q": "The speeding laws In Chandigarh are very strict. No one can drive over 50km/hour. Kamal drives his car so that the speed never exceeds 50km/hour so he never gets a speeding ticket. This is an example of", "concept": "The answer is b: Kamal keeps his speed under 50 km/hour so that he never gets a ticket, which is negative reinforcement. In operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner (1938) from Edward Thorndike's (1898) law of effect, a consequence either strengthens or weakens the behaviour it follows. Reinforcement always strengthens behaviour; punishment always weakens it. 'Positive' means something is added and 'negative' means something is removed or prevented. So positive reinforcement adds a pleasant event (a bonus for good work), while negative reinforcement removes or prevents an unpleasant one (a painkiller ends a headache, so taking painkillers increases). Negative reinforcement comes in two forms. In escape learning the aversive event is already present and the behaviour ends it. In avoidance learning, as here, the behaviour stops the aversive event from ever happening. Kamal's careful driving is kept up because it keeps fines and court visits away. Avoidance is very resistant to extinction, because the person never tests whether the feared event would really come; Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947) used this in his two-factor theory to explain why phobias persist. Example: Meera leaves home ten minutes early every day so that her strict professor never marks her late; her punctuality is maintained by avoidance. Option b is right because the behaviour (driving within the limit) is strengthened and maintained by avoiding an aversive consequence, the speeding ticket. Exam trap: negative reinforcement is not punishment. Punishment weakens a behaviour, whereas here the behaviour is strengthened; a ticket given after speeding would be positive punishment of speeding.", "traps": {"a": "Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant consequence, such as a reward for safe driving; Kamal gains nothing extra, he only avoids a fine.", "c": "Extinction is the weakening of a learned behaviour when its reinforcement stops; Kamal's careful driving is being maintained, not fading.", "d": "'Structured reinforcement' is not a standard term in operant conditioning; the recognised schedules are continuous, fixed or variable ratio, and fixed or variable interval."}}, {"q": "A person sees a snake, runs away and feels afraid. This sequence of stimulus, physiologic response followed by emotional response is based on the following theory of emotion:", "concept": "The answer is c: stimulus, then bodily response, then felt emotion is the James-Lange theory of emotion. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) independently proposed that an emotion is our perception of bodily changes. Common sense says we see the snake, feel afraid, and then run. James reversed the order: we see the snake, the body reacts (we run, the heart pounds, we tremble), and our awareness of these changes is the fear. In his famous phrasing, we feel sorry because we cry and afraid because we tremble, not the other way round. The theory implies that each emotion has its own pattern of bodily response. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked it: visceral changes are too slow and too similar across different emotions, and animals whose visceral feedback was cut still showed emotional behaviour. Partial modern support comes from the facial feedback hypothesis, as in the study by Fritz Strack, Leonard Martin and Sabine Stepper (1988), where holding a pen in the teeth (forcing a smile) made cartoons seem funnier. Example: Arjun's car skids on a wet road; he brakes hard, his heart races, and only a moment later does he feel the fear. Option c is right because the stem gives the exact James-Lange sequence: stimulus, then physiological response, then emotion. Exam trap: students who remember the snake example from the same textbook page often pick Cannon-Bard, but Cannon-Bard says arousal and emotion occur at the same time and independently, so there is no 'followed by'; Schachter-Singer adds a cognitive label that the stem never mentions.", "traps": {"a": "The Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon, 1927; Philip Bard, 1928) says the thalamus sends signals to the cortex and the body simultaneously, so emotion and arousal occur together, not in sequence.", "b": "The Schachter-Singer two-factor theory (Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer, 1962) says emotion needs both arousal and a cognitive label drawn from the situation; the stem has no appraisal step.", "d": "Opponent-process theory (Richard Solomon and John Corbit, 1974) explains how a primary emotion triggers an opposite after-reaction (fear then relief, a high then craving); it is about emotional aftereffects, not the stimulus-body-feeling order."}}, {"q": "A person visits the walk-in-clinic and informs the therapist that he has a fear of heights. This is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: a fear of heights is called acrophobia. Phobia names join a Greek word for the feared thing to phobos, fear; akron means peak or summit. In DSM-5-TR a specific phobia is a marked fear of a particular object or situation that is out of proportion to the real danger, almost always triggers immediate anxiety, is actively avoided or endured with intense distress, and usually lasts six months or more. Acrophobia belongs to the natural environment subtype, along with fears of storms and water; the other subtypes are animal, blood-injection-injury, situational (lifts, flying, enclosed places) and other. People with acrophobia may freeze on a balcony or ladder and avoid flyovers, hill roads or high floors. It is not the same as vertigo, a spinning sensation caused by inner-ear or brain disorders, though the two can coexist. Treatment is graded exposure, from Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation to modern in vivo and virtual reality exposure, which works especially well for heights. Example: Kabir refuses to look down from a fourth-floor window and takes a longer route to avoid a hill road, even though he knows his fear is excessive. Option a is right because acrophobia is the specific name for fear of heights. Exam trap: agoraphobia also starts with 'a' and is often confused with acrophobia, but it means fear of the marketplace, that is, of public places where escape may be hard; claustrophobia is the near-miss space fear, but for enclosed rather than high places.", "traps": {"b": "Agoraphobia (Greek agora, marketplace) is fear of situations where escape or help may be hard, such as crowds, public transport and open spaces; DSM-5 lists it as a separate anxiety disorder.", "c": "Claustrophobia is fear of enclosed spaces such as lifts, tunnels and MRI scanners, a situational specific phobia, not a fear of heights.", "d": "Scopophobia (scoptophobia) is fear of being looked at or stared at, which is related to social anxiety."}}, {"q": "A person comes to your office. He has a staggering gait, and slurring of speech. He appears like a person under influence of alcohol but on further inquiry you find out that he never drinks alcohol and is not on any other drug. He is likely to be having a disorder of the", "concept": "The answer is c: a staggering gait and slurred speech in someone who never drinks point to a disorder of the cerebellum. The cerebellum ('little brain') sits at the back of the hindbrain, behind the pons and medulla. It does not start movement; it coordinates it, comparing the intended movement with what the body is actually doing and correcting the error, so that movement is smooth, accurate and well timed. It also keeps balance and posture, working with the vestibular system, and supports motor learning, such as the practised timing of a cricket stroke. Damage produces ataxia: a broad-based, staggering, 'drunken' gait; dysarthria, slurred or scanning speech; intention tremor, which worsens as the hand nears its target; dysmetria, overshooting in the finger-nose test; dysdiadochokinesia, clumsy rapid alternating movements; and nystagmus. A lesion of one cerebellar hemisphere affects the same side of the body. Alcohol is the classic cause of temporary cerebellar dysfunction, which is why intoxication looks like this and why roadside sobriety tests (walking a straight line, touching the nose) are really cerebellar tests. Other causes include stroke, tumours, multiple sclerosis and inherited spinocerebellar ataxias. Example: Arjun, a lifelong teetotaller, is mistaken for drunk at a bus stop; a scan later shows a small cerebellar stroke. Option c is right because ataxic gait plus dysarthria is the hallmark of cerebellar disease, the same picture alcohol produces. Exam trap: the midbrain tempts students because it contains motor nuclei such as the substantia nigra, but nigral damage gives Parkinson's disease, with slowness, rigidity, tremor at rest and a shuffling rather than staggering gait.", "traps": {"a": "The thalamus relays sensation to the cortex; thalamic lesions cause sensory loss and pain syndromes, not a drunken gait with slurred speech.", "b": "The midbrain holds eye-movement nuclei and the substantia nigra; its damage tends to cause eye-movement palsies or parkinsonian features rather than cerebellar ataxia.", "d": "The occipital lobe is the visual cortex; its lesions cause visual field loss, cortical blindness or visual agnosia, not problems with gait or speech."}}, {"q": "The following is NOT a distinctive feature of Obsessive-Compulsive disorder:", "concept": "The answer is d: Munchausen syndrome is not a feature of obsessive-compulsive disorder; it is a different disorder altogether. OCD, as defined in DSM-5-TR, has two core features. Obsessions are recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images (fear of contamination, of harming someone, of things not being 'just right') that cause marked anxiety. They are ego-dystonic, felt as alien to the self, and the person tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them. Compulsions are repetitive behaviours or mental acts (washing, checking, counting, ordering, praying) that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or by rigid rules. They aim to reduce distress or prevent a dreaded event, but are not realistically connected to it or are clearly excessive. Many patients recognise that their thoughts are irrational, which DSM-5-TR records as good or fair insight. Example: Meera checks the gas knob twenty times before leaving home, knowing it is off, because the thought 'the house will burn down' will not go away. Munchausen syndrome, named by Richard Asher (1951) after the tall tales of Baron Munchausen, is the severe form of factitious disorder imposed on self: the person deliberately fakes or induces illness, tells dramatic stories and seeks repeated hospital admissions in order to take on the sick role. Option d is right because Munchausen syndrome sits in the somatic symptom and related disorders chapter of DSM-5, not in OCD. Exam trap: option a (impulsive behaviour) also looks shaky, since OCD compulsions are driven by anxiety reduction rather than impulsivity; but compulsions are loosely described as irresistible urges, while d is the clearly correct 'NOT' because it names a separate disorder.", "traps": {"a": "Impulsivity is not the true driver of OCD, since compulsions reduce anxiety rather than seek pleasure, and impulse-control disorders are a separate group; but compulsions feel like urges the person cannot resist, so the option is loosely linked to OCD, unlike d.", "b": "Ritualised behaviour is the compulsion itself (handwashing, checking, counting in a fixed order), a core feature of OCD.", "c": "Irrational thoughts are the obsessions, intrusive ideas the person usually recognises as excessive or senseless; they are central to OCD."}}, {"q": "The disorder where brain damage results in impairment of a person's ability to recognize faces is:", "concept": "The answer is a: an inability to recognise faces after brain damage is prosopagnosia, or face blindness. The German neurologist Joachim Bodamer (1947) coined the term from the Greek prosopon (face) and agnosia (not knowing). An agnosia is a failure to recognise things even though basic sensation, memory and language are intact; Heinrich Lissauer (1890) divided visual agnosia into apperceptive (no stable percept is formed) and associative (the percept is formed but not linked to meaning). In prosopagnosia vision is normal and the person can describe the eyes, nose and hair of a face, yet cannot tell whose face it is, even a spouse's or their own in a mirror. They still recognise people by voice, walk, clothes or a distinctive mole. The lesion usually involves the ventral occipitotemporal cortex on both sides or on the right, especially the fusiform gyrus, where Nancy Kanwisher (1997) identified the fusiform face area. Causes include stroke in the posterior cerebral arteries, head injury and encephalitis; a developmental form also exists without obvious brain damage. Oliver Sacks's book The Man Who Mistook His Wife for a Hat (1985) made visual agnosia widely known. Example: after a stroke Kabir's uncle walks straight past his own daughter at the station, but knows her the moment she speaks. Option a is right because prosopagnosia is the specific agnosia for faces. Exam trap: anosognosia sounds similar but means unawareness of one's own illness, classically denial of a paralysed left side after a right parietal stroke (Joseph Babinski, 1914); autotopagnosia is failure to locate one's own body parts.", "traps": {"b": "'Auto agnosia' most likely means autotopagnosia, the inability to name or point to one's own body parts (usually after left parietal damage); it concerns the body map, not faces.", "c": "Anosognosia (Joseph Babinski, 1914) is lack of awareness of one's own deficit, such as denying a paralysed left arm after a right parietal stroke.", "d": "Nominal (anomic) aphasia is a word-finding difficulty, especially for names of objects; the patient may know who a person is but cannot retrieve the name, a language problem rather than a recognition problem."}}, {"q": "According to Miller, the number of pieces of information that can be held briefly in our memory at any given time is:", "concept": "The answer is d: George Miller (1956) put the capacity of short-term memory at about seven items, plus or minus two. In his paper 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two', George Armitage Miller reviewed studies of immediate memory span and absolute judgement and found that people can hold about 5 to 9 units at once. The key word is units: Miller called them chunks, meaningful groups rather than single letters or digits. Chunking stretches capacity a great deal. Riya cannot hold twelve random letters, but I N D I A I I T I S R O becomes three chunks (INDIA, IIT, ISRO) and is easy. In the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), short-term memory holds information for only about 15 to 30 seconds without rehearsal; Lloyd Peterson and Margaret Peterson (1959) showed this rapid forgetting by blocking rehearsal with backward counting. Digit span subtests in the Wechsler scales measure this span directly. Later, Nelson Cowan (2001) argued that when rehearsal and chunking are prevented, the true limit is nearer four chunks, but seven remains the classic exam figure. Option d is right because seven is Miller's famous number for the items held briefly in short-term memory. Exam trap: option c (five) is the lower end of Miller's range (seven minus two) and is close to Cowan's modern estimate of about four, so students who remember either may pick it; the question asks for Miller's number, which is seven.", "traps": {"a": "Two is far below any estimate of short-term memory span; even the strict modern estimate is about four chunks.", "b": "Three is nearer Nelson Cowan's (2001) revised limit of about four chunks than Miller's figure, and still below both.", "c": "Five is only the lower bound of Miller's seven plus or minus two range, not his central figure."}}, {"q": "You are playing cards and somehow you begin to \"know\" which card will be dealt next accurately. You are displaying the extra-sensory perception of:", "concept": "The answer is b: 'knowing' which card will come up is classed here as clairvoyance, extrasensory perception of hidden information. Extrasensory perception (ESP) is the claimed ability to gain information without any of the known senses. Joseph Banks Rhine (1934) popularised the term in his book Extra-Sensory Perception and ran the parapsychology laboratory at Duke University. He used Zener cards, designed by his colleague Karl Zener (early 1930s): a deck of 25 cards with five symbols (circle, cross, wavy lines, square, star), so chance is 5 hits in 25. Rhine named three forms of ESP. Telepathy is mind-to-mind transfer, such as guessing the card another person is looking at. Clairvoyance is perceiving a hidden object or event directly, such as naming a face-down card no one has seen. Precognition is knowing a future event. Psychokinesis, by contrast, is not perception but influencing matter by mind; ESP and psychokinesis together are called psi. Mainstream psychology remains sceptical, because results fail to replicate and early studies suffered from sensory leakage and poor randomisation. Example: Arjun, playing cards at a Diwali party, 'senses' that the next card is the queen of hearts and is right; he forgets the many times he was wrong. Option b is right because, among the choices, clairvoyance is the form of ESP that covers knowledge of a hidden card that no other mind holds. Exam trap: telepathy needs a sender who already knows the card, and nobody knows the next card in a shuffled deck. Key note: strictly, 'knowing' which card will be dealt NEXT is precognition (knowledge of a future event), a term J. B. Rhine used alongside clairvoyance (knowledge of a present hidden object or event) and telepathy (another mind). Precognition is not offered, so b, the extra-sensory perception of hidden information, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Telepathy is mind-to-mind transmission, perceiving another person's thoughts; it needs someone who already knows the card, and nobody knows the next card in a shuffled deck.", "c": "Psychokinesis is the claimed power of mind over matter, such as influencing how dice fall; it is an action, not a perception, so it is not a form of ESP.", "d": "Pattern recognition is ordinary perception, identifying a stimulus by matching it to stored templates, features or prototypes; it uses the senses and so is not extrasensory at all."}}, {"q": "In an EEG the following waves are slowest (have the least frequency)", "concept": "The answer is c: delta waves are the slowest EEG waves, at about 0.5 to 4 cycles per second (hertz). The electroencephalogram (EEG), first recorded in humans by Hans Berger (1929), picks up the summed electrical activity of millions of cortical neurons through electrodes on the scalp. When neurons fire independently the trace is fast and low in voltage (desynchronised); when they fire together it is slow and high in voltage (synchronised). From fastest to slowest the bands are: gamma, above about 30 Hz (focused processing); beta, about 13 to 30 Hz (alert, active thinking, anxiety); alpha, about 8 to 12 Hz (relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed, strongest over the occipital lobe, and the first rhythm Berger described); theta, about 4 to 7 Hz (drowsiness, stage N1 sleep, and normal in children); and delta, below 4 Hz (deep slow-wave sleep, stage N3, and normal in infants). A simple order to remember, slow to fast, is delta, theta, alpha, beta. In an awake adult, delta waves suggest brain dysfunction such as a lesion, encephalopathy or coma. In the sleep scoring manual of Allan Rechtschaffen and Anthony Kales (1968), stages 3 and 4 were defined by the amount of delta activity; the American Academy of Sleep Medicine (2007) merged them into N3. Example: when Meera falls into deep sleep in the first third of the night, her EEG shows large, slow delta waves and she is hardest to wake. Option c is right because delta has the lowest frequency of the four bands offered. Exam trap: theta is the near miss, slow but faster than delta; and students confuse amplitude with frequency, whereas delta has the highest amplitude but the lowest frequency.", "traps": {"a": "Alpha waves (about 8 to 12 Hz) mark relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed; they sit in the middle of the range, not at the slow end.", "b": "Beta waves (about 13 to 30 Hz) are fast, low-voltage waves of alert, active thinking; they are among the fastest, the opposite of what is asked.", "d": "Theta waves (about 4 to 7 Hz) appear in drowsiness and stage N1 sleep; they are slow, but still faster than delta."}}, {"q": "A student in a hostel is observed to get up at night and move about while still asleep. This is called:", "concept": "The answer is d: getting up and moving about while still asleep is somnambulism, or sleepwalking. In DSM-5-TR sleepwalking is a type of non-rapid eye movement (NREM) sleep arousal disorder, grouped with sleep terrors. These are incomplete awakenings, usually from deep slow-wave sleep (stage N3), so they occur in the first third of the night, when N3 is most plentiful. The sleepwalker sits up, walks about or performs complex acts (opening doors, eating, even leaving the building) with open, glassy eyes and a blank face. He is hard to wake, confused if woken, and remembers little or nothing in the morning. Sleepwalking cannot arise from REM sleep, because in REM the core muscles are paralysed (atonia); acting out dreams during REM is a different disorder, REM sleep behaviour disorder. Sleepwalking is common in children, usually fades by adolescence and often runs in families. In adults it is triggered by sleep deprivation, stress, fever, alcohol and some sedative drugs. Management is mainly safety (locking doors and windows, clearing obstacles), regular sleep and avoiding triggers; scheduled awakening shortly before the usual episode time can help children. Example: Kabir, a first-year hostel student short of sleep before exams, is found by his roommate standing in the corridor at 1 a.m. and remembers nothing the next day. Option d is right because somnambulism is the term for walking or moving about during sleep. Exam trap: narcolepsy and cataplexy are listed alongside it as sleep disorders, but narcolepsy means falling asleep suddenly in the daytime, and cataplexy is a sudden loss of muscle tone while fully awake, the opposite of moving about in sleep.", "traps": {"a": "Cataplexy is a sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by strong emotion such as laughter, in full consciousness; it is REM atonia breaking into waking, part of narcolepsy, not movement during sleep.", "b": "Narcolepsy is a disorder of irresistible daytime sleep attacks with cataplexy, sleep paralysis and hypnagogic hallucinations, caused by loss of orexin (hypocretin) neurons in the hypothalamus.", "c": "Sleep apnoea is repeated stopping of breathing during sleep, usually obstructive, with loud snoring and daytime sleepiness; it is a breathing-related sleep disorder."}}, {"q": "If your mother tells you \"You must eat your vegetables before you get Ice-cream\" then she is using the:", "concept": "The answer is d: 'vegetables before ice-cream' is the Premack principle. David Premack (1959) proposed that a more probable (more preferred) activity can be used to reinforce a less probable (less preferred) one. On this view reinforcers are not things but activities, and their power is relative: whatever a person would rather do can reward whatever they would rather not do. Parents know it as 'Grandma's rule': first you work, then you play. In Premack's classic study, children who preferred eating sweets played more pinball when pinball earned them sweets, while children who preferred pinball ate more sweets when eating earned them a turn at the machine. Teachers, parents and behaviour therapists use the principle every day, in classroom management, token systems and activity schedules. Example: Riya's teacher lets the class go out for games only after they finish the arithmetic worksheet; games (high probability) reinforce worksheet completion (low probability). Option d is right because the mother makes the preferred activity, eating ice-cream, depend on first doing the less preferred one, eating vegetables. Exam trap: direction matters. A high-probability behaviour reinforces a low-probability one, never the reverse, so 'you may eat vegetables after the ice-cream' would not work. The other three options are well-known laws of management and office life; they sound like psychological principles because of their names, which is why examiners use them as distractors.", "traps": {"a": "Parkinson's law (C. Northcote Parkinson, 1955) says that work expands to fill the time available for its completion; it is a management observation, not a reinforcement principle.", "b": "The Peter principle (Laurence J. Peter, 1969) says that people in a hierarchy are promoted until they reach their level of incompetence.", "c": "The Dilbert principle (Scott Adams, 1996, from his Dilbert cartoon strip) says that the least competent employees are moved into management, where they can do the least harm."}}, {"q": "The principle of reinforcement that is applied in games of chance like gambling in a casino is:", "concept": "The answer is c: casino gambling runs on a variable ratio schedule of reinforcement. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957), in Schedules of Reinforcement, mapped how the pattern of rewards shapes behaviour. In continuous reinforcement every response is rewarded; this is best for teaching a new behaviour but extinguishes quickly once rewards stop. In partial (intermittent) reinforcement only some responses are rewarded, and there are four basic schedules. A ratio schedule depends on the number of responses made; an interval schedule depends on time passed. Fixed ratio (FR) rewards after a set number of responses, as in piece-rate pay, and gives a high rate with a short pause after each reward. Variable ratio (VR) rewards after an unpredictable number of responses that averages out to some value. A slot machine may pay on average once every 20 pulls, but the next win could come on the second pull or the fiftieth. Because any response might be the winner, VR produces the highest, steadiest rate of responding and the greatest resistance to extinction, so the gambler keeps playing through long losing runs. Fixed interval (FI) produces a scalloped pattern, a pause and then a speed-up; variable interval (VI) gives a moderate, steady rate. Example: Arjun keeps buying a lottery ticket every week although he has won only twice in three years. Option c is right because games of chance pay off after an unpredictable number of plays, which is the definition of a variable ratio schedule. Exam trap: variable interval is the near miss because it is also unpredictable, but in gambling the payoff depends on the number of bets, not on the passage of time; checking messages for a reply is the textbook VI example.", "traps": {"a": "Continuous reinforcement rewards every response; casinos do not pay on every bet, and behaviour under continuous reinforcement extinguishes quickly.", "b": "A fixed interval schedule rewards the first response after a set time (a monthly salary, a dish due out of the oven) and produces a scalloped pattern, not the steady play of gamblers.", "d": "A fixed ratio schedule rewards after a set number of responses (piece-rate pay, a free coffee after ten), so the gambler would know exactly when the win was due."}}, {"q": "The following is true regarding management of Somatic Symptom disorder (somatization disorder):", "concept": "The answer is b: biofeedback is a useful approach in managing somatic symptom disorder. In DSM-5-TR, somatic symptom disorder means one or more distressing bodily symptoms (pain, fatigue, stomach upset) together with excessive thoughts, feelings or behaviour about them: persistent worry about their seriousness, high anxiety about health, or too much time and energy devoted to them, usually for more than six months. It replaced the older somatisation disorder, descended from Briquet's syndrome (Paul Briquet, 1859), which required many medically unexplained symptoms. Management is mainly psychological and rests on a few principles. First, one primary doctor sees the patient at regular, scheduled short visits that do not depend on new complaints; this reduces doctor shopping and needless tests. Second, cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT), individual or in groups, has the strongest evidence: it challenges catastrophic readings of bodily sensations and reduces checking, reassurance seeking and avoidance. Third, adjuncts such as biofeedback, relaxation training, mindfulness and hypnosis teach control over muscle tension and arousal and show the patient that mind and body interact. Antidepressants help when depression or anxiety coexist, but medication is not the mainstay. Example: Meera, with years of headaches and normal scans, learns through EMG biofeedback to watch her forehead muscle tension fall on a screen as she relaxes, and her headaches ease. Option b is right because biofeedback is a recognised adjunct that lowers physiological arousal and shifts the patient's focus from disease to self-control. Exam trap: students pick a because the patient feels physically ill, but drugs mainly treat the coexisting depression or anxiety; options c and d are absolute statements ('no role', 'not effective') that the evidence contradicts.", "traps": {"a": "Medication is not the mainstay; antidepressants help mainly with coexisting depression or anxiety (and some pain), while CBT and steady care from one regular doctor are the core.", "c": "Hypnosis does have a role: it is a recognised adjunct for somatic and functional symptoms, such as pain and conversion symptoms, so 'no role' is false.", "d": "Group therapy, especially group CBT, reduces symptoms and health-care use in patients with somatic symptoms, so 'not effective' is false."}}, {"q": "The following is a Primary reinforcer:", "concept": "The answer is b: food is a primary reinforcer. A reinforcer is any consequence that makes the behaviour it follows more likely to happen again. A primary (unconditioned) reinforcer works without any learning, because it satisfies a biological need: food, water, warmth, sleep, relief from pain and sexual contact. A secondary (conditioned) reinforcer is a stimulus that was once neutral but became rewarding through repeated pairing with primary reinforcers, so classical conditioning is at work inside operant learning. Praise and status are secondary reinforcers. Money is a special case, a generalised reinforcer, because it is linked with many different primary reinforcers (food, shelter, comfort) and so stays powerful whatever need is active. B. F. Skinner (1953) set out these ideas in Science and Human Behavior, and Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) described the token economy on a psychiatric ward, where tokens, a secondary reinforcer, could be exchanged for snacks and privileges. Primary reinforcers lose force when the need is met (satiation), which is why animal experiments use hungry rats. Example: baby Arjun stops crying the moment he is fed; nobody taught him that milk is rewarding, whereas over the years he will learn that a gold star or his teacher's 'well done' is worth working for. Option b is right because food satisfies a biological drive and reinforces behaviour without any prior learning. Exam trap: money is the commonest wrong pick because it feels so basic to adult life, but its value is learned; a toddler will happily swap a coin for a sweet.", "traps": {"a": "Money is a generalised secondary reinforcer: it gains its value only through learned links with many primary reinforcers.", "c": "Status is a learned social reinforcer, valued because of its association with respect, resources and approval; it is secondary.", "d": "Praise is a social secondary reinforcer; it works because approval has been paired with comfort and care since infancy."}}, {"q": "A conditioned reinforcer is:", "concept": "The answer is a: money is a conditioned (secondary) reinforcer, a stimulus that strengthens behaviour only because it has been linked with other reinforcers. A reinforcer is any consequence that makes the behaviour before it more likely. Reinforcers come in two kinds. Primary (unconditioned) reinforcers satisfy biological needs and work without any learning: food, water, warmth, sexual pleasure, relief from pain. Conditioned or secondary reinforcers start out neutral and gain their power by being paired with primary ones, through the same associative process as classical conditioning. Money is the textbook case. A ten rupee note means nothing to an infant, but as a child learns that it buys sweets, toys and food, the note itself becomes rewarding. Because money is linked with so many different primary reinforcers, B. F. Skinner (1953), in Science and Human Behavior, called it a generalised reinforcer, one that works whatever need is active at the moment. John Wolfe (1936) showed the same process in chimpanzees, who learned to work for poker chips that they could later exchange for food; the idea grew into the token economy of Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968). Example: Arjun's son works for stars on a chart while they can be traded for treats, and stops once they buy nothing; a conditioned reinforcer fades if it is never backed by a primary one. Option a is right because money is reinforcing only through learned association with things that meet biological needs. Exam trap: sexual pleasure seems social and learned in humans, so students pick it, but it is a primary reinforcer like food and water. Also keep a conditioned reinforcer (which follows a response) apart from a conditioned stimulus (which comes before one).", "traps": {"b": "Food is a primary (unconditioned) reinforcer: it satisfies hunger and works without any prior learning, which makes it the opposite of a conditioned reinforcer.", "c": "Water is a primary reinforcer that reduces thirst, a biological drive; its power is inborn, not acquired by pairing with other reinforcers.", "d": "Sexual pleasure is a primary reinforcer tied to a biological drive; culture shapes how it is expressed, but it does not get its value from association with other reinforcers."}}, {"q": "In clinical psychology, the importance of external stimuli on our responses is the underlying principle of:", "concept": "The answer is c: behavioural theory holds that behaviour, normal or abnormal, is shaped by external stimuli in the environment and by the consequences that follow it. John B. Watson (1913), in 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It', argued that psychology should study observable behaviour, not the mind. Two learning processes explain how the environment controls us. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927), a neutral stimulus paired with a meaningful one comes to trigger a response by itself. In operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner (1938), a behaviour becomes more or less frequent depending on whether it is reinforced or punished. Clinical psychology turned these ideas into behaviour therapy. Joseph Wolpe (1958) treated phobias by systematic desensitisation, and behaviour therapists still begin with a functional analysis of antecedents, behaviour and consequences (the ABC model): what happens just before the problem, and what follows it. The problem is treated as learned behaviour that can be unlearned, not as a sign of hidden conflict. Example: four-year-old Kabir throws tantrums at the shop counter. A behavioural clinician notices that his parents buy him chocolate to stop the screaming, so the tantrum is being reinforced; the plan is to stop the payoff and praise calm behaviour instead. Option c is right because only the behavioural model places the causes of behaviour in external stimuli and their consequences. Exam trap: Gestalt theory also studies how we respond to stimuli, but it is about how the mind organises them into wholes, an inner process; the phenomenological and psychodynamic models both look inward too, to subjective experience and to unconscious conflict respectively.", "traps": {"a": "Phenomenological theory, the basis of Carl Rogers' (1951) person-centred approach, says behaviour follows from the person's subjective perception of reality (the phenomenal field), not from the external stimulus itself.", "b": "Psychodynamic theory, from Sigmund Freud (1900 onward), explains behaviour by internal unconscious conflicts among id, ego and superego and by early childhood experience, not by present external stimuli.", "d": "Gestalt theory (Max Wertheimer, 1912; later Gestalt therapy by Fritz Perls, 1951) concerns how the mind organises experience into meaningful wholes and here-and-now awareness, not environmental control of responses."}}, {"q": "The highest level attained based on Maslow's hierarchy is of:", "concept": "The answer is b: self-actualisation is the highest level of Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs. Abraham Maslow (1943), in 'A Theory of Human Motivation', and later in Motivation and Personality (1954), arranged human needs as a pyramid of five levels. From bottom to top they are physiological needs (food, water, sleep), safety needs (security, order, freedom from danger), belongingness and love, esteem (self-respect plus recognition, status and prestige from others), and self-actualisation. Lower needs are prepotent: while they are unmet they dominate behaviour, and they must be largely satisfied before higher needs take charge. The first four are deficiency needs, which stop motivating once they are met. Self-actualisation is a growth or being need: the drive to become everything one is capable of becoming, which grows stronger as it is satisfied. Maslow studied people he saw as self-actualisers, such as Abraham Lincoln and Albert Einstein, and described them as accepting, spontaneous, problem-centred and prone to peak experiences. Example: Meera has a secure job, a loving family and respect at work, yet she feels restless until she starts painting seriously; that pull is self-actualisation. Option b is right because self-actualisation sits at the top of the classic five-level pyramid. Late in life Maslow wrote of self-transcendence beyond self-actualisation, but the standard five-level model that exams test ends with self-actualisation. Exam trap: security, self-esteem and prestige all belong to lower levels; prestige in particular sounds lofty, but Maslow placed it inside the esteem needs, the fourth level.", "traps": {"a": "Security belongs to the safety needs, the second level from the bottom: the need for protection, stability and freedom from fear.", "c": "Self-esteem is part of the esteem needs, the fourth level; it is a deficiency need that must be met before self-actualisation becomes the main motive.", "d": "Prestige is the outward half of the esteem needs (status, recognition and reputation from others), so it sits at the fourth level, below self-actualisation."}}, {"q": "The term Clinical Psychology was first proposed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: Lightner Witmer first proposed the term clinical psychology. Lightner Witmer (1867 to 1956) studied with James McKeen Cattell at the University of Pennsylvania, took his doctorate under Wilhelm Wundt (founder of the Leipzig laboratory, 1879) in 1892, and then returned to Pennsylvania. In March 1896 he opened the first psychological clinic there, after a schoolteacher brought him a boy who could not learn to spell. Later that year he described his 'clinical method' to the American Psychological Association, and in 1907 he founded the journal The Psychological Clinic, whose first issue carried his article 'Clinical Psychology', the first printed use of the name. His clinic worked mainly with children who had learning and behaviour problems, combining assessment with remedial teaching, so early clinical psychology was closer to what we now call school psychology and special education than to adult psychotherapy. For founding both the name and the first clinic, Witmer is called the father of clinical psychology. Option a is right because Witmer coined the term and set up the first clinic in which it was practised. Exam trap: Alfred Binet and James McKeen Cattell are famous for mental testing, which clinical psychologists later relied on heavily, so they look like founders of the field; but neither of them named it. Ernst Heinrich Weber is a psychophysicist with no link to clinics. Remember the pair: 1896, the first clinic; 1907, the name in print.", "traps": {"b": "Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon, built the first practical intelligence test (1905) to identify Paris schoolchildren who needed special teaching; he founded modern intelligence testing, not the named field of clinical psychology.", "c": "James McKeen Cattell coined the term 'mental test' (1890) and measured reaction time and sensory abilities; he pioneered the study of individual differences and was Witmer's teacher, but he did not name clinical psychology.", "d": "Ernst Heinrich Weber was a German physiologist known for Weber's law (1834), that the just noticeable difference is a constant fraction of the original stimulus; he belongs to psychophysics, not clinical psychology."}}, {"q": "A student tells the counsellor \"I do not hate the teacher, he hates me\" is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is a: saying 'I do not hate the teacher, he hates me' is projection, attributing one's own unacceptable feeling to someone else. A defence mechanism is an unconscious strategy that protects the ego from anxiety by distorting reality. Sigmund Freud (1896) first described projection in paranoia, and Anna Freud (1936), in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, set it among the ego's standard defences. In projection, an impulse that would make the person feel guilty or ashamed, such as hostility, envy or sexual desire, is pushed out of awareness and then 'seen' in another person. The person gains twice: he no longer has to own the feeling, and his own anger now feels justified as self-defence. In his analysis of the Schreber case (1911), Freud traced persecutory delusions to a chain whose last step is exactly this: 'I hate him' is turned into 'he hates me'. That is the student's sentence. George Vaillant (1977) classed projection as an immature defence, common in adolescence and in paranoid and prejudiced thinking. Example: Arjun, who quietly resents his elder brother's success, keeps telling friends that his brother is jealous of him. Option a is right because the student disowns his own hatred and places it in the teacher. Exam trap: the stem opens with a denial ('I do not hate'), which pulls students towards option d; but pure denial would stop there. The defining move is the second half, relocating the feeling onto the teacher, and that relocation is projection.", "traps": {"b": "Assimilation is Jean Piaget's (1936) term for fitting new experience into an existing scheme; it is a process of cognitive development, not a defence mechanism.", "c": "Regression is a retreat under stress to behaviour of an earlier developmental stage, such as an older child bed-wetting after a sibling is born; nothing childish is shown here.", "d": "Denial is refusing to accept a painful reality ('I do not have a drinking problem'); the student does more than deny, because he transfers his hatred onto the teacher."}}, {"q": "On the basis of Piaget's concept, children develop knowledge by:", "concept": "The answer is a: in Jean Piaget's theory, children develop knowledge by constructing reality out of their own experiences with the world. Jean Piaget (1936), in The Origins of Intelligence in Children, described the child as a 'little scientist' who learns by acting on objects and noticing what happens, not by passively absorbing what adults say. This view is called constructivism. Knowledge is organised into schemes, mental patterns of action or thought. When a new experience fits an existing scheme, the child assimilates it; when it does not fit, the scheme is changed or a new one is made, which is accommodation. The tension between the two is resolved by equilibration, a drive to regain a balanced understanding, and each new balance is more advanced than the last. Over years this produces the four stages: sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational and formal operational. Example: two-year-old Riya calls every four-legged animal 'dog' (assimilation). At a farm she meets a cow that moos and is far too big, so she builds a new scheme for 'cow' (accommodation). No one taught her the rule; she built it from her own encounters. Option a is right because Piaget saw knowledge as actively built by the child, not received, inherited or copied. Exam trap: Lev Vygotsky (1978) is also called a constructivist, but his social constructivism stresses learning through culture, language and more skilled partners. 'Modelling the cultural influences around them' is therefore closer to Vygotsky and Albert Bandura than to Piaget's individual, self-directed construction.", "traps": {"b": "Traditional learning environments, with a teacher transmitting facts to passive pupils, are the opposite of Piaget's discovery learning; he argued that children learn best through active exploration.", "c": "Responding to physiological changes describes a maturational view such as Arnold Gesell's (1920s), in which development unfolds on a biological timetable; Piaget counted maturation as one factor but said knowledge comes from the child's own activity.", "d": "Modelling cultural influences fits Albert Bandura's (1977) observational learning or Lev Vygotsky's (1978) sociocultural theory, where knowledge is passed on by others, not Piaget's self-constructed understanding."}}, {"q": "The traditional scientific approach to understand \"everyday life experiences of people\" is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the phenomenological approach is the traditional method for understanding the everyday lived experience of people. Phenomenology began with the philosopher Edmund Husserl (1913), who wanted to describe experience exactly as it appears to the person living it, before any theory is imposed on it. His tool was bracketing (epoche): the researcher sets aside his own assumptions so that the participant's experience can show itself. Husserl later called the taken-for-granted world of daily life the lifeworld (Lebenswelt), and Alfred Schutz (1932) carried the idea into the study of everyday social life. Martin Heidegger (1927) added that all understanding is interpretation, which led to hermeneutic phenomenology. In psychology the approach became a qualitative method: the researcher interviews a small group of people who have lived through the same phenomenon, reads their accounts closely and distils the essential structure of that experience. Interpretative phenomenological analysis (IPA), introduced by Jonathan Smith (1996), is a widely used modern form. Example: Meera interviews eight mothers about what it is like to care for a child with autism and describes the shared core of their experience: exhaustion, guilt and fierce protectiveness. Option b is right because phenomenology alone makes the person's own lived, everyday experience the object of study. Exam trap: ethnography also studies daily life, but its unit is a culture or group and its method is long participant observation of shared practices; phenomenology studies how individuals experience a phenomenon. 'Ecologic' in epidemiology means studying whole populations, not anyone's experience.", "traps": {"a": "Anthropology is the broad discipline that studies human societies, cultures and biology; 'anthropologic' names a field, not a specific scientific approach to people's lived experience.", "c": "Ethnography (Bronislaw Malinowski, 1922) describes a culture from the inside through long immersion and participant observation; its focus is shared group patterns, not the essence of individual lived experience.", "d": "An ecologic (ecological) study in epidemiology compares whole populations, such as suicide rates across states, using group-level data; it says nothing about how individuals experience their lives."}}, {"q": "While swimming in the ocean, Richie got frightened by a dark shadow even before he could identify it. The neurologic basis of this frightening experience is explained by messages being sent from:", "concept": "The answer is a: Richie's instant fright comes from the fast route that sends sensory messages from the thalamus straight to the amygdala. The thalamus is the brain's relay station: almost all sensory information (smell is the exception) passes through it before reaching the cortex. The amygdala, a small almond-shaped structure in the temporal lobe, detects threat and triggers fear. Joseph LeDoux (1996), in The Emotional Brain, showed from fear conditioning work in rats that sensory signals leave the thalamus by two roads. The low road runs directly from the sensory thalamus to the amygdala. It is fast but crude: it carries only a rough sketch such as 'large dark shape', yet that is enough to set off fear. The high road runs from the thalamus to the sensory cortex and only then to the amygdala. It is slower but detailed, and it tells you whether the shape is a shark, a rock or a cloud's shadow. The amygdala then drives the hypothalamus (racing heart, sympathetic arousal) and the brainstem (freezing or startle). The low road is adaptive: jumping back from what may be a shark costs little if it proves to be a rock, while waiting for certainty could cost your life. This is why fear can arrive before recognition, as it did for Richie. Option a is right because only the thalamus to amygdala pathway explains fear arising before the object is identified. Exam trap: students reach for cortical routes because seeing the shadow seems to need the visual lobes; but conscious identification is the high road, and Richie was frightened before it had finished.", "traps": {"b": "The parasympathetic nervous system calms the body after arousal (rest and digest); fear is driven by the sympathetic system, and the autonomic system does not send fright messages up to the cortex.", "c": "The pituitary is controlled by the hypothalamus, not the frontal lobe, and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal hormone cascade is a slower stress response; the prefrontal cortex, if anything, damps fear down.", "d": "Occipital to parietal is the dorsal 'where' stream of vision (Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin, 1982), a cortical route for locating objects; it belongs to slower, conscious processing and does not generate fear."}}, {"q": "The WRONG statement regarding the EEG is that:", "concept": "The answer is c: the wrong statement is that delta waves occur during the awake state, because delta is the mark of deep sleep. The electroencephalogram (EEG) records the summed electrical activity of cortical neurons through electrodes on the scalp; Hans Berger (1929) made the first human recordings and named alpha and beta waves. Waves are described by frequency, in cycles per second (hertz, Hz). Beta waves, 13 to 30 Hz and low in amplitude, appear when a person is alert, eyes open, thinking or anxious. Alpha waves, 8 to 12 Hz, appear in relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed, strongest over the back of the head; they are what you see when someone switches off the light and lies down to sleep. Theta waves, 4 to 7 Hz, mark stage N1, the drowsy transition into early sleep. Stage N2 adds sleep spindles and K-complexes. Delta waves, 0.5 to 4 Hz and high in amplitude, define N3, slow-wave sleep, the deepest stage, concentrated early in the night. In a healthy awake adult, delta activity is abnormal and points to brain dysfunction such as a lesion, encephalopathy or coma. Example: as Kabir lies in bed with the lights off his EEG shows alpha; as he dozes it slows to theta; an hour later, in deep sleep, delta dominates. Option c is right because delta belongs to deep sleep, not to the waking state. Exam trap: REM sleep is the stage whose EEG looks awake (fast and low voltage, hence 'paradoxical sleep'); do not let that reversal tempt you into thinking slow delta can also appear while awake.", "traps": {"a": "True statement: beta waves (13 to 30 Hz, low amplitude) are the normal EEG of an alert, awake, actively thinking person.", "b": "True statement: alpha waves (8 to 12 Hz) dominate relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed, exactly the state of lying in the dark before sleep.", "d": "True statement: theta waves (4 to 7 Hz) characterise stage N1, the light, early stage of sleep."}}, {"q": "Research analysis was done on the various meanings of newspaper editorial cartoons which appeared during a period of 4 years. This is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is d: analysing the meanings of newspaper editorial cartoons is semiotics, the study of signs and how they create meaning. A sign is anything that stands for something else: a word, a picture, a colour, a gesture. Ferdinand de Saussure (1916), in his Course in General Linguistics, split the sign into the signifier (the form, such as a drawing of a crow) and the signified (the concept it calls up). Charles Sanders Peirce, writing from the 1860s, sorted signs into icons (which resemble their object), indexes (which are caused by it, as smoke indicates fire) and symbols (linked by convention, as a dove stands for peace). Roland Barthes (1957), in Mythologies, applied semiotics to advertisements, photographs and popular culture, showing that images carry a literal meaning (denotation) and a deeper cultural or ideological one (connotation). Cartoons are built almost entirely from such signs: exaggerated features, symbolic animals, labels and captions. A semiotic analysis asks what each sign means and how together they build the cartoon's message. Example: Arjun studies four years of R. K. Laxman's 'You Said It' cartoons in The Times of India and asks what the silent Common Man, in his checked coat, signifies about ordinary citizens and their rulers. Option d is right because the research is about the meanings carried by visual signs and symbols. Exam trap: hermeneutics and discourse analysis also deal with meaning, so they tempt; but hermeneutics is the general interpretation of texts as wholes, and discourse analysis studies how language in use constructs social reality. Meanings of signs and images point to semiotics.", "traps": {"a": "Hermeneutics (developed by Friedrich Schleiermacher and Wilhelm Dilthey in the 1800s, and by Hans-Georg Gadamer in Truth and Method, 1960) is the art of interpreting texts by moving between parts and the whole (the hermeneutic circle); it is a general theory of interpretation, not the specific study of visual signs.", "b": "Discourse analysis (for example Jonathan Potter and Margaret Wetherell, 1987) studies language in use, how talk and text construct identities and social reality; it centres on spoken and written language, not the symbolic meaning of drawings.", "c": "Ethnography studies a living culture through long immersion and participant observation; analysing archived cartoons involves no fieldwork among people."}}, {"q": "The following is TRUE regarding transcranial magnetic stimulation:", "concept": "The answer is d: transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) is free of the memory and thinking problems that electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) can cause. TMS passes a brief, strong magnetic pulse from a coil held against the scalp. By electromagnetic induction the pulse creates a small electric current in the cortex just beneath the coil, which can excite or quieten the neurons there. Anthony Barker (1985) and his team in Sheffield built the first modern TMS device. Given in repeated trains (rTMS), it changes activity in mood circuits, and the US Food and Drug Administration cleared rTMS for depression in 2008. For depression the coil is placed over the left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex, an area that is underactive in many depressed patients. The patient is awake and needs no anaesthesia or muscle relaxant; he sits in a chair for each session, usually on weekdays for four to six weeks, and can go straight back to work. The main side effects are scalp discomfort and headache; a seizure is rare. Unlike ECT, TMS does not set out to produce a seizure and does not cause memory loss. Example: Kabir's depression has not improved with two antidepressants; he has rTMS sessions before work and drives himself home afterwards. Option d is right because, among these statements, the absence of cognitive side effects is the one true feature, and it is what sets TMS apart from ECT. Exam trap: options b and c borrow from ECT and from the wrong brain area; anaesthesia is needed for ECT, and the target for depression is prefrontal, not occipital. Key note: 'no cognitive side effects' is the intended contrast with ECT; strictly, TMS has no significant cognitive side effects, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Reversed: depression is the main approved use of rTMS; its use for psychotic symptoms such as persistent auditory hallucinations remains experimental.", "b": "General anaesthesia with a muscle relaxant is needed for ECT (Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini, 1938), not for TMS, which is given to an awake, seated patient.", "c": "For depression the coil goes over the left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex; stimulating the occipital cortex mainly makes people see flashes of light (phosphenes) and is a research tool, not a treatment."}}, {"q": "When a car driver fails to notice a pedestrian who has just stepped onto the road, it is an example of", "concept": "The answer is c: a driver who fails to see a pedestrian in plain view is showing inattentional blindness. Inattentional blindness is the failure to notice an unexpected object that is fully visible, because attention is focused elsewhere. Arien Mack and Irvin Rock (1998) named it after finding that many people busy judging the arms of a cross on a screen did not see an extra shape that appeared right beside it. The best-known demonstration is by Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris (1999): when viewers counted basketball passes in a short film, about half failed to notice a person in a gorilla suit who walked through the scene and thumped her chest. The eyes take in the image, but without attention it never reaches awareness. On the road this is the 'looked but failed to see' accident: the driver is watching the signal or the car ahead, or talking on the phone, and a pedestrian is not expected, so he goes unseen. David Strayer and William Johnston (2001) showed in a simulated driving task that phone conversations make drivers miss more signals. Option c is right because the pedestrian was visible yet not perceived for lack of attention, which is the definition of inattentional blindness. Exam trap: divided attention may be the cause (attention split between the road and a call), so it tempts; but the question asks for the name of the failure to notice, which is inattentional blindness. Also keep it apart from change blindness, which is missing a change between two views of the same scene.", "traps": {"a": "Anton's syndrome (Gabriel Anton, 1899) is cortical blindness from damage to both occipital lobes in which the patient denies being blind and confabulates what he 'sees'; the driver here has normal vision.", "b": "Amaurosis fugax is a brief, painless loss of vision in one eye, usually from a small embolus cutting off blood flow to the retina, and a warning sign of stroke; it is a vascular event, not a failure of attention.", "d": "Divided attention is attending to two tasks at once; it can set up the miss, but the miss itself, an unseen object in plain view, is called inattentional blindness."}}, {"q": "A housewife is cooking food on the stove and also participating in a videoconference of her department. This is possible due to:", "concept": "The answer is b: cooking while taking part in a videoconference is divided attention, attending to two tasks at the same time. Attention is limited, so the question is how we share it. Daniel Kahneman (1973), in Attention and Effort, described attention as a single pool of mental effort that can be split between tasks as long as their total demand stays within capacity. Christopher Wickens (1984) refined this in his multiple resource theory: tasks interfere less when they use different senses, codes and responses. Cooking uses the hands and eyes; the meeting uses the ears and voice, so the two compete only a little. Practice helps too. Walter Schneider and Richard Shiffrin (1977) showed that well-practised tasks become automatic and need almost no attention, which is why an experienced cook can stir the dal while following a discussion. Two hard, unfamiliar tasks using the same channel, such as writing a report while listening to a lecture, are very hard to divide. Clinicians use the model of McKay Sohlberg and Catherine Mateer (1987), which ranks attention from focused through sustained, selective and alternating to divided, the most demanding level. Option b is right because the woman keeps both tasks going at once rather than shutting one out or taking them in turns. Exam trap: alternating attention means switching back and forth, giving each task full attention in turn (stop cooking, speak, then return to the stove). Research suggests that much everyday multitasking involves rapid switching, but the textbook label for doing two things at once is divided attention.", "traps": {"a": "Alternating attention is the mental flexibility to shift focus back and forth between tasks with different demands, one at a time, such as stopping the cooking to present and then returning to the stove.", "c": "Selective attention is focusing on one source while ignoring others, as in Colin Cherry's (1953) cocktail party effect; the woman is not ignoring either task.", "d": "Sustained attention, or vigilance, is keeping focus on one task over a long period, as in Norman Mackworth's (1948) clock test for radar operators; the stem is about two tasks at once, not endurance."}}, {"q": "The following is true regarding suicide:", "concept": "The answer is c: when a severe depression begins to lift, the apparent improvement can mean the person has decided to end his life, so suicide risk may rise rather than fall. Severe depression brings psychomotor slowing, exhaustion and indecision; a person may wish to die yet lack the energy to plan or act. In the early weeks of recovery, for example soon after starting an antidepressant or on discharge from hospital, energy and drive often return before hopelessness and low mood have eased. That gap gives the person the means to act on thoughts that are still there. A second pattern is the sudden calm that follows a decision: the inner struggle stops, the person looks peaceful, settles his affairs and gives away belongings, and relatives think he is better. Clinicians therefore increase monitoring in early recovery and after discharge. Other well-established facts sit alongside this one: the strongest single predictor of suicide is a previous attempt, and hopelessness predicts suicide better than the severity of depression. Example: Arjun, depressed for months, suddenly seems relaxed and cheerful, returns borrowed books to friends and writes a long letter to his sister; his psychologist reads this as a warning sign and reassesses his risk at once. Option c is right because the lifting of depression is a recognised period of raised suicide risk. Exam trap: option b reverses the gender paradox. Women attempt suicide more often, but men die by suicide more often because they use more lethal methods such as hanging and firearms; 'successful' in the option means completed suicide, which is commoner in men.", "traps": {"a": "Reversed: people with schizophrenia have a lifetime suicide risk of about 5 per cent, many times that of the general population, with young men early in the illness and after a relapse at highest risk.", "b": "Reversed: women make more suicide attempts, but men complete suicide more often (the gender paradox), largely because they choose more lethal methods.", "d": "False: many tools exist, such as Aaron Beck's Hopelessness Scale (1974) and Scale for Suicide Ideation (1979), the SAD PERSONS mnemonic (William Patterson and colleagues, 1983) and the Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale."}}, {"q": "The repetition or prolongation of a visual phenomenon beyond the time limit of the physiological image is:", "concept": "The answer is c: palinopsia is the persistence or recurrence of a visual image after the object that produced it has gone, beyond the time a normal afterimage lasts. The word comes from the Greek palin (again) and opsis (seeing). Everyone sees brief physiological afterimages: stare at a bright bulb and a dark or complementary coloured blob lingers for a few seconds, then fades. Palinopsia is different in kind. In the hallucinatory form, a clear, formed copy of a real object persists or reappears, sometimes minutes or hours later, often in another part of the visual field. In the illusory form, moving objects leave trails or images smear and linger. Palinopsia is a disorder of visual perception linked with lesions of the occipital or parieto-occipital cortex, migraine, seizures, some medicines, and hallucinogen persisting perception disorder (HPPD) after drugs such as LSD. Example: after a small stroke at the back of her brain, Meera keeps seeing the nurse's face on the wall a minute after the nurse has left the room. Option c is right because the stem describes a visual image repeated or prolonged beyond the normal physiological image, which is the definition of palinopsia. Exam trap: all four options are rare, similar-sounding '-opsia' or '-prosopia' terms. Fix each by its root meaning: akinetopsia is motion blindness, teleopsia makes objects look far away, paraprosopia distorts faces, and only palinopsia means 'seeing again'.", "traps": {"a": "Akinetopsia is motion blindness: moving things are seen as a series of still frames, caused by damage to area V5 (MT) in the middle temporal region; it is a loss of motion, not a persisting image.", "b": "Paraprosopia is an illusory distortion of faces, in which a face looks altered or grotesque; it concerns faces, not prolonged images.", "d": "Teleopsia is a dysmetropsia in which objects look much farther away than they are, seen in migraine and the 'Alice in Wonderland' syndrome named by John Todd (1955); it distorts distance, not duration."}}, {"q": "Sometimes we are able to read the article and understand its content despite the fact that there are many misprints. This is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: reading an article correctly despite many misprints is a completion illusion, the tendency to perceive an incomplete or faulty pattern as complete and correct. Perception is not a passive copy of the page. The brain combines bottom-up input (the letters actually printed) with top-down knowledge (vocabulary, grammar, the topic and what we expect to read). When a word is misspelt or a letter is missing, expectation fills the gap and we 'see' the right word, usually without noticing the error at all. The principle behind it is closure, one of the Gestalt laws of organisation set out by Max Wertheimer (1923): we complete broken or partial figures into whole, familiar forms. The same process explains why proofreading our own writing is so hard, and why most people read the classic sign 'PARIS IN THE THE SPRING' without noticing the doubled 'THE'. It is an illusion in the strict sense, because a real stimulus is misperceived: the page says one thing and we perceive another. Example: Kabir reads a college notice saying 'Studnets must sumbit fomrs by Fridya' and understands it at once, noticing the four spelling errors only when a friend points them out. Option b is right because the reader completes and corrects the faulty text from expectation, which is what a completion illusion means. Exam trap: pareidolia also involves the mind adding meaning, but it is seeing a meaningful pattern, such as a face, in a random or vague stimulus like clouds or a stain on a wall; a misprinted article is not random, it is a nearly complete pattern that gets filled in.", "traps": {"a": "Learned illusions are those whose strength depends on past experience and culture, such as the Muller-Lyer illusion being stronger in people raised among rectangular buildings (Marshall Segall, Donald Campbell and Melville Herskovits, 1966); the stem describes filling in gaps, not a culturally learned distortion.", "c": "Similarity is a Gestalt grouping law (Max Wertheimer, 1923): items alike in colour, shape or size are seen as belonging together; it organises a display but does not correct missing or wrong parts.", "d": "Pareidolia is seeing a meaningful image, usually a face, in a random or vague stimulus, such as the 'man in the moon' or faces in clouds; misprinted text is not random noise."}}, {"q": "A histrionic (hysterical) personality could have the following clinical features:", "concept": "The answer is c: seductive behaviour is a core feature of histrionic personality disorder. Histrionic personality disorder is a Cluster B ('dramatic, emotional, erratic') pattern of excessive emotionality and attention seeking that begins by early adulthood and shows across many settings. The older label, hysterical personality, came from the psychoanalytic idea of hysteria; DSM-III (1980) made 'histrionic', meaning theatrical, the official name. DSM-5-TR (2022) lists eight criteria, of which five are needed: discomfort when not the centre of attention; interaction with others that is often characterised by inappropriate sexually seductive or provocative behaviour; rapidly shifting and shallow expression of emotions; consistent use of physical appearance to draw attention; speech that is impressionistic and lacking in detail; self-dramatisation, theatricality and exaggerated emotion; suggestibility; and seeing relationships as more intimate than they really are. The seductiveness is usually less about sexual desire than about winning attention and approval. Example: Riya, 26, flirts with every new colleague, dresses provocatively for routine meetings, sulks when someone else is praised, and calls a film 'absolutely life-changing' but cannot say a single thing about its story. Option c is right because seductive or provocative behaviour is one of the formal diagnostic criteria for histrionic personality. Exam trap: dramatic suicidal gestures sound attention-seeking and therefore 'histrionic', but recurrent suicidal behaviour, gestures, threats or self-harm is a criterion of borderline personality disorder, not of histrionic personality disorder.", "traps": {"a": "Superstitiousness and magical thinking belong to schizotypal personality disorder (Cluster A, odd and eccentric), not to histrionic personality.", "b": "Recurrent suicidal behaviour, gestures or threats, and self-mutilation, form a criterion of borderline personality disorder; histrionic people may make dramatic threats, but suicidality is not among their criteria.", "d": "Shyness and social inhibition with fear of rejection mark avoidant personality disorder (Cluster C); the histrionic person is the opposite and seeks the spotlight."}}, {"q": "Ramu was initially conditioned to be fearful of brown rats. Over time he began to fear all brown animals like dogs. This is indicative of:", "concept": "The answer is c: Ramu's fear spreading from brown rats to all brown animals is stimulus generalisation. In classical conditioning, once a neutral stimulus has become a conditioned stimulus (CS), other stimuli that resemble it also come to trigger the conditioned response (CR). Ivan Pavlov (1927) found that a dog conditioned to salivate to one tone also salivated to tones of nearby pitch, and that the response grew weaker as the new tone became less similar. This falling curve is the generalisation gradient. The classic human case is Little Albert. John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) paired a white rat with a sudden loud noise until the infant feared the rat; his fear then generalised to a rabbit, a dog, a fur coat and a Santa Claus mask with a white cotton beard, all things that shared the rat's furry, light-coloured look. Ramu's case works the same way: the shared feature, brown colour, carries the fear to dogs he was never conditioned with. Generalisation is adaptive, because a dangerous thing rarely looks exactly the same twice, but it also explains why phobias and traumatic fears can spread far beyond their original trigger. Option c is right because a conditioned fear has spread to new stimuli similar to the original CS. Exam trap: stimulus discrimination is the opposite process (responding only to the original stimulus), and 'stimulus dispersion' is not a conditioning term at all; it simply borrows the idea of spreading to sound plausible.", "traps": {"a": "Higher order conditioning uses an established CS as if it were an unconditioned stimulus to condition a new neutral stimulus (bell paired with food, then a light paired with the bell); Ramu's new fears come from similarity, not from a second pairing.", "b": "'Stimulus dispersion' is not a standard conditioning term; it is a distractor made to sound like the spreading of a response.", "d": "Stimulus discrimination is learning to respond only to the original CS and not to similar stimuli; it would mean Ramu fearing brown rats but not brown dogs, the reverse of the stem."}}, {"q": "In an interview for this post, you are asked \"Who was the first President of India?\" and you promptly answer correctly \"Dr. Rajendra Prasad\". This is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is c: recalling that Dr Rajendra Prasad was the first President of India is semantic memory, memory for general facts and knowledge. Endel Tulving (1972) divided long-term declarative memory into two systems. Episodic memory holds personal events tied to a particular time and place, the 'what, where and when' of your own life, such as your first day at college. Semantic memory holds facts, word meanings and concepts that make up your general knowledge of the world, stored without any record of when or where you learned them. You know that Rajendra Prasad held office from 1950 to 1962, that Delhi is the capital and that a triangle has three sides, but you probably cannot recall the day you learned each fact. Both systems are explicit (declarative) memory, which Larry Squire (1987) contrasted with implicit (non-declarative) memory such as skills and priming. Brain evidence supports the split: Tulving's patient K. C., injured in a motorcycle accident in 1981, could not recall a single personal event from his past, yet kept much of his general knowledge. Option c is right because the answer is a general fact retrieved from stored knowledge, not a relived personal experience. Exam trap: the interview itself is an episode in your life, which pulls students towards episodic memory; but the question asks what you retrieved, and that is a fact. Describing the day you visited Rashtrapati Bhavan on a school trip would be episodic memory.", "traps": {"a": "Episodic memory (Endel Tulving, 1972) is memory for personally experienced events with their time and place; recalling a general fact about history is not reliving an event.", "b": "Working memory (Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch, 1974) holds and manipulates information for a few seconds; it briefly holds the answer as you speak, but the fact itself comes from long-term semantic memory.", "d": "Implicit memory shows itself without conscious recollection, as in skills, habits and priming (Daniel Schacter, 1987); consciously answering a factual question is explicit memory."}}, {"q": "Brainstorming is a form of:", "concept": "The answer is c: brainstorming is a form of divergent thinking, generating many different possible ideas or solutions to one problem. Joy Paul Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, distinguished two kinds of productive thinking. Convergent thinking narrows down to the single correct answer, as in an arithmetic sum or a multiple-choice question. Divergent thinking spreads outward to many possible answers, as in 'List all the uses of a brick'. Guilford scored divergent output for fluency (how many ideas), flexibility (how many categories), originality (how rare) and elaboration (how detailed), and he linked it closely with creativity. Brainstorming was devised by the advertising executive Alex Osborn (1953), in Applied Imagination, as a group method for producing creative ideas. Its rules are to defer judgement, aim for quantity, welcome wild ideas and build on other people's ideas, exactly the conditions that free divergent thinking from early criticism. Ideas are judged only afterwards, which is the convergent step. Later research by Michael Diehl and Wolfgang Stroebe (1987) found that people who brainstorm alone and then pool their ideas often produce more than interacting groups, partly because group members block each other while waiting to speak. Example: in fifteen minutes Meera's team lists forty ideas for a college mental health week, from street plays to a poster contest, before choosing any. Option c is right because brainstorming aims at many varied ideas, the defining feature of divergent thinking. Exam trap: brainstorming feels free-flowing, so 'non-directed thinking' tempts; but brainstorming is aimed at a set problem, while non-directed thinking is aimless, like daydreaming.", "traps": {"a": "Convergent thinking (Joy Paul Guilford, 1950) narrows the options to the single best or correct answer; brainstorming deliberately postpones that narrowing.", "b": "Non-directed (undirected) thinking is aimless, free-associative thought such as daydreaming and reverie; brainstorming is directed at a stated problem.", "d": "A heuristic is a mental shortcut or rule of thumb for solving problems or making judgements quickly (Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman, 1974, on judgement heuristics); brainstorming is a method of generating ideas, not a shortcut."}}, {"q": "You teach students Hydroelectricity. You ask a question \"Due to monsoons, there is a dangerous rise in level of water in Bhakra Dam. Formulate a plan to release the water gradually and avoid flooding in the plains\". This type of question is most likely to be at Bloom's level of:", "concept": "The answer is c: asking students to formulate a plan for releasing the water is a synthesis question in Bloom's taxonomy. Benjamin Bloom (1956) and his colleagues published the Taxonomy of Educational Objectives, Handbook I: Cognitive Domain, which arranges learning goals in six levels from simple to complex: knowledge (recall facts), comprehension (explain in one's own words), application (use a rule in a new case), analysis (break material into parts and see how they relate), synthesis (put parts together to form a new whole) and evaluation (judge value against criteria). Each level builds on those below it. The handbook lists 'production of a plan, or proposed set of operations' as one of the forms of synthesis, so a question asking students to design a release schedule for Bhakra Dam fits it almost word for word. The students must combine what they know about reservoir level, monsoon inflow, spillway gates and the plains downstream on the Sutlej into a new plan of their own; there is no single answer to recall. In the revised taxonomy of Lorin Anderson and David Krathwohl (2001), the levels became verbs (remember, understand, apply, analyse, evaluate, create), and synthesis was renamed 'create' and moved to the top, above evaluation. Option c is right because formulating a plan means building something new from parts, which is synthesis. Exam trap: in the original 1956 order the top level is evaluation, so students who equate 'hardest' with 'highest' pick d; but judging a plan is evaluation, while producing one is synthesis.", "traps": {"a": "Analysis is breaking material into its parts and finding how they relate, such as 'Identify the factors that made the water level in Bhakra Dam rise'; it examines, it does not create a plan.", "b": "Knowledge, the lowest level, is recall of facts, such as 'On which river is Bhakra Dam built?'; a recall question never asks for a new plan.", "d": "Evaluation is judging the worth of something against criteria, such as 'Which of two release plans is safer, and why?'; it comes after a plan exists, rather than producing one."}}, {"q": "Thorndike suggested that the ability to deal with skilled trades and scientific appliances is a form of", "concept": "The answer is c: Edward Thorndike called the ability to deal with skilled trades, tools and scientific appliances concrete (mechanical) intelligence. Edward Thorndike (1920), in an article titled 'Intelligence and its Uses', proposed that intelligence is not one general ability but at least three broad kinds. Abstract intelligence is the ability to handle ideas and symbols: words, numbers, formulas and concepts, the kind measured by most school and IQ tests. Concrete or mechanical intelligence is the ability to understand and manage things: tools, machines, instruments and practical materials, as in skilled trades and laboratory work. Social intelligence is the ability to understand and manage people and to act wisely in human relations; it later fed into the idea of emotional intelligence. Thorndike's wider view is called the multifactor or 'anarchic' theory: intelligence is the sum of many specific connections in the nervous system, with no single general factor like Charles Spearman's (1904) g. His CAVD test (1927) sampled completion, arithmetic, vocabulary and directions. Example: Kabir left school early and scores modestly on verbal tests, yet he can strip and rebuild a motorcycle engine and set up a laboratory centrifuge without a manual; he has high concrete intelligence. Option c is right because skilled trades and scientific appliances are dealings with things, which is Thorndike's concrete intelligence. Exam trap: 'concrete' here has nothing to do with Jean Piaget's concrete operational stage, and 'mechanical' and 'concrete' are two names for the same category; fluid intelligence belongs to a different theorist and a different scheme.", "traps": {"a": "Abstract intelligence, in Edward Thorndike's (1920) scheme, is skill with ideas and symbols such as words, numbers and concepts; it is not about handling tools and appliances.", "b": "Social intelligence, Thorndike's (1920) third kind, is the ability to understand and manage people and act wisely in relationships, a forerunner of emotional intelligence.", "d": "Fluid intelligence belongs to Raymond Cattell (1963): the ability to reason and solve new problems independent of learned knowledge, contrasted with crystallised intelligence; it is not one of Thorndike's three kinds."}}, {"q": "On the basis of Gardner's theory of multiple intelligence, the potential career choice for a person with Spatial intelligence should be a:", "concept": "The answer is a: in Howard Gardner's theory, spatial intelligence suits careers such as airline pilot. Howard Gardner (1983), in Frames of Mind, argued that intelligence is not one general ability but several relatively independent intelligences. He identified them from evidence such as brain damage that destroys one ability and spares others, prodigies and savants, and separate developmental histories. The original seven were linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal and intrapersonal; naturalist was added in 1999. Spatial intelligence is the ability to perceive the visual and spatial world accurately, form mental images, rotate and transform them, and find one's way through space. Damage to the right parietal region tends to impair it, which supported Gardner's claim that it is a separate ability. Gardner's examples include navigators, sculptors, architects and chess players; he described the Puluwat sailors of the Caroline Islands in the Pacific, who navigate between islands without instruments by holding a mental map of stars, swells and islands. A pilot must constantly picture the aircraft's position in three dimensions, judge distances and angles on approach and read spatial displays. Example: Arjun, who could always rebuild a Lego model from memory and never gets lost in a new city, chooses pilot training. Option a is right because flying depends above all on visual and spatial judgement. Exam trap: teacher and psychologist sound like intelligence-heavy jobs, but in Gardner's scheme they rest mainly on interpersonal (and linguistic) intelligence, not spatial; a lawyer relies on linguistic and logical-mathematical intelligence.", "traps": {"b": "A lawyer relies chiefly on linguistic intelligence (argument, persuasion, precise wording) and logical-mathematical intelligence (reasoning), not on visual and spatial skill.", "c": "A teacher relies mainly on interpersonal intelligence, understanding and motivating others, along with linguistic intelligence for explaining clearly.", "d": "A psychologist draws mainly on interpersonal and intrapersonal intelligence, understanding other people and oneself, rather than on spatial ability."}}, {"q": "Among the biological and psycho-social human needs, the following is an example of a biological need:", "concept": "The answer is b: the need for sexual satisfaction is a biological need, while independence, achievement and self-assertion are psychosocial needs. Motivation textbooks divide motives into two broad classes. Biological (primary, physiological) needs are unlearned and rooted in the body: hunger, thirst, sleep, breathing, avoidance of pain, temperature regulation, the maternal drive and sex. Most are homeostatic, driven by a bodily deficit that the behaviour corrects. Psychosocial (secondary) needs are learned through experience and culture: achievement, affiliation, power, approval, independence and self-assertion. Henry Murray (1938), in Explorations in Personality, drew the same line between viscerogenic (bodily) needs, among which he listed sex, and psychogenic (mental) needs such as achievement, autonomy and dominance. Abraham Maslow (1943) also placed sex among the physiological needs at the base of his hierarchy. Sex is a slightly unusual biological motive: it is not needed for the survival of the individual, only of the species, and it is not homeostatic in the strict sense; but it rests on hormones, the hypothalamus and inborn reflexes, so it is classed as biological, even though culture shapes how it is expressed. Example: Riya's hunger after a long fast and her sex drive are biological needs; her wish to top the class, to live on her own and to speak up firmly in meetings are psychosocial. Option b is right because sex is the only listed need based on a bodily, unlearned drive. Exam trap: self-assertion sounds instinctive, and William McDougall (1908) did list it among his instincts, but in the modern biological versus psychosocial split it is a learned social motive.", "traps": {"a": "The need for independence (autonomy, in Henry Murray's 1938 list of psychogenic needs) is learned through social experience; no bodily deficit lies behind it.", "c": "The need for achievement, studied by Henry Murray (1938) and David McClelland (1953), is the classic learned psychosocial motive, a desire to excel against a standard.", "d": "Self-assertion, the wish to stand up for oneself and influence others, is a psychosocial motive akin to Murray's need for dominance; William McDougall (1908) called it an instinct, but modern texts class it as learned."}}, {"q": "The Theory formulated by Bandura that human behaviour is learned by observation and imitation is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: Albert Bandura's theory that behaviour is learned by watching and imitating others is social learning theory. Albert Bandura (1977), in Social Learning Theory, argued that people do not have to perform a behaviour and be reinforced in order to learn it; they can learn simply by observing a model and the consequences the model receives. His Bobo doll studies with Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961) showed this. Nursery school children who watched an adult punch, kick and hit an inflatable Bobo doll with a mallet later attacked the doll in much the same ways, often copying the adult's exact actions and words, while children who had seen a calm model rarely did. Later work showed vicarious reinforcement: children who saw the model rewarded imitated more than those who saw the model punished, yet all of them could reproduce the acts when offered a reward, so learning and performance are separate. Bandura named four processes in observational learning: attention to the model, retention in memory, reproduction of the act and motivation to perform it. In 1986 he renamed the approach social cognitive theory, adding self-efficacy and reciprocal determinism. Example: Kabir learns to roll chapatis by watching his grandmother, though no one ever rewarded him for trying. Option b is right because learning by observation and imitation is the heart of Bandura's social learning theory. Exam trap: Neal Miller and John Dollard (1941) wrote Social Learning and Imitation, and Julian Rotter (1954) also used the label social learning; but the theory of observational learning and modelling named in the stem is Bandura's.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalytic theory is Sigmund Freud's (1900 onward) account of behaviour as driven by unconscious conflicts and early psychosexual experience, not by observing and copying models.", "c": "Optimum (optimal) level theory is the arousal theory of motivation (Donald Hebb, 1955, building on the Yerkes-Dodson law of 1908): people seek a moderate level of arousal; it explains motivation, not observational learning.", "d": "Incentive theory says behaviour is pulled by external goals and rewards (incentives) rather than pushed by internal drives; it is a theory of motivation, not Bandura's account of learning by imitation."}}, {"q": "According to Bronfenbrenner's ecological Systems theory, the workplace of the spouse is part of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the spouse's workplace belongs to the exosystem in Urie Bronfenbrenner's ecological systems theory. Urie Bronfenbrenner (1979), in The Ecology of Human Development, pictured a person at the centre of nested layers of environment, like a set of rings. The microsystem holds the settings the person is directly in, with face-to-face, two-way relationships: home, school, their own workplace, friends. The mesosystem is the set of links between two microsystems, such as a parent-teacher meeting that connects home and school. The exosystem holds settings the person never enters but which still reach them indirectly: a spouse's or parent's workplace, the local school board, the mass media. The macrosystem is the overarching culture: values, laws, customs and the economy. He later added the chronosystem, the dimension of time: life transitions such as divorce and historical events such as a pandemic. Example: Meera never sets foot in her husband Arjun's office. Yet when his manager moves him to night shifts, Arjun comes home tired and irritable, the family routine changes, and Meera's own days become harder. The office touches her only through him, which is exactly what an exosystem does. Option c is right because the spouse's workplace is a setting in which the person is not an active participant, but whose events still affect the person through the spouse. Exam trap: students pick microsystem because a workplace sounds like an immediate setting. It is a microsystem only for the person who works there (Arjun); for Meera it lies outside her direct experience, so it is exosystem. Also do not confuse the mesosystem, a link between two settings the person is in, with the exosystem, a setting the person is not in.", "traps": {"a": "The microsystem is the person's own immediate settings with direct two-way contact, such as home, school or their own job. The spouse's workplace is a microsystem for the spouse, not for the person in question.", "b": "The mesosystem is the web of connections between two of the person's own microsystems, such as home and school meeting at a parent-teacher conference. It is a link between settings the person is in, not a setting the person never enters.", "d": "The macrosystem is the broad cultural blueprint: the values, laws, ideology and economic system of a society. One particular workplace is far too specific and local to be macrosystem."}}, {"q": "The Thematic Apperception Test is designed to measure:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was designed to measure motivation, that is, a person's needs, drives and inner conflicts. The TAT was published by Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935) at the Harvard Psychological Clinic. It is a projective test: the person is shown ambiguous pictures of people in uncertain situations and asked to tell a story about each one, saying what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters think and feel, and how it ends. The full set has 31 cards, including one blank card, and examiners usually use a selection of about 10 to 20. The theory behind it is Murray's need-press model from Explorations in Personality (1938). A need is an inner motive (achievement, affiliation, dominance, aggression); a press is a force in the environment that helps or blocks a need. Because the pictures are vague, the person 'apperceives' them through their own needs, and the hero of each story tends to carry those needs. David McClelland (1953) later scored TAT-style stories for the need for achievement. Example: shown a card of a boy looking at a violin, Kabir tells a story in which the boy practises day and night to win a national prize. The theme of striving and success suggests a strong need for achievement. Option a is right because the TAT reads needs, motives and conflicts from story themes, which is what motivation means here. Exam trap: the TAT is a personality and motivation test, not an ability test. Do not confuse it with the Rorschach (Hermann Rorschach, 1921), which uses inkblots and reads perception and thought, not story themes.", "traps": {"b": "Scholarly ability is what achievement and aptitude tests measure, such as a school examination or the Differential Aptitude Tests. The TAT has no right or wrong answers and does not test ability.", "c": "Emotional quotient is a score for emotional intelligence, popularised by Daniel Goleman (1995) and measured by tools such as Reuven Bar-On's EQ-i. The TAT was built decades earlier to study needs and motives, not emotional skills.", "d": "Workplace assessment means tools for selecting or appraising employees, such as assessment centres, work samples and performance ratings. The TAT is a clinical personality test, even though TAT-type stories were later borrowed for management research on motives."}}, {"q": "The conflict that arises when a person gets a good job opportunity BUT he will have to go to a place he does not like is called:", "concept": "The answer is d: a single goal that both attracts and repels creates an approach-avoidance conflict. Kurt Lewin (1935) described conflicts as clashes between forces that pull a person towards goals or push the person away from them. In an approach-approach conflict there are two attractive goals (two good job offers); it is the easiest to resolve, because moving towards one makes it look even better. In an avoidance-avoidance conflict there are two unpleasant options (a painful operation or continued pain); people waver and may try to escape the choice altogether. In an approach-avoidance conflict one goal has both a good and a bad side. A fourth type, double approach-avoidance, has two options that each have good and bad sides; it is the commonest in real life. Neal Miller (1944) showed with rats why approach-avoidance produces wavering: both tendencies grow stronger as the goal comes closer, but the avoidance gradient is steeper. So from far away the person wants the goal, close to it the fear wins, and he hovers where the two gradients cross. Example: Arjun is offered a well-paid job, but it is in a city he dislikes. While the joining date is months away he is keen; as it comes near, the dislike grows sharper and he starts to doubt. Option d is right because one opportunity carries both an attractive feature (the job) and an aversive feature (the place). Exam trap: if the stem had two different jobs, each with good and bad sides, the answer would be double approach-avoidance. Here there is one job, so it is plain approach-avoidance.", "traps": {"a": "Affective-subjective conflict is not one of the classic conflict types. Organisational writers do speak of affective or relationship conflict, but that is friction between people, not one person torn by a single goal.", "b": "Opportunity-issue based conflict is not a recognised term in Kurt Lewin's scheme or anywhere else in psychology; it is a distractor built from the words of the stem.", "c": "Value-based conflict describes clashes over beliefs or principles, usually between people or groups (different views on religion or ethics, for example). The stem is about one attractive goal with an unattractive feature, not a clash of values."}}, {"q": "In conflict management, the approach that engenders a \"losing-losing\" situation is", "concept": "The answer is d: in the conflict management model, compromising is the approach usually labelled 'lose-lose' among the options given. Kenneth Thomas and Ralph Kilmann (1974) built their Conflict Mode Instrument on two dimensions: assertiveness (how far you try to satisfy your own concerns) and cooperativeness (how far you try to satisfy the other person's). Crossing them gives five modes. Competing is high assertiveness and low cooperativeness: I win, you lose. Accommodating is the reverse: I give in so that you win (lose-win). Avoiding is low on both: the issue is sidestepped and nobody gains (lose-lose). Collaborating is high on both: the parties dig into the problem until both are fully satisfied (win-win). Compromising sits in the middle: each side gives up part of what it wanted, 'splitting the difference', so neither is fully satisfied. The model grew out of Robert Blake and Jane Mouton's (1964) managerial grid. Example: Riya wants the team outing in Goa and Kabir wants Manali. They settle on a nearby resort that neither of them really wanted. Each has partly lost, which is why compromise is often called lose-lose. Option d is right because, of the four modes offered, compromising is the only one in which both parties give up something they wanted and neither gets a full win. Exam trap: accommodating also involves losing, but only one party loses (lose-win); competing has one clear winner (win-lose); collaborating is the classic win-win. Key note: many textbooks reserve the purest 'lose-lose' label for avoiding and describe compromise as partial win, partial loss. Avoiding is not among the options, so compromising, the mode in which both sides give something up, is the answer used here, and it stands.", "traps": {"a": "Collaborating (high assertiveness and high cooperativeness in the Thomas-Kilmann model) works the problem through until both sides are fully satisfied. It is the textbook win-win style, the opposite of lose-lose.", "b": "Accommodating (low assertiveness, high cooperativeness) means yielding to the other side, so you lose and they win. It is lose-win, with only one loser.", "c": "Competing (high assertiveness, low cooperativeness) pursues your own goals at the other person's expense. It is win-lose, because one side clearly wins."}}, {"q": "The test designed to assess ability to predict future performance is:", "concept": "The answer is b: an aptitude test is built to predict how well a person will learn or perform in a specific area in future. Psychologists separate ability tests by the direction in which they look. An achievement test looks back: it measures what a person has already learnt in a subject, usually through teaching, such as a final mathematics examination. An aptitude test looks ahead: it estimates the capacity to acquire a skill with training, such as mechanical, clerical, musical, verbal or numerical aptitude. An intelligence test samples broad general ability and gives one overall score. Because their jobs differ, achievement tests are judged mainly by content validity (do the items cover the syllabus?), while aptitude tests are judged mainly by predictive validity (do today's scores correlate with later success?). A well-known example is the Differential Aptitude Tests of George Bennett, Harold Seashore and Alexander Wesman (1947), used in school and career guidance. Anne Anastasi (1954), in Psychological Testing, pointed out that the difference is one of degree, since all ability tests measure developed ability, but the purpose still differs. Example: Meera, aged 15, is unsure whether to take science. Her counsellor gives an aptitude battery; high mechanical reasoning and numerical scores suggest she is likely to do well in engineering even though she has never studied it. Option b is right because prediction of future performance is the defining purpose of an aptitude test. Exam trap: achievement and aptitude tests can contain similar items, so students mix them up. Ask whether the test checks past learning (achievement) or forecasts future learning (aptitude).", "traps": {"a": "An achievement test measures what has already been learnt in a specific subject, such as a school examination, and rests on content validity. It looks back at past learning, not forward.", "c": "The intelligence quotient is a score, not a test: William Stern (1912) proposed the ratio of mental age to chronological age, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied it by 100. IQ tests sample general ability rather than being designed to forecast performance in a specific field.", "d": "The NEO Personality Inventory of Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985) measures the Big Five personality traits. It describes typical behaviour, not ability or future performance."}}, {"q": "The following test is a projective test for measurement of personality:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Rorschach inkblot test is a projective test of personality. Projective tests rest on the projective hypothesis: faced with an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, people fill it with meaning drawn from their own needs, conflicts and ways of thinking. The term 'projective methods' was popularised by Lawrence Frank (1939). Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published Psychodiagnostik with ten symmetrical inkblots: five in black and grey, two in black and red, and three multicoloured. In the free association phase the person says what each blot might be; in the inquiry phase the examiner asks where it was seen and what made it look that way. Responses are scored for location (whole blot or a detail), determinants (form, colour, shading, movement), content and popularity. John Exner (1974) standardised scoring in his Comprehensive System, and the newer R-PAS continues that work. Example: Arjun sees 'two people fighting over something' in several cards; a clinician would note the human movement and aggressive content and check it against other evidence before drawing any conclusion. Option a is right because the Rorschach uses an ambiguous stimulus with no right answer, the hallmark of a projective test. Exam trap: the MMPI and MCMI are personality tests too, but they are objective self-report inventories with fixed items and standard scoring. 'Personality test' does not mean 'projective test'.", "traps": {"b": "The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory, by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943), is an objective true or false inventory built by empirical criterion keying. It measures personality and psychopathology, but not by projection.", "c": "The Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory, by Theodore Millon (1977), is an objective self-report inventory aimed at personality disorders and clinical syndromes. Its fixed items and standard scoring make it objective, not projective.", "d": "Positron emission tomography is a brain imaging method that tracks a radioactive tracer to show metabolic or blood-flow activity. It is a neuroimaging tool, not a psychological test of personality at all."}}, {"q": "The socio-cultural factors that affect personality development are:", "concept": "The answer is c: the peer group is a socio-cultural factor in personality development. Psychologists usually sort the determinants of personality into biological and environmental factors. Biological factors include heredity, temperament, physique, the endocrine glands, nutrition and health; twin and adoption studies suggest genes account for up to about half of the variation in traits. The environment shapes the rest, and its socio-cultural part covers the family (parenting style, birth order), the school and teachers, the peer group, the neighbourhood, social class, religion, the mass media and the wider culture. Peers matter because children and adolescents spend much of their day with them and want to be accepted by them. Through peers a child learns cooperation, competition, sharing, leadership and the norms of the group, and peer approval shapes self-esteem and identity. Judith Rich Harris (1995), in her group socialisation theory, argued that peers may shape personality even more than parents do; that claim is debated, but peer influence itself is well established. Example: Riya was shy at home, but after joining a theatre group in Class 8 she began to speak up, took a lead role and grew more confident. The group's norms and approval drove the change. Option c is right because a peer group is a social grouping that passes on cultural norms and values, which is what 'socio-cultural' means. Exam trap: diet, illness and alcoholism can all change behaviour and mood, but they act through the body, so they are classed as physical or biological influences, not socio-cultural ones.", "traps": {"a": "Alcoholism is a substance use disorder that acts on the brain and body. In a parent it can disturb family life, but as listed it is a health or biological factor, not a socio-cultural one.", "b": "Diet is a nutritional, physical factor; malnutrition can affect brain growth, energy and temperament. It is grouped with the biological determinants of personality, not the socio-cultural ones.", "d": "Illness is a physical health factor: long or severe illness can affect mood, activity and self-image. It belongs to the biological or physical determinants, not to socio-cultural influences."}}, {"q": "Carl Jung classified personality on the basis of certain characteristics; one of the terms in his classification was:", "concept": "The answer is b: 'ambivert' is the term that belongs on Carl Jung's introversion-extraversion dimension. Carl Jung (1921), in Psychological Types, described two attitudes, the directions in which a person's psychic energy flows. In extraversion energy flows outward to people, objects and events; the extravert is sociable, active and quickly at ease. In introversion energy flows inward to thoughts, feelings and the inner world; the introvert is reflective, reserved and prefers small groups. Jung combined the two attitudes with four functions: thinking and feeling (ways of judging) and sensation and intuition (ways of perceiving), giving eight psychological types, the basis of the later Myers-Briggs Type Indicator of Katharine Cook Briggs and Isabel Briggs Myers (1940s). Jung stressed that both attitudes exist in everyone and that most people are not pure types. The middle of the dimension, where a person shows introverted and extraverted features fairly equally, is called the ambivert. Example: Kabir loves leading his college quiz team and chatting with strangers at events, yet he needs a quiet evening alone with a book to recharge. He is neither clearly introverted nor clearly extraverted; he is an ambivert. Option b is right because ambiversion names the middle position on Jung's introversion-extraversion attitude, and it is the only option linked with his typology. Exam trap: the other three are also 'type' words, but they belong to body-type and temperament theories, not to Jung; and pyknic is often wrongly attributed to William Sheldon, when it is Ernst Kretschmer's. Key note: the word 'ambivert' itself was coined after Jung, usually credited to the sociologist Kimball Young (1927). It is still the only option on Jung's dimension, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Pyknic is one of Ernst Kretschmer's (1921) body types, short and round, linked with a cyclothymic temperament and manic-depressive illness. It is a body-build type, not Jung's term, and not William Sheldon's either.", "c": "Mesomorphic is one of William Sheldon's (1940s) somatotypes, a muscular, athletic build linked with the somatotonic temperament (bold and energetic). It belongs to constitutional psychology, not to Jung.", "d": "Phlegmatic is one of the four temperaments of the humoral theory of Hippocrates (about 400 BCE), later developed by Galen: calm and slow, supposedly from an excess of phlegm. It predates Jung by more than two thousand years."}}, {"q": "On the basis of Allport's trait theory of personality development, a rich person who gives away all his property to society is", "concept": "The answer is b: a single ruling passion, such as giving away all one's wealth to society, is what Gordon Allport called a cardinal trait. Gordon Allport (1937), in Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, treated traits as real neuropsychological structures that guide behaviour across situations. He sorted a person's individual traits (later renamed personal dispositions) into three levels by how much of the life they govern. A cardinal trait is so dominant that almost every action can be traced to it; it is a master motive. Few people have one, and those who do often become a byword for it: Mother Teresa for selfless service, Gandhi for non-violence, Machiavelli for cunning. Central traits are the five to ten outstanding qualities you would mention in a reference letter, such as honest, friendly or hard-working. Secondary traits are narrow and situation-bound: preferences, attitudes and habits that show only in certain settings, like disliking crowds or loving mangoes. Example: Arjun, a wealthy businessman, spends his later life building schools and finally donates all his property to a trust for village children. His generosity is not one quality among many; it organises his whole life. That is a cardinal trait. Option b is right because giving away all one's property reflects a single, all-pervasive disposition that dominates the person's life, the definition of a cardinal trait. Exam trap: central traits are also important and stable, so students choose them. The test is breadth: central traits describe a person; a cardinal trait rules a person.", "traps": {"a": "Central traits are Gordon Allport's five to ten main qualities that describe a person, such as kind or punctual. They are important but do not dominate every part of life in the way that giving away everything implies.", "c": "Secondary traits are Allport's least general dispositions: preferences, attitudes and habits that appear only in particular situations. A life-defining act of total generosity is far too pervasive to be secondary.", "d": "Temperament traits are not one of Allport's three levels; the label belongs to Raymond Cattell (1950s), who divided source traits into ability, temperament and dynamic traits. It has no place in the cardinal, central and secondary hierarchy."}}, {"q": "In Borderline Personality disorders, the Behavioral approach includes:", "concept": "The answer is d: dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) is the behavioural treatment designed for borderline personality disorder. Marsha Linehan (1993) built DBT for chronically suicidal women with borderline personality disorder; her first randomised trial appeared in 1991 and the treatment manual in 1993. Her biosocial theory says borderline problems arise when a child with high emotional sensitivity grows up in an invalidating environment that dismisses or punishes her feelings, so she never learns to regulate emotion. DBT is a cognitive behavioural therapy with a dialectical core: the therapist balances acceptance (validating the person as she is, drawn partly from Zen practice) with change (behavioural skills and problem solving). Standard DBT has four parts: weekly individual therapy, a weekly skills group, telephone coaching in crises, and a consultation team for therapists. The skills group teaches four modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Trials show DBT reduces self-harm, suicide attempts and days in hospital. Example: Meera, 23, cuts herself after rows with her boyfriend. In DBT she keeps a diary card, learns distress tolerance skills such as holding ice or paced breathing to ride out the urge, and practises asking for what she needs without threats. Option d is right because DBT is the specific, evidence-based behavioural programme built for borderline personality disorder. Exam trap: biofeedback, desensitisation and behavioural activation are all genuine behavioural techniques, so they look plausible, but each was built for a different problem: physiological arousal and stress, phobias, and depression respectively.", "traps": {"a": "Biofeedback gives a person live readings of a body signal (muscle tension, skin conductance, heart rate) so they can learn to control it. It is used for stress, headache and anxiety, not for the emotional and relationship instability of borderline personality disorder.", "b": "Behavioural activation, developed from the work of Neil Jacobson and colleagues (1996) and Christopher Martell, schedules rewarding activities to counter withdrawal in depression. It is a depression treatment, not the specific programme for borderline personality disorder.", "c": "Systematic desensitisation, from Joseph Wolpe (1958), pairs relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy to treat phobias by reciprocal inhibition. It targets specific fears, not self-harm and emotion dysregulation."}}, {"q": "On the basis of Jean Piaget's theory of personality development, the stage in which a child can do simple arithmetic but not algebraic manipulation is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: a child who can do simple arithmetic but not algebra is in Jean Piaget's concrete operational stage. Jean Piaget (writing from the 1920s to the 1960s) described four stages of cognitive development. In the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) the infant learns through action and acquires object permanence. In the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) the child uses symbols and language but is egocentric and fails conservation; its later intuitive phase (about 4 to 7) relies on how things look. In the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11) the child gains operations, mental actions that can be reversed. She can conserve number, mass and volume, classify, put objects in order (seriation) and add, subtract, multiply and divide, because these work on real or easily pictured quantities. What she cannot yet do is reason about abstract symbols and hypothetical possibilities. That arrives in the formal operational stage (about 11 or 12 onward), with hypothetico-deductive reasoning, the handling of variables such as x and y, and systematic testing of hypotheses, as in the pendulum task of Bärbel Inhelder and Piaget (1958). Example: Kabir, aged 9, quickly solves 'Meera has 12 sweets and gives away 5; how many are left?' but is baffled by 'if x plus 5 equals 12, what is x?', because x stands for nothing he can picture. Option b is right because arithmetic on concrete quantities is a concrete operation, while algebra demands the abstract, symbolic reasoning of formal operations. Exam trap: the stem calls this 'personality development', but Piaget's theory is about cognitive development; do not let that wording pull you towards a personality or moral stage.", "traps": {"a": "Formal operations is Jean Piaget's final stage (about 11 or 12 onward), marked by abstract, hypothetical and symbolic reasoning. A child in it could handle algebra, which the stem says this child cannot.", "c": "The intuitive phase (about 4 to 7) is the second half of Piaget's preoperational stage, when judgements rest on appearances and conservation fails. Reliable arithmetic operations point beyond it.", "d": "Role conformity is not a Piagetian stage; it echoes Lawrence Kohlberg's (1958 onward) description of conventional moral reasoning as the 'morality of conventional role-conformity'. It concerns moral judgement, not arithmetic."}}, {"q": "The personality of a person who exhibits extreme \"Black-and-White\" thinking and has instability in relationships will be classified as:", "concept": "The answer is b: black-and-white thinking together with unstable relationships points to borderline personality disorder. DSM-5-TR (2022) defines borderline personality disorder as a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood; five of nine criteria are needed. They include frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; unstable, intense relationships that swing between extremes of idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; marked mood reactivity; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and brief stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. The black-and-white style is called splitting. Otto Kernberg (1975) described it as a primitive defence in which people, including oneself, are seen as all good or all bad, with no blend of the two. Because the label can flip overnight, relationships lurch between adoration and rage. Example: in the first week Riya tells her new flatmate Meera that she is 'the best friend I have ever had'. A month later Meera forgets to reply to a message, and Riya decides she is cruel and selfish, shouts at her and threatens to move out, then begs her not to leave. Option b is right because splitting and a pattern of intense, unstable relationships are core features of borderline personality disorder, and no other option combines them. Exam trap: histrionic personality disorder also shows shifting emotions and dramatic relationships, but its core is attention-seeking and shallow emotion, not all-or-nothing splitting driven by fear of abandonment.", "traps": {"a": "Paranoid personality disorder (Cluster A) is pervasive distrust and suspicion: others are assumed to be exploiting or deceiving the person. Relationships are guarded and cold rather than swinging between idealisation and devaluation.", "c": "Histrionic personality disorder (Cluster B) centres on excessive emotionality and attention-seeking, with rapidly shifting but shallow feelings and a tendency to see relationships as closer than they are. It lacks the core splitting and abandonment panic of borderline personality.", "d": "Avoidant personality disorder (Cluster C) involves social inhibition, feelings of inadequacy and hypersensitivity to criticism, so the person avoids closeness for fear of rejection. Instability and all-or-nothing thinking are not its hallmark."}}, {"q": "The psychology of a caregiver of a sick person and that of a person grieving for the loss of a family member (Kubler-Ross stages) have the following in common:", "concept": "The answer is b: denial is the shared reaction, the first of Elisabeth Kubler-Ross's stages. Elisabeth Kubler-Ross (1969), a Swiss-American psychiatrist who interviewed dying patients at a Chicago hospital, described five stages in On Death and Dying: denial ('this cannot be happening to me'), anger ('why me?'), bargaining (trying to buy time, often with God), depression (sadness at what is being lost) and acceptance (calm readiness). She noted that the families of dying patients pass through similar reactions, and the model was later applied to grief after any major loss. Denial comes first because it acts as a buffer: it gives the mind time to absorb shocking news before it has to be faced in full. Later research shows the stages are not fixed or universal; people may skip, repeat or mix them, so they are best treated as common reactions, not a timetable. Example: when Arjun's father is diagnosed with advanced cancer, Arjun keeps saying 'the reports must be wrong' and books a third opinion. Months later, after his father dies, Arjun's sister Riya keeps setting a plate for him at dinner and says she half expects him to walk in. Caregiver and mourner share the same first move: refusing, for a while, to believe the loss. Option b is right because denial is the opening stage of the Kubler-Ross model, seen both in those caring for the seriously ill and in the bereaved. Exam trap: anger is a Kubler-Ross stage, so 'aggression' and 'blaming' look tempting, but those are behaviours within anger, not stage names; displacement is a defence mechanism, not a stage.", "traps": {"a": "Blaming (oneself, the doctors, God) can occur within Elisabeth Kubler-Ross's anger stage, but it is not a stage name and is not the common opening response of caregivers and the bereaved.", "c": "Aggression is a behaviour that may appear during the anger stage; the stage itself is called anger, and it typically follows denial rather than being the shared first reaction.", "d": "Displacement is a defence mechanism (Anna Freud, 1936) in which feelings are redirected to a safer target, such as snapping at a nurse instead of raging at the illness. It is not one of the Kubler-Ross stages."}}, {"q": "The correct statement regarding Primordial prevention is:", "concept": "The answer is a: primordial prevention aims to stop disease risk factors from developing in the first place. Public health describes prevention in levels that match the natural history of disease. Primordial prevention, a term introduced by Toma Strasser (1978) for heart disease, acts before risk factors even appear in a population. It works on the whole population and its way of life: healthy diet, physical activity, no tobacco, and sound social and environmental conditions. Primary prevention acts in the stage of susceptibility, when risk factors exist but disease has not begun; it targets susceptible people and modifies existing risk factors through health promotion and specific protection (immunisation, treating high blood pressure, quitting smoking). Secondary prevention acts in early disease: early diagnosis and prompt treatment, such as screening for cervical cancer or diabetes. Tertiary prevention acts in established disease in symptomatic patients: limiting disability and rehabilitation. Gerald Caplan (1964) brought the primary, secondary and tertiary levels into mental health. Example: a school in Pune removes junk food from its canteen and runs daily sport so that its pupils never become obese or start smoking; that is primordial. Giving a diet plan to Kabir, an overweight 40-year-old with high cholesterol, is primary; screening him for diabetes is secondary; cardiac rehabilitation after his heart attack is tertiary. Option a is right because preventing the emergence of risk factors themselves, not just disease, is the definition of primordial prevention. Exam trap: option b sounds similar, but modifying risk factors that already exist is primary prevention. The word 'existing' is the giveaway.", "traps": {"b": "Modifying existing risk factors (treating high blood pressure, helping a smoker quit) before disease appears is primary prevention. Primordial prevention comes earlier, before the risk factors exist.", "c": "The susceptible population, people exposed to risk factors but not yet ill, is the target of primary prevention. Primordial prevention targets the whole population, including people who have no risk factors yet.", "d": "The symptomatic patient with established disease is the target of tertiary prevention, which limits disability and rehabilitates. It is the latest level, the opposite end from primordial prevention."}}, {"q": "A stimulus that does not initially on its own elicit a response from the organism is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: a stimulus that does not on its own produce the response under study is a neutral stimulus. Ivan Pavlov (1927), a Russian physiologist who had won the 1904 Nobel Prize for work on digestion, noticed that his dogs began to salivate before food reached their mouths, and he worked out the parts of classical conditioning in Conditioned Reflexes. The unconditioned stimulus (UCS), such as food, produces a response naturally, without learning. That response, salivation to food, is the unconditioned response (UCR). A neutral stimulus (NS), such as the sound of a metronome or bell, produces no salivation at first; at most it causes an orienting response, the dog pricking up its ears. After the neutral stimulus is repeatedly presented just before the food, it becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that triggers salivation on its own, now called the conditioned response (CR). Timing matters: learning is best when the neutral stimulus comes slightly before the unconditioned stimulus (forward conditioning). Example: Meera's mother always puts the pressure cooker on before serving dinner. At first the whistle means nothing to Meera's stomach (neutral stimulus). After weeks of whistles followed by food, Meera's mouth waters the moment it whistles (conditioned stimulus). Option b is right because a stimulus that does not initially elicit the response is, by definition, neutral. Exam trap: the same physical stimulus changes its label over time. The bell is a neutral stimulus before conditioning and a conditioned stimulus after it, so check whether the question describes it before or after pairing.", "traps": {"a": "An unconditioned stimulus, such as food in Ivan Pavlov's experiments, elicits a response naturally, without any learning. That is the opposite of a stimulus that produces no response at first.", "c": "A conditioned stimulus is a formerly neutral stimulus that elicits a response only after being paired with an unconditioned stimulus. It does elicit the response, so it cannot be one that elicits nothing on its own.", "d": "'Negative stimulus' is not a standard term in classical conditioning; it borrows the word 'negative' from operant terms such as negative reinforcement (B. F. Skinner, 1938). It does not name a stimulus that starts with no effect."}}, {"q": "The Neurotic defence mechanism by which a person who cheats on his own Income tax return but also criticizes the government for corruption is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: holding two contradictory positions in separate mental boxes, cheating on tax while condemning government corruption, is compartmentalisation. Defence mechanisms, first described by Sigmund Freud (1894) and catalogued by his daughter Anna Freud (1936), are unconscious ways in which the ego protects itself from anxiety. Compartmentalisation keeps conflicting attitudes, beliefs or behaviours apart in separate mental compartments, so that the person never feels the contradiction between them. Each attitude is switched on in its own setting and never set beside the other, so there is no guilt and no dissonance to resolve. It is usually described as a neurotic-level defence: it distorts reality less than psychotic defences such as delusional projection, but more than mature ones such as humour or suppression. It is close to isolation of affect and rationalisation, and it is familiar in everyday hypocrisy: a doctor who smokes, or a strict vegetarian who happily wears leather. Example: Kabir under-reports his income every year and is proud of 'saving' on tax. At dinner he speaks passionately about how corrupt politicians are ruining the country. He sees no conflict, because his own tax return and 'public corruption' sit in different compartments of his mind. Option c is right because the man holds two incompatible standards at once without ever connecting them, which is exactly what compartmentalisation does. Exam trap: projection is the closest distractor. In projection a person denies his own impulse and sees it in others instead. This man does not deny his cheating or locate it in the government; he simply keeps his cheating and his criticism apart.", "traps": {"a": "Displacement, described by Anna Freud (1936), redirects a feeling from a threatening target to a safer one, such as shouting at a younger brother after being scolded by the boss. The man is not shifting an emotion onto a substitute target.", "b": "Dissociation is a disruption of the normal integration of memory, identity or awareness, as in dissociative amnesia or depersonalisation. The man remembers both his cheating and his criticism; he just never puts them side by side.", "d": "Projection attributes one's own unacceptable impulse to someone else while denying it in oneself. This man does not deny his own cheating by seeing it in the government; he holds both positions at once, kept apart, which is compartmentalisation."}}, {"q": "A mature defence mechanism where a painter who has obsessive sexual ideas redirects them into his paintings is:", "concept": "The answer is b: channelling forbidden sexual ideas into painting is sublimation, a mature defence. Sigmund Freud (1905), in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, described sublimation as redirecting sexual or aggressive drive energy away from its original aim towards a socially valued, non-sexual goal such as art, science or sport. He saw it as the healthiest defence and a source of much of civilisation, and Anna Freud (1936) kept it in her list of defences. George Vaillant (1977), in Adaptation to Life, drew on the Harvard Study of Adult Development and ranked defences from psychotic through immature and neurotic to mature. His mature defences are altruism, anticipation, humour, sublimation and suppression, and men who used them more had better health, careers and marriages. Sublimation counts as mature because the impulse is neither denied nor acted out; it gets real but acceptable expression, and the person gains from it. Example: Kabir, a young painter, is troubled by intense sexual thoughts that he finds shameful. Instead of acting on them or forcing them out of mind, he pours their energy into vivid, sensual paintings of the human form that win him an exhibition. The drive is kept; only its outlet has changed. Option b is right because the stem describes an unacceptable drive transformed into socially valued creative work, the textbook definition of sublimation. Exam trap: anticipation is also on Vaillant's list of mature defences, so the word 'mature' alone does not decide the answer. The deciding feature is the redirection of a forbidden impulse into a valued activity.", "traps": {"a": "Introjection, a term from Sándor Ferenczi (1909), is taking another person's values, attitudes or qualities into oneself, as a child absorbs a parent's rules. It involves no redirection of a drive into creative work.", "c": "Anticipation is one of George Vaillant's mature defences, but it means realistic planning for future discomfort, such as rehearsing for a feared interview. It does not transform a sexual impulse into art.", "d": "Compensation, linked with Alfred Adler (1907) and his idea of inferiority, is making up for a real or felt weakness by excelling in another area. The painter is not covering a deficiency; he is redirecting a drive."}}, {"q": "The Psychological approach in Anxiety is:", "concept": "The answer is b: acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT) is the psychological treatment for anxiety in this list. Steven Hayes, Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson (1999) set out ACT, one of the 'third wave' cognitive behavioural therapies. It grows from relational frame theory, an account of how language lets thoughts take on the power of the things they describe. ACT does not try to argue thoughts away or remove anxiety. It holds that much suffering comes from experiential avoidance: struggling to get rid of unwanted feelings, which narrows life and often makes the feelings stronger. Its goal is psychological flexibility, built through six processes (the 'hexaflex'): acceptance, cognitive defusion (seeing a thought as just a thought), contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values and committed action. Trials and meta-analyses support ACT for anxiety disorders, depression and chronic pain. Example: Riya avoids presentations because she fears her voice will shake. Her ACT therapist helps her notice 'I will look stupid' as a passing thought, make room for the racing heart, and step towards what she values, a career in teaching, by volunteering to present anyway. Option b is right because ACT is a structured psychotherapy that changes the person's relationship with anxious thoughts and feelings. Exam trap: do not confuse ACT with the dialectical behaviour therapy of Marsha Linehan (1993), which teaches four skill modules, or with the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (1976), which changes the content of thoughts; ACT changes how a person relates to them. Key note: biofeedback is also used for anxiety and is taught by psychologists as a behavioural self-regulation technique, so option a is arguable. But it trains a physiological signal rather than being a full psychological therapy, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Biofeedback uses instruments to feed back body signals such as muscle tension, skin temperature or heart rate so the person can learn to control arousal. It is a physiological self-regulation method, usually an adjunct, not a psychotherapy in its own right.", "c": "Peer support is help from people with shared experience, such as self-help groups. It is a social support resource, valuable but not a structured psychological therapy for anxiety.", "d": "The Masters and Johnson technique (William Masters and Virginia Johnson, 1970) is sex therapy, chiefly sensate focus, for sexual dysfunctions such as erectile disorder. It is not a treatment for anxiety disorders as such."}}, {"q": "The following is NOT a principle in the Counselling process:", "concept": "The answer is d: making decisions for the client breaks a basic principle of counselling. Counselling is a helping relationship in which a trained person helps a client understand a problem and find his or her own way through it. Carl Rogers (1951), in Client-Centered Therapy, argued that the client, not the counsellor, is the expert on his own life and has an inborn drive towards growth; the counsellor's job is to provide empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness. Felix Biestek (1957) set out seven principles of the casework relationship that counselling shares: individualisation, purposeful expression of feelings, controlled emotional involvement, acceptance, a non-judgemental attitude, client self-determination and confidentiality. Client self-determination means the client makes the final choices. Deciding for him breeds dependence, removes his ownership of the outcome and lets the counsellor's values take over. The other options fit accepted practice: accept and respect all of the client's feelings, even odd or 'bizarre' ones; involve the family when the problem or its solution lies partly in the family; and help the client build a hopeful, positive attitude. Example: Arjun, 22, cannot decide whether to take a job in Bengaluru or stay home to care for his ageing parents. A good counsellor helps him weigh the pros and cons, explore his values and feelings, and perhaps meets the parents too. She does not tell him 'take the job'. Option d is right because counselling respects client self-determination; decisions belong to the client. Exam trap: advice-giving and directive guidance are older styles, so 'deciding for the client' can sound helpful. In counselling it is a violation, not a technique.", "traps": {"a": "Involving family members when needed is good practice, since problems and their solutions often lie partly in the family; that is the basis of family counselling. It is a counselling principle, not the exception.", "b": "Respecting the client's feelings, however odd or 'bizarre', reflects acceptance and a non-judgemental attitude (Felix Biestek, 1957) and Carl Rogers's unconditional positive regard. It is a core principle, not a violation.", "c": "Helping the client develop a positive, hopeful attitude is a recognised aim; Jerome Frank (1961) named the arousal of hope as a common factor in all effective therapies. It fits counselling, so it is not the answer."}}, {"q": "The following statement is true regarding isolated adjustment disorders:", "concept": "The answer is a: in an isolated adjustment disorder, treatment centres on strengthening the person's coping. An adjustment disorder is an emotional or behavioural reaction to an identifiable stressor, such as a divorce, job loss, exam failure or move, that is out of proportion to the stressor or impairs functioning. In DSM-5-TR the symptoms begin within 3 months of the stressor and do not last more than 6 months after the stressor or its consequences have ended. Subtypes include with depressed mood, with anxiety, with mixed anxiety and depressed mood, and with disturbance of conduct. 'Isolated' means it stands alone, without another disorder such as major depression. Because it is by definition a time-limited response to a specific stress, the main treatment is brief: supportive counselling or crisis intervention, problem solving, reducing or removing the stressor where possible, mobilising family and social support, and teaching coping skills such as relaxation and structured problem solving. Medicines are not the core; a short course of a sedative or anxiolytic may be used for a few nights of severe insomnia or acute distress. Example: after failing her final-year exam, Meera, 21, stops eating properly, cries daily and skips classes for six weeks. Her counsellor helps her draw up a revision plan, rebuild her routine, lean on friends and practise breathing exercises; within two months she is back to normal. Option a is right because adjustment disorder is a short reaction to stress, and building coping skills and support is the main aim. Exam trap: options that sound more intensive (long in-depth therapy, months of SSRIs) are tempting because they seem thorough, but adjustment disorder usually resolves with brief support.", "traps": {"b": "Prolonged, in-depth (for example psychoanalytic) psychotherapy is meant for long-standing personality or neurotic problems. Adjustment disorder is time-limited, so brief supportive therapy is usually enough.", "c": "SSRIs given for many months are first-line for major depression and anxiety disorders. For an isolated adjustment disorder there is little evidence they are needed, and the condition usually resolves within 6 months anyway.", "d": "This statement is false: a short course of a sedative or anxiolytic can relieve severe insomnia or anxiety in the acute phase. Such drugs are used briefly and sparingly, not avoided because they give no relief."}}, {"q": "In a case of Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder, once the diagnosis is made, the following is true regarding management:", "concept": "The answer is b: cognitive processing therapy (CPT) is a useful, first-line, evidence-based treatment once PTSD is diagnosed. Post-traumatic stress disorder follows exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence; in DSM-5-TR it has four symptom clusters (intrusions, avoidance, negative changes in thoughts and mood, and changes in arousal and reactivity) lasting more than a month. Patricia Resick (1988 onward) developed CPT, first for survivors of rape. It is a structured cognitive therapy of about 12 sessions. The person writes an impact statement about what the trauma means to them and works on 'stuck points', distorted beliefs about blame, safety, trust, power, esteem and intimacy, using Socratic questioning and worksheets; an optional written account of the trauma adds exposure. Major guidelines name trauma-focused therapies first: CPT, prolonged exposure (Edna Foa, 1980s onward), eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing (Francine Shapiro, 1989) and trauma-focused CBT. Example: after an assault, Riya, 26, believes 'it was my fault for going out at night' and 'nowhere is safe'. In CPT she examines the evidence for the self-blame, comes to see that responsibility lies with the attacker, and her shame and nightmares ease. Option b is right because CPT is a specific trauma-focused therapy with strong trial support for PTSD. Exam trap: 'prolonged psychotherapy' sounds like Prolonged Exposure therapy, a real and specific PTSD treatment, but the option only describes long, vague therapy, which is not a recommended plan.", "traps": {"a": "'Prolonged psychotherapy' is vague: guidelines recommend brief, structured trauma-focused therapies, not open-ended long therapy. Do not confuse it with Prolonged Exposure, Edna Foa's specific therapy, which this option does not name.", "c": "Clonazepam is a benzodiazepine; guidelines advise against benzodiazepines in PTSD because they do not treat core symptoms, may hinder recovery and risk dependence. First-line drugs are SSRIs such as sertraline and paroxetine, or the SNRI venlafaxine.", "d": "Thought stopping, popularised by Joseph Wolpe (1960s), is an old technique for unwanted thoughts and is not an evidence-based PTSD treatment. Pushing trauma memories away feeds avoidance, which keeps PTSD going."}}, {"q": "The following is true regarding management of Generalized Anxiety Disorder:", "concept": "The answer is a: for generalised anxiety disorder (GAD), antidepressants are more useful than benzodiazepines as long-term treatment. GAD is excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters, more days than not for at least 6 months, with three or more of six symptoms: restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension and sleep disturbance. It tends to be chronic, so treatment must be safe for months or years. First-line medicines are selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (escitalopram, sertraline, paroxetine) and serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors (venlafaxine, duloxetine); pregabalin and buspirone are alternatives. They take 2 to 4 weeks to work but treat both the anxiety and the depression that often comes with it, and they do not cause dependence. Benzodiazepines (diazepam, alprazolam, clonazepam) act within an hour by boosting GABA, but tolerance, dependence, withdrawal, sedation and memory problems mean they are kept for short-term or bridging use, usually 2 to 4 weeks. Among psychological treatments, cognitive behavioural therapy (worry exposure, cognitive restructuring, applied relaxation) is first-line. Example: Kabir, 35, has worried constantly about money, his children and his health for a year. His psychiatrist starts escitalopram, adds clonazepam only to cover the first few weeks and then tapers it off, while a psychologist begins CBT. Option a is right because SSRIs and SNRIs are first-line long-term drugs for GAD, while benzodiazepines are limited by dependence. Exam trap: benzodiazepines work faster and feel more 'anti-anxiety', so students rate them higher. Speed of relief is not the same as long-term usefulness.", "traps": {"b": "Exposure and response prevention (Victor Meyer, 1966, later developed by Edna Foa) is the first-line psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder. GAD has no compulsions to prevent, so CBT aimed at worry is used instead.", "c": "Emotive imagery (Arnold Lazarus and Arnold Abramovitz, 1962) uses pleasant, exciting images to counter anxiety, a variant of desensitisation mainly for children's fears. Imagery-based relaxation can ease anxiety, so the blanket claim that it is not useful is not a safe true statement.", "d": "Deep brain stimulation is an invasive neurosurgical treatment used for Parkinson's disease and, experimentally, for severe treatment-resistant OCD or depression. It is not an established treatment for GAD."}}, {"q": "In a patient with obsessive compulsive disorder, the Psychological Cognitive Behaviour Therapy approach is:", "concept": "The answer is a: among these options, thought stopping is the cognitive behavioural technique aimed at the obsessions of OCD. Obsessive-compulsive disorder has obsessions, which are intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety, and compulsions, which are repetitive acts or mental rituals done to reduce that anxiety. Thought stopping was first used for obsessional thinking by James Taylor in the 1950s and popularised by Joseph Wolpe (1960s) as a behaviour therapy technique. The person deliberately brings on the obsessive thought, and the therapist (later the person, silently) says 'Stop!' firmly to interrupt it; snapping a rubber band on the wrist or switching to a pleasant scene may be added. With practice the person learns to cut the thought off early. Example: Arjun keeps getting the thought 'my hands are contaminated'. In session he lets the thought come, then says 'Stop!' and turns his attention to a planned task. Option a is right because thought stopping is the only option that is a cognitive behavioural method directed at obsessional thoughts. Exam trap: today the first-line psychological treatment for OCD is exposure and response prevention, which grew from the work of Victor Meyer (1966) and was refined by Edna Foa; if ERP were offered, it would win. Key note: modern guidelines put exposure and response prevention first because suppressing a thought can make it rebound, as Daniel Wegner showed in his 'white bear' studies (1987). Thought stopping is now seen as weak for OCD, but among these options it is the only OCD-directed cognitive behavioural technique, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Vocational rehabilitation restores the capacity to work and earn, for example through job training for people with chronic mental illness. It is a rehabilitation service, not a CBT technique for obsessions.", "c": "Relaxation training, such as the progressive muscle relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1929), lowers general arousal and is used in GAD and stress. It does not target obsessions, and in OCD trials it is often used as the comparison condition.", "d": "Emotive imagery (Arnold Lazarus and Arnold Abramovitz, 1962) uses exciting, positive images to counter children's phobic fears. It is a desensitisation variant for phobias, not an OCD technique."}}, {"q": "The development of new onset diabetes mellitus is most likely with the use of atypical antipsychotic:", "concept": "The answer is a: among atypical antipsychotics, clozapine carries the highest risk of new-onset diabetes mellitus. Atypical (second-generation) antipsychotics block serotonin 5-HT2A as well as dopamine D2 receptors, so they cause fewer movement side effects than older drugs such as haloperidol. Their price is metabolic: weight gain, raised blood sugar, insulin resistance, raised lipids and, in some patients, new type 2 diabetes, occasionally with diabetic ketoacidosis. The risk varies sharply by drug. Clozapine and olanzapine are highest; quetiapine and risperidone are intermediate; aripiprazole, ziprasidone and lurasidone are lowest. The mechanisms include histamine H1 and serotonin 5-HT2C blockade, which increase appetite and weight, and direct effects on insulin action that can appear even before weight rises. Clozapine is kept for treatment-resistant schizophrenia, where it is the most effective drug, so patients on it need regular checks of weight, waist, fasting glucose or HbA1c and lipids, along with blood counts for agranulocytosis. Example: Kabir, 30, with treatment-resistant schizophrenia, finally improves on clozapine, but within a year he gains 12 kilograms and his fasting sugar rises into the diabetic range. His psychiatrist adds lifestyle advice and metformin rather than stopping the one drug that works. Option a is right because clozapine, together with olanzapine, has the strongest link with weight gain and new-onset diabetes among the atypicals. Exam trap: clozapine's famous side effect is agranulocytosis, so students forget its metabolic risk. Remember both: clozapine tops the list for agranulocytosis and for diabetes.", "traps": {"b": "Lurasidone is a newer atypical antipsychotic with one of the lowest risks of weight gain and diabetes, which is why it is often chosen for patients with metabolic concerns.", "c": "Aripiprazole is a dopamine D2 partial agonist with low metabolic risk, often used when a patient has gained weight on other antipsychotics. Its typical side effect is akathisia, not diabetes.", "d": "Ziprasidone is close to weight-neutral with low diabetes risk; its notable side effect is QTc prolongation on the electrocardiogram. It sits at the safe end of the metabolic spectrum."}}, {"q": "A Client goes to bed at 10PM and is still unable to sleep till 11PM. The next step is:", "concept": "The answer is d: meditation, a calming relaxation method, is the right next step for someone still awake an hour after going to bed. Sleep needs low arousal. Anything that stimulates the body or mind delays sleep, and lying in bed awake and frustrated teaches the brain to link the bed with wakefulness. Cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia. Its key part is stimulus control, from Richard Bootzin (1972): go to bed only when sleepy; use the bed only for sleep and sex; if not asleep within about 20 minutes, get up, go to another room and do something quiet and relaxing in dim light, returning only when sleepy; get up at the same time every day; avoid daytime naps. Other parts include sleep restriction (Arthur Spielman, 1987), relaxation training, cognitive work on sleep worries and sleep hygiene (no caffeine late in the day, no screens before bed). Meditation and relaxation lower heart rate, muscle tension and racing thoughts, which lets sleep come. Example: Riya lies awake from 10 to 11 pm worrying about work. Rather than scrolling her phone or making tea, she sits in a dim chair and does 15 minutes of slow-breathing meditation, then returns to bed when her eyes feel heavy. Option d is right because meditation lowers arousal, and it is the only option that does. The paper printed 'mediation', which means meditation. Exam trap: 'trying' to sleep feels sensible but backfires, because effort raises arousal; and television, though passive, brings stimulating content and bright screen light that suppresses melatonin.", "traps": {"a": "Coffee contains caffeine, a stimulant that blocks adenosine (the brain's sleep-pressure signal) for hours. It would make falling asleep even harder.", "b": "Television gives stimulating content and bright screen light that suppresses melatonin; stimulus control allows only quiet, dim-light activities out of bed. Watching a programme keeps the brain alert.", "c": "Turning on the light raises alertness, and lying in bed 'trying' to sleep adds effort and frustration, which raise arousal further. Stimulus control says to leave the bed and return only when sleepy."}}]});
